        <p>LAT.T. RIGHTS Se?VtD.J tie must form his own judgment without any • - help from Charles Dignal. There was a mystery WRITTEN 'IN SC PI ET here , which it was not his business to fathom. ° He was not curious; a doctor with a large practice comes across too many strange happenings to bother about or fooral out secrets BT ALICE MAUD MEADOWS, which are not imparted to him. He looked at Charles Dignal. "I must make a Author of "One Life_ Between," "A Million Of thorough examination," he said. "Is your Money," "The Extreme Penalty," "For daughter nursing "—he hesitated ever so slightly of &amp; Lady," "I Charge You Both," Ike. —"her uncle ? " "Helping," Dignal returned, laconically. " You' —he looked at the girl—"are capable ?" CHArTEH XVII. hs risked. SUSPICION. Her eyes seemed to turn lothfutly from the "I want to see the doctor." handsome face of the sick man. If Dr. Walker "He's engaged." ) had not been sure before that Charles Dignal had "For how long ?" Dignal asked. "I must sea omeoeted his story, he wild have known now; him quickly—it's a matter of life and deatissol the expression in the griTs beautiful eyes was Can't he leave the other patient ? " not the look a niece gives an uncle. Jos Dobbins smiled slowly. This impstient "Quite, I think," she answered. "I have old fellow amused him. "Of course he can't," nothing to do; lam fond of nursing; I will obey he answered. "What's the matter with you PI any instructions." You don't want him for yourself, do you? You Dr. Walker nodded approvingly. "Very well," look hale and hearty enough A bit tallowy, but ' he returned, "I will give you my instructions that's nothing. I've known men and women later. You can leave us now." whom one would .have thought just fit to drop, so She opened her lips to protest; changed her pale and amemic-looking, be strong as horses. mind. She had no idea who this man was. Her it's not blood, or bone, or flesh as constitutes father said his brother; she did not believe that. strength, it's pluck and wiriness. You're wiry, She knew also, as well as though he had told I should say, pale as lard, and hard as nails." her so, that the doctor did-not credit what had Dignal looked at himself in the glass. Whether been told him. She gave one more look at the he looked hard as nails was certainly a query. sick man, and went quietly from the room. Pale as lard, as Jos Dobbins had picturesquely Then very thoroughly, very quietly, Dr. Walker put it, he certainly did appear, and well he made his examination found the seat of the might. A man seemed dying, in his little cottage, mischief, yet could not fathom the mystery. Ile and he didn't want him to uie. looked very searchingly at Charles Dignal. "Whether I look strong or not," he said, "I "This will ho a long business," he said, don't want the doctor for myself. if I did , slowly—"the man may not even recover." I should not be in such a hurry. No. I want Dignal's hands were clasped; they gripped him "—he seemed to swallow with difficulty— ome another painfully. His pale face grew even "for my brother—my brother who has come home der. • from the wars, off his head, poor chap, and I "Don't say that!" he said. "For Heaven's don't know what to du with him. He was Fiske, don't! He must recover—must, I tell raying when I came away, raving like a maniac, y It's your trade to drag him back from the with only my girl to look after him, to held him j %M.l of death, if he's so eeer them; if he's half down. I don't know whether she'll have the tarough the portals you must catch hold of him, strength. You'll keep this to yourself; I don't d -ew him to life again. You'd laugh, wouldn't want it to get about. •It would do her harm, y in, at any other workman who wasn't up to poor child, if it wore known she had an unel hs business ? You're not up to yours, you're who had gone off his head." fh; to be laughed at, if you can't help a handsome JOB Dobbins looked duly impressed. "I shan't y sung fellow like that to live his allotted spell. mention it," he said. "Why should ? I never I tell you you must—it's vital!" knew you had a brother. Ali, there's the bell, Dr. Walker bent over the man, lifted his you shall go in first. I always understood, with e fends looked deep into his eyes, shook his the exception of your daughter--" h :ad Dignal did not speak. Jos Dobbins left him, "I shell do my best, ef ecurse," he answered. returning in a minute or two. "The doctor will, "You can cell your daughter in again, if she's see you," he said, and, looking more than to nurse him," nervous, Dignal passed into the eanetum' Charles Dignal Opened the door: the girl was Dr. Walker nodded to him pleasantly. "'What's standing close outside. Dignal made a gesture, wrong ?" he asked. "Not quite yourself again, she mitered the room, and took up her seat once Dignal ?" more beside the bed. • "Quite myself, thank you, sir," he answered, Dr. Walker looked at her. "This man," he turning and twisting his hat nervously in his said, slowly, "is very is only right you hands. "There's never much the matter with awl your father should know that—very, very iii. me, but you will remember I'd a brother out in There is no need for me to enter into the why Africa ?" o wherefore—that ifs the fact of the ease; he is Dr. Walker looked surprised. "No," he dengerouely near death. I don't say he can't answered, "had you? Well, well, we all seem to ba saved. I don't say he Can—tho balance is have had someone there, but happily, most against life. The best we can do for him iis to happily, it's all over now. What about your give him absolute quiet; the room must be brother ? " darkened, not absolutely dark, but the light _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>"He was hit," Dignal said. "Shell bursting near him hit him with a splinter. He wasn't killed, but injured, injured in the brain. They "—he paused and passed his handkerchief across his damp brow—"they kept him there as long as they could, for a matter of a year or more, up there—up there in Kimberley; then, because he wouldn't, or couldn't, pull round, therS.</p>
        <p>must be subdued. I will sern some medicine, and he lutist he induced to take nourishment. I will write out the treatment, you must keep to it absolutely—that will constitute his .only chance. He must never be left—never for a single memenh; you must have someone to help you, Miss Dignal. Shall I send a nurse ? " He turned to Charles Dignal. The old man</p>
        <p>_ sent him home to me, and he's raving. Doctor, he's as mad as a hatter. I want you to come and see him, and I want you to keep quiet about it. My girl would—would never get a husband ssif it was known she had a mad uncle, and I dQn't want to leave her When I peg out without a protector. Will you come ? " Dr. Walker nodded. "Why, of course," be answered. "But surely, if the man is in the condition you mention, he ought to be in one of the military hospitals. Any way, I'll come at once. Who's looking after him now ? " Dignal rose slowly. He was evidently upset, agitated. "My daughter," he answered. "She's plenty of nerve, she's not in the least afraid, and she's strong enough to hold him. I don't mind her looking after him, but I don't want aaybne to know. That's natural, isn't it? " 'Natural to you, evidently," Dr. Walker said, with a laugh. "It isn't everyone who is se sensitive. And set your mind at ease on that • point. Your brother having gone off his head through a wound wouldn't in any way affect your daughter's matrimonial chances. Folk are not quite so foolish as that. Besides, a pretty girl like your Ruth would get a husband if she had . half-a-dozen mad uncles." Digital looked more nervous than ever. "I don't know," he answered. "Folk have a great horror of madness. Any way, you won't mention it ? " Dr. Walker laughed. "I won't mention it," he said. "Doctors are like priests, they keep things to themselves. But you are making a great mietake; your brother would get nothing hut sympathy, and you, through him. Get along home. i fancy, if your description is accurate, your patient is a little beyond the nursing of your daughter. ". Charles Dignal went at once. He was ovidenkly very, very much troubled, very much upset. He wee a man who had lived a hard life in one way; from hand to mouth so far as his daily bread was concerned. For all that he was a man who had had singularly little trouble of late years. For this reason, the advent of his sick brother probably troubled him more than it would have most. For an elderly mats he walked home very quickly, looking about him suspiciously, nervously, as he went. When he reached the cottage he let himself in with a key, closed the door after him, and, passing through a sitting- room, went to a bedrom beyond. On the bed a young and handsome man, a man who, far from ravine' or showing any rest- lessness, was very still. By his side sat a very beautiful girl dressed in a rough serge frock. As her father entered the room, her hand dropped to her lap. It bad been clasping that of the sick man.</p>
        <p>flushed. "No," he answered, "I will take it in turn with Ruth. You know what I told you—l won't have anyone else here. We three will look after him. And, remember, he must be saved!" Dr. Walker rose. "I can only do my best," he said. "I will come again this evening. will send the medicine as soon as possible. If you will give me paper and pen and ink I will write down my instructions." Five minutes later he left the house. It was natural he should wonder a little. "The man is a young man," he said to himset! —"no more Dignal's brother than I am ! I wonder what it moans? Well, well, it's none of my business. He's a man to be saved if poesible, any way. I must make haste back. to the surgery:" Dr. Walker was a walking doctor—that is to sly, he did not keep a carriage, for the shnple and only reason that he could not afford it. Ha stepped out briakly, however, never pausing until a weary-looking Man stopped him. It was Chief Warder Claak. "I think I'll come back to the surgery with you," he said. "I'm just dead beat ; you shall, if you will, give me a dose of the most vivifying drug you have. I've been up all night. We have not caught the man who escaped last night yet, though we've been scouring the country. Vu haven't seen any suspicious character, I suppose ?" Inwardly, Dr. Walker started; outwardly, he slimed no emots..m. Was that the-meaning of Charles Dignal's emotion? Was he 'harbouring the convict, and telling lies to shelter him ? It looked lilac- it. What ought he to do ? Inform Chief .Warder Clark ? Surely not. Had he not said doctors were like priests—anything told theta was sacred ? "No," he answered, Slowly. "What sort of looking fellow is this man ? " "Handsome," the chief warder said, with a vicious snap in his tone, "young—oh! if you saw him in ordinary dress, you wouldn't think him what he is. Gentleman Jim we uaed to call hi ,a in the prison. He made a wonderful escape ; the wonder is the man did not break his neck. Can you give me something to pull me round, Dr. Walker ? "- "At all events, I'll try," he answered, "but a.good night's rest would be the best thing for you. Come along, I'm very busy to-day." "Charles, Dignal harbouring a convict," he said to himself—"putting himself within peril of tha law! I Wonder why ? What is the man ta him ?"</p>
        <p>CIIA.PTER XVIII, A DECIII2.TION.</p>
        <p>"Anythino</p>
        <p>"Well ? " Charles Dignal said. happened?" "Nothing," she said. "He's -been just the same, never moved, lying there like a log, so pale, so still, that if hadn't been for the flicker of his eyelids now and then I should have thought him dead. Is the doctor coming, lather ? "</p>
        <p>In playing any game, even including the great game of life, opinions differ as to whether the bold or the careful player scores most points. In all probability it is sometimes one, sometimes the other,- as chance decrees. `..ernon Salisbury, the convict, was behaving v,ith what seemed like recklessness, and yet he was careful too. lie seldom went beyond the grounds of John Ilea,ndon's country.bonse; he declined all invita- 'ions, though the county seemed more than willing to take him up, making the excuse that in spite of his robust looks he had really made the journey to England for the sake of his health, and Was far from strong. lie botanised in the garden with Mrs. Cole, played tennis with Olive, and drove Paul Clifford half mad with jealousy. There are few women, whether engaged or not, who have not a weak corner in their heart for a handsome, bright, breezy young man. Olive liked Vernon Salisbury, and made no secret of her liking. Mrs. Cole was lima over ears in love with him, and, apparentl), did not care who knew it.</p>
        <p>"Yes, and remember he's just lying here now from exhaufition. He's been raving. It's been almost more than you could do to hold him. He's been talking about shot and shell and the burning heat of Africa. Don't forget." The girl looked puzzled. "I won't forget,'" she said. "But why, father, and who is he ?" "Never mind the why. As to who he is, he's your uncle*my brother." "Your brother? Why, father, he if; a young man."</p>
        <p>The look of worry on Charles Dignal's face grew deeper. . "I know," he said. "Well, he is my young brother. Your—your—your uncle Fred, who went to Africa—mid don't make . any blunders or mistakes. It's—it's vital you shouldn't make any mistakes. And he mustn't die, mind that—we've got to ,get him well." The girl turned . her .beautiful eyes upon the sick man. Every woman is at heart a mother. It was the lovely mother look that slimed on her face now. "He shan't die if I can help it," she said. "You may be ouita—quite sure of that." "Has he taken anything ? " "Nothing but a few drops of brandy I have forced between his lips. That's all. I " "Hush!" he said. "The doctor is coming. Leave me to do the talking, or Most of it. Turn his head a little." He reached towards the silent figure as lit spoke.. Ruth Dignal stretched out her hand. "No—no !" she said. "It hurts him; let him lie so, he's easier. I fancy he's been injured, poor fellow—poor fellow !" Charles Dion] held up is hand. "Hush! be said, and a moment later Dr. Walker entered the room. One glance at the man assured him that for somq, reason or c tier Charles Dignal had not told him the truth. 'ibis was no ease of raving delirium ; no injury of long standing. The pale face was not worn an it would have been with a lengthened illness. The invalid was uncenscious; had been so for lio;va. Dr. Walker, however, would have staked PCs medical reputation that he had not been ill nace than twenty-four hours. A year was ridiculout It is, however, no part of a doctor's duty to j.'ry into family matters. Ha had been called in to k ttend upon a sick man; to do the best he eoula for him. There his duty berin and ended. Lie asked co ,iuestioar, He knew at once that</p>
        <p>As for the young man, the situation amused him very much. Everyone was so good to him, he was the guest of John Brandon—that was, o/ clurse, taken to be a guarantee of his social posiLion—and so far as he and his looks were concerned, he was quite charmine.'. Like most folk who have lived in a country a long time, he had little to say of Australia. Had ho aimulv paid it a flvine visit of it funntl: • PIANOS and OAS by the</p>
