        <p>..... .was handsome, but she was old in his eyes. A man never considers himself beyond the age when women will love him—a woman must be content with a few years of the heyday of life. Big harvest is to continue until the great harvester eathera him in. " I shall not be fearful when I go a-wooing," he said, repeating her words, "because I know the ground I shall stand upon. There is some- thing in that, is there not ? I--" He paused. The sun shone full into his handsome eyes, but the thick, dark lashes shading them enabled him to look straight ahead. A man was crossing the lawn—a man whom he instinctively recognised. He was not afraid of Chief Warder Clark, but discretion is the better part of valour. "A visitor," he said, calling Mrs. Cole's attention to the man. "I wonder are you ever in that mood when you feel you cannot talk to strangers ? I am in that mood now. You are so good to me, I wonder whether you would go and meet him, intercept him ? I will wait for you here." She sheltered her eyes with her hand, then she rose. "It's one of those wretched werdees from the prison," she said. "I can't think—upon my word,l can't think—why they worry us. Certainly I will go to him. Naturally you do not wish to be bothered. I consider it a great piece of impertinence that the man conies here at all." She went off at once. Chief Warder Clark had now disappeared behind a clump of evergreens. Vernon Salisbury rose and strolled off. , Mrs. Colo met the man. "You are a little troublesome," she said. "I wonder what you've come for now?" He looked surprised, hurt. "Troablesome ?" he repeated. "Well, you're not like many, and that I must say. Most are going down on their knees to me for taking care of them. Most don't like the thought of a convict at large murdering them in their beds, robbing and thieving and stealing from them frightening them out of their wits! You're a fatly of nerve, perhaps, and don't bother about such things. Others are different. I came to tell you he's still at large, and to bring you his description. He's that artful that, like as not, you'd not know him for a convict if you saw him. But with this paper you'll spot hint at once if you 2.!ome across him, and then 'you'll do your duty, of course, and give him up. He's hereabouts still—that's the one thing that's a moral—and however long he conceals riiirself he can't get away. You've! had nothing in the nature of gentleman's! clothing stolen, I suppose ? " Mrs. Cole took the paper. "Nothing," she said, crossly. "Personally—and I've always s been famed for my clear judgment—l don't ! think the man is anywhere about here. Is it likely ? He got away, of course. Wouldn't you I have ? Shouldn't I have ? I'm not to suppose! he's a born idiot as well as a convict, am I? If t. he is, he ought to be in Broadinoor!" Chief Warder Clark grinned. "Far from it, ma'am," he said. "He's about as cute as they! make them, though I wouldn't wonder if he overreaches himself by being too daring. Most likely I think he'll do that. I hope he will, then we shall catch him. You might run your eye over the description." Mrs. Cole looked at it carelessly, not reading it. "That will do later," she said. "What was the man's crime, anything very bad ?" Chief Warder Clark• had been standing with his hat in his hand, now he put it on again. "Worse than murder in my opinion," he said, "because murder, as a rule, means the slaying of one. Ile Was like Samson, I think it was, he slew his fifties and his hundreds. You remember ' the great Romeo Mordaunt Swindle ? You remember how many homes were broken up, how many lives made desolate, how many husbanda and fathers simply put, an end to their lives when they found everything gone ? this man, this escaped convict, is Roseo Mordaunt. The man is responsible for as much misery and poverty and sorrow in this world as the notorious-- Well, well, I won't mention names, but that's the man that's at large, and if he gets away from England you may take your Javy he'll be up to the same tricks elsewhere, in America or Australia, or some of those parts.' Mrs. Cole nodded. "I shall read the description very carefully," she said, "you may be sure of that. If I see anything of the man-- which, candidly, I do not expect—you shall certainly hear of it. But please do not coins -worrying. It seems absurd and womanish, no doubt, but I don't like the peisotren the hearth, so to speak." Chief Warder Clark laughed. "It would be I worse, would it not, to have the convict on the hearth ? " he said. "And I tell you he's quite etinahlP nf suiph wnrli. even a% that You.W.e.</p>
        <p>all vegetation on with amazing rapidity. Every t-ce and hedge had thickened; grass that had b,en short and spare was bushy now. It was Trite possible the body was very effectually hidden; for. all that, he felt it would ho wise and bitter in every way to dispose, of it differently; it was scarcely likely the Man had not something upon him which would proclaim his identity. Everyone had known the love-story of the two brothers; he did not wish any' auspicion to rest upon John Brendon. He looked round; Mrs. Cole was still talking to Chief Warder Clark. He strolled off, hoping she would not follow him, doing his best to hide from her sight. "A day or so past," he said to himself, with a little laugh, "I should have looked upon her as an angel, a goddess, a creature from another world; to have talked to her, touched her hand, would have seemed to me the height of bliss; my heart would have leaped, my pulses thrilled— now, she is nothing, nothing, because another is everything." He made his way to the spring. -Rather feebly it came bubbling up out of the earth, immediately around it the vegetation was very, very green, very refreshing looking • it trickled away, boring its way into the earth, making a channel for itself by slow degrees, a channel that widened and deepened, that in rainy weather was not too big to accommodate the volume of water, though much too big to do so now. And right from the source of the spring nature had planted grasses and rushes, *shady trees and undergrowth to shelter it. Vernon Salisbury walked close upon one side, parting the leaves, branches and grasses with his walking-stick, carelessly, but eagerly. "What a jump I shall give when I see a white face--4 suppose it will be white—looking up at me," he said to himself, "and yet I shall be glad. How shall I mark the spot so as to be able to know it to-night—to-night, when I must come and fetch him—it—weight it, and sink it in the river ? " He kept a weather-eye around him to see he was not observed; but no one was about—it was hardly likely they would be, the ground being p rivate. "Though those confounded warders seem to think they've the right to wander everywhere," he said to himself. For the first live or ten minutes he whistled as , he walked, switching at the grass and rushes carelessly. Then he grew grave; he was sure, 1, .quite sure, he had passed the place where he had put the body. Who had ree-oved it ? had someone found it, robbed it or all he knew, it might have been worth robbi g—and otherwise disposed of it? "It is possible, of cottise," he said to himself, • "hut it does not meem probable. Any way, it's gone. I'm certain, perfectly certain, I did not hide it further on." For all that lie went right onward to the river, and turned away from that, ,feeling anything but comfortable. "Is it possible anyone saw me that night he said to himself. "If so, it is possible, quite possible, they might have thought me the murderer. lirthat ease--" He paused a minute in his thoujits, then he laughed. "It was too dark for anyone to see my face," he said to himself--"one man would have been exactly like another. Dans la milt toes les chats son' ens. "</p>
        <p>tie Ws,Med on slowly to where the diten charged the little water in it into the river,•then looking puzzled and worried, he retraced his steps, meeting Mrs. Cole,,who, not finding him at the trysting-place, had conic to look for him. "So you grew -tired of waiting," she said, reproachfully. "Well, that is not to be wondered at ; if I had been young and pretty, things would have been different, I suppose ?" She smiled pensively, put her hand for a minute on his coat sleeve. He glanced down at her, and thought how extremely good-looking she was.</p>
        <p>"You are better than pretty," he answered, with downright flattery, "you are handsome— handsomeness lasts, mere prettiness fades yen soon. No, I did not get tired of waiting, though I confess I sin not a patient man as a rulel but it seemed to me that I might just as well walk about as sit there. What did the man want " _ _</p>
        <p>He asked the question abruptly, harshly. The almost angry Dote in his voice pleased her; he had been vexed at her leaving him. "He wanted the escaped convict," she answered; "he seems still to think he is hiding about here —goodness only knows why! I confess I'm a bit tired of the subject. He's given me a full description of the man. l'm to look out for hiss. tea. let -nim know it I See abynOne in the least iike him. We might Lind a seat, and read the ieseription together." Her eyes as they looked into his were a trifle audacious; but widows are, perhaps, allowed a certain amount of latitude. It seemed to her that to sit beside him, their heads bending close over the same paper, laughing at the description of the escaped convict, would be delightful. She was surprised to notice there wAs not much responsive gaiety in his glance; indeed, there was none—his hamdsorae eyes looked grave. If that had not been impossible, she would have se,id they looked afraid, but, of course, he could have nothing to fear. Whether he had , looked grave or no—and, perhaps. one never knew. she had frightened kiim anghtly—ne soon recovered hinuselt ; the smile Came back to his lips, then to his eyes. "That will be very pleasant," he said. "A book, or a poem, or even a description, is always more pleasant to read when two heads are bending over it. I wonder whet the poor wretch is like ? One thing "—he laughed lightly—"we may 1)3 sure of, he has very short hair—l've always heard tle-_,y keep it short in prison." She looked—not because she had the slightest suspicion, eke had not—at his well-covered head. "It would be a wicked thing to cut sonic men's hair short," she said—"especially if it curled. I wonder where we can 'find a sent."</p>
        <p>Had it not been for the paper they were to read together she would rather have chosen a seat in the ehadow; somewhere where a dusky gloom reigned. Under the circumstances this Wag not possible; a felled tree, therefore, suited their purpose. She smoothed out the paper. "I must enlist you as an extra detective," she said. "If you nee this man you also must promise to help in his arrest ; though really, poor fellow—one always says icor fracw, does one not, of anyone who has escaped from prison; and we don't give them a thought otherw;se ?—stv-1- I think he deserves to escape; it seems to in bit would be a little unsportsmanlike to give him She kept on passing her hand over the paper, aggravating him to such an extent that ahneat he felt he mud; snatch her fingers away. Ho wanted to neid the description, to see if now, now he was clothed like a gentleman, it fitted him. He remembered how ha had been photo- graphed at the Government's expense, in what Was gupposed to be ordinary man's attire—a black coat collar' and necktie, dark trousers. He had danced down at himself and laughed. He looked more of a gentleman in his convict's dress then in that get-up, which every man about his size wore to be photographed in. He had stood end sat as awkwardly as possible, though the fit of the garments had been so uncom- fortable, had gripped him so here, left him Fie loose there,- that he really felt anything but easy. He had put on a forbidding expression, not at all habitual to him. If they described him from that liken:?.ss, he did not think the description would tally very greatly with his appearance. Be put his hand on hers for an instant. 'Shall we read it?" he asked, and leant nearer towards her. - -</p>
        <p>She leant her head close to him, and laughing inwardly, though his hemt beat quickly, ha moved his to meet it.</p>
        <p>Perhapa she heard his heart beat ; perhaps she thought the man from Australia beautifully im- pression%ble. She lifted her hand from the pat: so that together they could read the description. And together they scanned it word after word the height, the breadth, the complexion of ths man, the colour of his hair, eyes, the strong marking of his eyebrows, the length of his lashes. Seemingly there was no mark of any particular description on the man, no infirmity even, over so slight. Heading the description of him, he seemed a perfect specimen of manly</p>
        <p>beauty. A queer thrill passed through Mrs. Colo as she read. It seemed so strange, but feature for feature almost was described the man from Australia—the man at her side, John Brandon's friend. It Was a coincidence, of course, but it gave her a feeling that Vernon Salisbury was in some way uncanny. She looked at him, and forced a smile. "Does it strike you in any way :The asked. He was glad she looked down up. n the paper Once more. He had to lick his lips before he could answer. "I'm afraid Ido not understand," he returned. "It strikes me he must be what some folk would call a very presentable man. 15 that what you mean ? " She shook her bead. "I mean, exeepting for the hair," she answered, "it might + be your description. Surel) you see that ? The eyes, the lashes, the brows—it is wonderful. The height, too, must be about the same. It makes me almost afraid." He did not know it, but his fingers closes: over the police description, and crumpled it into a ball. "But," be asked, "surely von are not so mad as to think--" Ile broke off abruptly, and she laughed. "That you are the escaped convict? No, I'm not quite so absurd as to think that, but really, if Chief Warder Clark had happened to see you he might have had a certain amount of enspicion— suspicion which would have leen difficult to dissipate. Perhaps the man is pig-headed. If he had thought you were his convict, you'd have had to prove you were not. If he comes aenin— she laughed excitedly—"l shall not let him see you." Vernon Salisbury drew his handsome eyebrows together. "That betreya a certain amount of , suspicion," he said. "For all that, I'm very much obliged to you. I've no wish whatever for prison warders, or any uncomfortable folk of ' that sort. Besides, I'm not in the least interested in men of any description ; perhaps I've seen too much of them. Women, on the other lnand, find very interesting in the abstract, and in the concrete. Do you want this description any more, or shall we tear it up ?" She looked at him and laughed. "I believe voter(' half afraid of it," she answered. "Yes, of cow se, tear it up. I was only joking ; only it just shows, does it not, how ridiculous a description is ?" "Yes," he returned. "And yet, after all," she went on, "when it collies to describing, one man is very like another —two eyes, a nose, a mouth. I darenay had it been a woman who had escaped, the description would have fitted me quite as well as this has you." "I daresay," he answered, and tore the paper into shreds. "I'm on dangerous ground here," he said to himself. "I'm a fool to stay, lint I can't help it. I shall stay, and I shall fight—fight with the best weapons 17 have—for all I went, and t' only , avant one thing, what every man wants, of I ceurse—the woman."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XX.</p>
        <p>"HAVE, YOU HEARD FROM DICK ? "</p>
        <p>'I bough for a little while, on account of Dick's letter, there been anything but harmony between John and his bride in the railway carriage on the day of their wedding, the disagreement had quickly blown over. Had Celia done what she would have liked to bare done she would have roturned to her home, nritten to her lover, i:rplored him by book or by crook to free her from her marriage. She had sufficient sense, however, to know this was not possible. She also eared very greatly for the material comforts of this world. She could, of course, have refused to live wail her husband. Tho law may direct that man an:: wife shall live together, hut the law is powerless to make them do :;o. She realised, however, that the position of it wife separn ted from her husband is always snit • t....?;).02,1„!</p>
