        <p>nub Ul to very row WI:MUCH who prefer live single lives. She would, so she told herself, have been deliriously happy had Dick been her husband ; but since, so far as that joy was concerned, she nad burnt her boats behind her, she must put up with John, and, no doubt, they would be comfortable enough. It would be awful for poor Dick, of course, when she wont home, saw him, if he really was alive. She hardly know what she would say to him, bow excuse her faithlessness, how in any way make it up to him. He would have the right to reproach her. It was all too terribly and dreadfully sad. Between hei• ,hu,iband's devoted love and her own emotional thoughts of her lover she enjoyed herself very much, far more, probably, than she would had Dick returned in time to stop the wedding. It pleased her to shut her eyes when John took her in his arms, kissed her passionately, and pretend to herself the kisses came from the other man. It seemed to her such a joke against her husband—it was like having two lovers. _</p>
        <p>For a day or two she said not one word of the man who had perhaps returned, though-. thought of him continually; more, indeed, thaa. she had ever thought of him before. A few days of wifehood Imo] browdcworlh viowg of lifr. sue anew now, as she had ifOt -before, how very much man and wife are to each other. What she was to John she would have been to Dick, and since she loved him best she could not but think she would have been happier. On the third morning after her marriage she approached the subject abruptly. Secretly, he had hoped she would have left it alone. A pile of letters lay beside John's plate. She looked from them to him. "I have not asked before' she said, "but should like to know have you heard from Dick ?" Somehow, he had known whet was coming. He did not start or change colour. "No," he smswered. "I did not expect to hear. I told you, dearest. the'lettair was a hoax. "When we return home wo will, if you wish, find out who sent I had hoped you also saw that must be so." She stirred her tea nervously, not looking at him. "I do not think it was a hoax," she answered. " I believe he returned. Are you sure--quite sure, on your honour? Have you not heard from him ? " "On my honour, no; I should have told you it I had."</p>
        <p>She looked at him slowly. "I wonder if you would ? " she said. "It would have been more natural not to, I think. Jack, I am sure that letter was genuine. If you have not heard from Dick, does it not seem strange to you ? " "Strange—in what way ? " "He was in the village the night before our marriage, and he wrote to me. The whole place was alive with preparations for our wedding ; go where he might he must have heard of it, and, having heard of it, I am almost sure he would have come to me, or to you. He did not come to me—did he come to you ? " Somethin6, like a frown crossed hi s face. "No," he answered, "how could he? 1 tell you, the man is dead!" _ _</p>
        <p>She shivered a little. "If he is," she answered, "something happened to him that night. Jack, he is your brother, you ought to be anxious about him.'"</p>
        <p>He leant across the table, and took her hand. "I have nu room for thought of anyone but you in my mind," he answered. "Do you blame me for that? "</p>
        <p>She shrugged her shoulders disdainfully. "I don't blame you, of course, but I don't think I your happfness—l suppose —she laughed softly —"I am your happiness—should make you selfish. I think we ought to curtail our honey- ; moon ; I think we ought to go home and make inquiries." He felt frightened. Make inquiries—that was the last thing they must do! He ha,d heard from 1 Vernon Salisbury—heard that the body had dis- appeared from the ditch where he had hidden it. "Probably," he had added, "someone has robbed, perhaps stripped it, and put it in the river. I thought, however, it was best to let you know ; it might turn up at any time." "And be thought mad for our pains!" he answered. "If that letter had really been from Dick, don't you know that he would have , turned up in the morning; that ho would have come to me if he had not to you ? Do you think he would have come and gone like a thief in • the night, calling upon no one ? Do you think it would have been possible that no one saw him ? My dearest child, you knew Dick as well as I did; you know quite well he was not the sort of man to have suppressed himself, to have taken himself off under any circumstances. Had he come home, as that letter seems to infer, at the eleventh hour, he would have made -a fusa ; he would have done his best to have induced you to give me up ; he would, probably, have half murdered me. And you "—his voice grew very tender—"you are such a foolish little woman that you, probably, would not be my wife now—my happy wife, eh ? " She was a woman who liked excitement—what she called romance. She nodded smilingly. "Yes, I'm happy enough, in a way," she answered. "You—l'm going to be very nice to you, Jack—have made me happier than I thought you could. I like you better than I did -that's natural, perhaps, now 1 am yours and you are mine—but I can't take this thing as quietly as you do. If the letter was a hoax, I should like to prove it a hoax. l'm married to you; l'm your wife so long as our two lives last. I'm going to try to be happy with you, but I can't be quite happy unless 1 know for certain whether the man 1 loved is alive or no. I can't go home and perhaps meet him suddenly, unexpectedly. 1 should break down. You wouldn't like that any more than I should." He dropped her hand; he had been holding it closely clasped in his. "No, 1 should not like it," he answered ; "and I hope you'd have more self-respect. You loved, or think you loved, my brother—that's past, you are now my wife ; that being so, and you a daintily-bred woman, you will put him out of your mind. For all that, and to set your mind at rest, I'll have inquiries made, and let you know the result ; though, upon my word, I don't quite know how I can do so." "You can mention the letter, of course." "And so revive the talk about your engagement to my brother ? I don't know whether you think that will bo pleasant to me ; it's bad enough to know I'm not the first man you loved. I don't wish to insist upon impressing everyone else with the fact." "Everyone else knew it." "I know—l know," he said, impatiently, "but remembrance .even of an engagement does not stick in folks' minds for ever. Still, I will do what you wish, of course. I suppose I shall always do what you wish, clearest." He rose and came round to her, pulled a chair to her side, and sat down. A man is, probably, never so utterly in love as during the first days of his married life; sweetheart and wife are yet one. After is time the sweetheart fades away like a ghost, and the romance of marriage is gone! "Suppose," she said, as he put his arm round her, drew her to him, and kissed her, "suppose Dick had turned up at your place the night before the wedding; suppose he had told you—as I think he would—that he still claimed me, what would you have done ? " His hold tightened. "What do you think I should have done ? " he asked. "I don't know." A curious expression came into his face. "Then I think I will tell you," he said. "I wonder if you will like me the better for it ? 1 think somehow you will, most women would, and all women are alike—.that sounds contradictory, but it isn't really, if you think it out. If Dick had come home on the night before our marriage; if he had threatened to interfere, and I had thought him dangerous—which, mind you, I should not have--I should have killed him!" She started and turned pale; she half eriad out.</p>
