      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>/Mil r one repeated. " our own brother ? "</p>
        <p>"Yes, killed him—my own brother th• e relationship would have been a mere detail, where two men and one woman were concerned. So, you see, it's a fortunate thing for him that he was not alive to come home." She looked at him' very seArchingly, more so than she ever had before. "You must be very fond of r! . Ole said, and the knowledge engendered a feeling of triumph in her heart.</p>
        <p>"I love you!" he answei•ed. “The word sound usual—trite—l daresay, but if those words are• said, and meant, they infer a very great deal. wish you could say the same, meaning it!" s He stooped and kissed her; she a,ccepted, but did not return, the embrace.. "Perhaps I shall somo day " she answered. "Already, as I said, I like you better than I did; and since it's for my happiness to love you now, you may be quite sure I shall if I eau. You• wili write about Dick to-night "If you wish, though I shall fool a fool to do so.''</p>
        <p>"You will let me see the answer ?." "Certainly, though I am sorry you ask; it sounds suspicious—as though you aid not believe I should write. But you may be s_quite sure ure —if Dick had returned wo should have heard of it." .</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
