        <p>Mrs. E. C.. Gore, a well-known lady residing at 39 Claiendon-place, Dover, has suffered such prolonged agonies following acute indigestion that she is anxious, to tell how Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People restored her debilitated digestire organs, gave her new strength, and so enriched her blood that to-day she enjoys better health than ever before. ".1 began to feel run down and depressed," said Mrs. Gore. ' People remarked that I was lookirr, ill, for my face grew worn. Life seemed to drag, and little journeys tired me exceedingly. The company of frienda- beeame distasteful because I was so irritable and my nerves easily upset. "One aftetnoon while shopping a sudden weakness seized me, and the ground seemed to sink beneath my feet. I went home to bed; doctors were summoned and prescribed rest, but before lone I bad violent fits of shivering. My head seemed as though it would split, and I vomited whatever nourishment I took until quite prostrated. My medicine and diet were changed, but the terrible sickness continued. I was placed on boiled milk foods to no purpose. My tongue became thickly coated ; appetite I had none, of coure, and soon I fell away to a shadow. Every breath felt like a knife striking my lungs, and heavy, dull pains hung over my. heart. The doctors then called in a specialist, when ray husband was told that I was a victim of acute dyspepsia and nervous debility. "Stiffness settled in my joints, and my limbs were racked with shooting pains through my elbows and fingers and from my knees to my toes. Every muscle• in my body felt as if constantly being severed with red hot knives. I became so tender that I could not bear to be moved. Doctors said they could not do more for me. The poisons in my system bad set up Rheumatism, and I lingered on exhausted by pain. Friends gave me up, and, indeed, I should have died had it not been for Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
