      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>AGED PEOPLE'S TREAT.—The aged poor of Stockton -Heath were entertained on Tuesday in the Church Insti- tute, when they were given their annual tea. About 90 men and women over 60 years of age sat down to an inviting repast. After tea an excellent entertainment was provided, the chair being taken by the Vicar. Several gramophone selections were given by Mr. Davies, and the choir boys were warmly applauded for well-rendered Christmas carols, their best effort being "The star of faith." The humorous element was in the hands of Mr. Williams, of Liverpool, who kept the audi- ence highly amused with his sketches. The accompani- ments were played by Mr. F. Rose. During an interval in the programme the Rev. H. A. Lester said that after a good deal of waiting they had at last obtained the services of a parish nurse. She lived in the parish and would be able to go round and visit the sick. All the people, especially the old people present, would know the value of a really good nurse. (Applause.) They had secured one who had had a great deal of experi- ence, and she would he' anxious to help any of them. '(Applause.) After the entertainment all the old people received a mince pie each, the old men in addition having a quantity of tobacco and the women a quantity of tea. As in past years Mr. H. J. Westbrook acted as hon. secretary of the Treat Committee, and the success of the function was in no small measure due to his efforts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>HARRY LAUDER AND PEPS. • Recovery from an Attack of Laryngitis. --- Recommends PEPS FOR SORE THROAT AND TIGHT CHEST.</head>
        <p>HARRY LAUDER AND PEPS. • Recovery from an Attack of Laryngitis. --- Recommends PEPS FOR SORE THROAT AND TIGHT CHEST.</p>
        <p>There are few more popular men than Mr. Harry Zander, the eminent Scottish comedian. When he fell a victim to laryngnitis a few weeks ego all England was anxious lest their favourite comedian should lose his fine baritone voice, and be obliged to retire from " the Halls." His re-appearance at Manc‘bester, in better voice than ever, was a pleasant and welcome surprise. In the following letter to the Peps Pastille Co., Mr Lauder throws light on the mystery of his rapid and .complete recovery "Dear Peps,—When I was laid up in London recently -with a very bad throat, I was lucky enough to hear of Peps as a pleasant remedy likely to do me a real good turn. So I obtained a supply of Peps, and they did my throat a world of good. They helped me to throw off an attack of laryngitis, which was so bad that I was almost afraid I should )oose my voice altogether. "Since then I have adopted Peps as a bosom friend. I never think of going to 13.„-&lt;1 without a couple of the pleasant little brown Pens on my tongue. They keep ray voice box in excellent trim; they get ridiof that throat fag which always follows the real hard work of singing and they keep my breathing free and deep. These are facts, and, as Bobby Burns says, " facts are chiels that ninna ding ";—mind a'm tellin ye! I feel that I should be really ungrateful if I did not write to let you know how much I think of Peps, and I heartily recommend them to anybody who is troubled with sore throat or tight chest, and especially to members of my profession. "'ours sincerely, FIARRY LAUDER." This characteristic letter is full of human interest, speaking to all of genuine gratitude ' for a genuine remedy for ills to which all are subject. Mr. Lauder -has proved that the Peps treatment is safe, sound, and scientific, and that Peps literally bring the pure breath of the pine-forest to every home. The Peps treatment is unique, and differs entirely from the cheap lozenges sold by the ounce under similar sounding names. Peps are free from all the objections of old•style and obsolete cough-mixtures. Peps con- tain neither opium, morphia, chloral, cocaine, bromides, nor any other narcotic or sedative. As Peps dissolve pleasantly on your tongue the volatile essences of the pure pines till the month and breathing passages, and are breathed into the lungs. The lining membranes of the throat, nasal cavities and bronch;al tubes are deeply impregnated with the healing, demulcent, antiseptic, and germicidal pine-essences. In this way a potent natural remedy is brought into direct and constant con- tact with the parts affected, healing the sore and bruised tissues, allaying inflammation and pain, encouraging healthy flow of natural secretions, and expelling all imtmrities and accumulations of dust and phlegm. Peps are stocked by all chemists and stores at is. or 2s. 9d. per box, and may be obtained, post, free. at -the same prices, from the Pepq Pastille Co., Carlton Hill, Leeds. Refuse all cheap substitutes and imitations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>LIFE-BOAT SERVICES IN 1906.</head>
        <p>LIFE-BOAT SERVICES IN 1906.</p>
        <p>In 1906, tug in1905-.the first-three _months of the year were somewhat remarkable for the frequent recurrence of gales and storms, which led to much trouble on our coast, and the Life-Boats of the Royal National Life- -Boat Institution were as the result frequently called out on services. These launches were happily instru- mental in saving many lif es from shipwrecks on the coasts of the United Kingdom. The weather of the rest of the year was for the most part remarkably line, there being only a few gales of any force to interrupt its even tenor. The heaviest gales of 1906, frym a life- saving point of view, occurred on the 17-21st January, and 19-22nd November. On the first occasion 31 lives were saved, and 2 vessels, 12 Life-Boats being launched while during the November gale 11 Life-Boats were launched,rosulting in 65 lives and 2 vessels being saved. Between the Ist January and the 31st December, inclu- sive,the Institution's Life-Boats were launched 273 times on service, in addition to which crews were assembled in readiness for service on 46 occa- sions, when their aid was fortunately not required. The total number of lives saved in 1906 by the Life-Boats was 561. In addition, the Institution granted rewards for the saving of 177 lives by shore boats and by other means; and 45 vessels and boats were also saved from total or partial loss. The total number of lives for which the Life-Boat Institution has granted rewards since its establishment on the 4th Mcrch, 1814-92 years ago —has now reached 46,177. The cost of mlintaining the Institution's large fleet of 280 Life-Boats in thorough efficiency is very great, and increases; but the financial report received from the public in annual subscriptions and donations is not nearly sufficient for the purpose. Further help is there- fore, much needed. Annual subscriptions and donations •will be thankfully received and acknowledged by the -secretary, Charles Dilidin, Esq., 20, Charing Cross-road, London, W.C., by any of the Branch Honorary Secretaries, and by all the. Bankers in the United Kingdom.</p>
        <p>'Whilst the swimming baths on Dover sea front were being tilled a large conger-eel made its way through the Inlet pipe from the sea into the baths: After an exciting hunt it was captured. It turned the scale at 14Albs. A Twickenham man whose Christmas revel led to the Brentford Police Court had to pay 20s. and costs for clinging to the whiskers of the policeman who arrested ,him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The' Good Old-Fashioned Winter."—After a succes- sion of comparatively mild winters, we have found our- selves confronted with weather which must satisfy the most ardent admirers of the "good olds-fashioned winters." Some of it is, it may be, have been saying that if these people could have the "good old-fashioned winters "to themselves, they would be heartily welcome to them, and would be in that case at liberty to become as enthnsiastic as they please, whether in verse or prose, concerning the snow storms which they tell us are agreeably seasonable, and picturesque.—Such an experience rpay be pleasing to people who are in a position to watch the snow from the window of a well- warmed room; but the policeman who has to remain for hours in a snaw storm, may be pardoned if he sees the matter in a different light. One has to consider also the position of the poor whose health is imperilled by having to remain at work, or in school, with boots which have been saturated by snow water. Nobody would wish to deny that an expanse of snow is very beautiful in the country, especially in the Lake district, or the other mountainuous areas,but in London and the great towns, snow has not even the gift of beauty, and there is nothing to compensate for the dis- comfort to pedestrians, the delayed railway traffic, the peril to horses and drivers, which arises from frozen , roads, the burst water-pipes and other accompaniments 1 of the " seasonable and picturesque." Weather Prophets and a Hard Winter.—According to accepted country weather lore, if "many leaves wither on the boughs and hang there, it betokens a frosty i winter and much snow." Such a state of things was characteristic of last autumn, owing no doubt to the dry summer, and we are reminded of this fact by those people who profess to be able to foretell a hard winter from Nature's peculiarities. There are so many of these indications of a hard winter, that never a season passes withont our being reminded of them. Numberless proverbs could be found to justify all possible weather conditions, and those who like "to make our flesh creep" with tales of a terrible winter have been telling , us "I told you so" although their triumph is usually ' followed by a speedy downfall. The Royal Party at Chatsworth.—There are few country houses where Christmas and New Year festivities are more fully observed, than at Chatsworth, where the Duke and Duchess of Devonshire have this week had this honour of entertaining the King and Queen, and Princess Victoria. The Royal visit to the " Palace of the Peak "in the opening days of the New Year, has become something of an annual fixture, and the house party invited to meet their Majesties does not vary much from year to year. Shooting, golf, motor-tours, and private theatricals have been the order of the day, the latter entertainments being under the smangement of Mr. Leo Trevor. For motor tours, the Peak district offers many attractions, including the I famous Hardwinke Hall, near Chesterfield; Newstead 1. i Abbey, the home of the Byrons ; and Sherwood Forest. In the ordinary way the indoor and outdoor staff of servants at Chatsworth numbers about 120, but extra hands are engaged during their Majesties' visit and all ' the arrangements are in accordance with the tradition • of splendid hospitality for which Chatsworth is famous. Achen Ambassadress.—Mrs. Bryce, who will accom- pany her distinguished husband when he takes up his ambassadorial ditties at Washington, knows the United States quite well, having travelled a good deal in North America, and she is partly of American parentage. She is the daughter of Mr. Thos. Ashton, at one time a leader of Liberal opinions in Manchester, but her mother although Lancashire born, was the daughter of American parents. Mrs. Bryce married in 1889 and she has always manifested a great interest in public affairs, taking an active part in the Women's National Liberal Association besides being on the Executive Committees of several Associations connected with education and political movements. She is a fluent public speaker, and her tall and commanding presence makes her very effective on the platform. But although occupied with so many pablio interests, Mrs. Bryce believes that home duties should always take first place in a woman's life, and she is not in favour ot extending the Parlia- mentary franchise to women. An Unbecoming Mode.—Although none of our Princesses, and few—very few—of our grand dames patronise the elbow sleeve for day wear, these still remain girls and women v&amp;:, apparently glory in exhibit- ing red, rough, and geneilliy speaking, anything but but prettily shaped arms. In the streets they cover them up with long knitted gloves that look like I , stockings, and, taken altogether, it is long since Dame I Fashion insisted upon so unbecoming a mode. The i Queen has never worn the elbow sleeve, save in an evening gown, the comparatively tightly fitting sleeve, reaching to the wrist, being preferred by Her Majesty , for day wear. I Simple Toilettle.—As a rule, the higher the rank, the ! simpler the dress. There- are many women of high I degree to day, wlio wear, and wear, the quietest, neatest • toilettes, until indeed they almost reach the point of sabbiness, toilettes which in their best days, the "smart "woman would not as much as glance at. And 1 these great ladies set us an excellent example, more especially all those who cannot afford to go to the best ,• modistes. Cheap elaboration is hateful, while true ; simplicity never grates upon our artistic sense, and ' possesses at the same time the merits of iefinement and •, economy. 1 The Coiffure.—Waves and curls are to appear from , the fashionable coiffure, a state of affairs which will ' please the few and worry the mat % The hair must be ! renderel soft ant glossy until indeed it resembles spun silk, drawn softly up to the top of the head, and there ' arranged in a loose artistic looking coil. 'Should the • forehead be high, or the shape of the head demand it, , the hair may be drawn slightly forward in front, but , nothing approaching. a fringe is permissible. The owners of luxuriant tresses will, appreciate the notion, but alas,' luxuriant tresses, like perfect sets of, teeth, ' are few and far between and less favoured ones who know full well the value Of artificial waving and curling ia " helping out "will lament the in-coming style. : Early Swing Wraps.—The little sac coatee which reaches just below the waist, will be much in evidence when the weather permits us -to cast off our warmer wraps. The coatee is cut up at the sid-3s over the hips, has a somewhat full sleeve, and a pretty flat cape-like collar, the latter affording us considerable scope in the matter of colour schemes. Like so many of the modes of the present day, this little garment is suitable only for the slenderly built. " Comfortable " figures, which just manage to wear a skilfully cut bolero, will find that the new coatee is a covering they will do well to leave alone. A Revival.—Sealing wax is coming into favour again, and all over the country the demand for old signet rings is considerable. lany fashionable women are keeping several colours going at the same time, for example white for invitations to weddings and christenings, violet for all expressions of sympathy, black for mourning, and chocolate for invitations to 'dinners, dances, receptions, Scc. Those among us who can find among old stores of trinkets, a suitable signet ring, should lose no time In hunting it out. and tusing it forthwith.</p>
        <p>MAD GE.</p>
        <p>• At the Scarborough Aquarium on Christmas Day a sketch entitled " The Artful Dodger "was brought to an abrupt close by an accident to one of the players who was made unconscious by falling against the</p>
        <p>I scenery.</p>
