        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>.v. nottr, ;men straxti off the broth. To 4 pint of this broth add pint of milk or cream, the v.,c11-beaten eggs, and a little pepper and salt. Put -the minced turkey into a stewpan, moisten it with about half of the broth, &amp;c., and stir over the fire to get thoroughly hot, being. careful not to let it boil. Cut some rounds of bread about in. thick from in:Lf a day old, let these soak in the remainder of the broth, &amp;c., until they have absorbed •as much as possible, then fry them quickly, -both sides, in butter. Spread the minced turkey over them, and serve very 'hot.</p>
        <p>)</p>
        <p>The prisoners who crowded the ceuttyards and sombre hall of Laforce were sitting, standing, or moving restlessly about in all stages of fear, from sullen silence to the most vigorous hysterics. 'Twas a jumpy business, this sudthn capture and despatch to Versailles ga9l ; and these effete Parisian dandies and arimmic girls were not yet sufficiently used to the daily arrest of friends and the speed .of the guillotine knife to take such proceedings quite calmly. A 'little later they would Pull themselves together and meet, death in proud, stern silence ; but just now the crash of that knife disconcerts their' -nerves. When they are arrested they ask "What for ? " in terrified astonishment, and their gaolers in answering occasionally suffer lapses of politeness and pity. In a word, nobody except a certain rather awesome tribunal in Paris has quite settled down to business, and on this May morning the gay sunlight .and flying cloud-shadows and spring's strong life-sap mock the bare idea of death.</p>
        <p>Especially to one pair of new-corners here the prospect of such a gory business as is going on with.daily-increasing rapidity in the Place d 4,3 la Revolution seemed simply ridiculous.- One Norval, restaurant-keeper of the Rue de Seize in Paris, and his ten-year-old daughter Helene. These persons had sold pâtés and salmis, calf's head with olives and boar's head stutTed with truffles and chestnuts, to anybody who could pay for them and a good many persons who could not. They had not to their knowledge poisoned any high Republican functionary with doubtful mushrooms or given him a stomach-ache with corked claret, and why in the course of one of their numerous visits to Versailles they should suddenly find themselves in Laforce Orison was a mystery about which they were ,still talking. "We shall, without doubt, b 3 released directly after our trial this morning," repeated the man s for the hundred and eiglAth time during the past twenty-four hours. "Perhaps the judges will even see that it would be right to compensate us for the loss of a day. I shall certainly put it to them," added Monsieur Norval, seriously. The news of the day buzzed about the Restaurant Norval as it did elsewhere, but the greatest of philosophers have been known to.fall into bewilderment when a wide and extensive scheme of injustice, concerning which they have hitherto shrugged philosophic shoulders, suddenly includes themselves. "Without doubt, father," • repeated little Mademoiselle Norval, 'very doubtfully indeed. "But, oh, I am frightened 1 I am dreadfully frightened I" "Nay; mignon, thou hast nothing to to afraid of. Leon, or one of the other waiters, will feed thy mice and canaries, and to-night thou wilt be at home twain eating a supper of the apricots and cream Aiech thy greedy little soul loves so well. . . A customer of ours, surely ? Yes, it is the sister of the poor Due de Camnartin. She used to be sometimes among his parties in our sa7,oas."</p>
        <p>A tall and very beautiful girl swept across the courtyard looking evidently for a little seclusion and silence, and eyeing her too numerous com- panions with frightened vexation. Norval and the child rose and bowed low to her, but the respect of their obeisance obviously could not make up to the young patrician for the humiliation of being a co-prisoner with this mob, and she returned a cold stare and the faintest possible movement of her head. "Truly, a very lovely young lady," said Norval, sympathising with the girl's fear too much to notice her rudeness. "She is betrothed to the Marquis de Chartres, who came here just after us yesterday morning and did me the honour to recognise me last night. He loves thee, my baby. Dost thou remember when lie gave thee the bon- bons and asked if thou wouldst come and be a little daughter to him for half the year? And thou said that thou wouldst not leave me for one day of one month. . • Ali I he is corning to speak to us. Thy best curtsey for him, little Helene." A young man, whose plain, businesslike dress— so plain as tci suggest that he had been arrested in some attempt at disguise—was in noticeable contrast to his Court manner and delicate, bored-looking face, made his way through the crowd; or rather the waves of humanity carried him passively forward, since Monsieur the Marquis de Chartres looked as if he would have scorned to make his way anywhere for any purpose whatever, till he was delivered up in front of the Norvals. The same waves, not without assistance in this case from Other powers of attraction, carried Mademoisellt de Caumartin to the side of the marquis. She arrived in time to hear him being assured by Monsieur Norval that Helene was alarmed by their arrest, but that he himself was caliu, counting on freedom this afternoon at latest. Mademoiselle, , standing near her lover and opposite to . the reatanrant-keeper, suddenly permitted a certain consciousness of the latter's existence to appear on her face, and even listened graciously to what he was saying. Then she muttered something in the marquis's ear, in reply to which he nodded assent and looked nervously round him. The waves had retreated again, and the little group of. four Persons stood alone in a corner of the courtyard. Mademoiselle... de Cautuartin put out two long delicate fingers and gave Helene a little push. "Stand aside for a few minutes' child," she. said. "The marquis wishes to speak privately to your father." Monsieur Norval put a-large plump hand on his little daughter's shoulder, so near to the taper fingers of mademoiselle that the latter were snatched away with an angry exclamation, and turned to 'the marquis. "If you wish to tell me secrets, Monsieur the Marquis," he said, directing glances of disapproval at de Chartres and Marguerite alternately, "I must listen, and of course I will help you in any reason- able way; but will you in your clemency remember that I do not love hashes or stews in life or/on the dinner-table? As for Helene here, she has kept for three years the secret of my turbot a la Norval, and—l prefer to have her near me now." "As you please, my friend," said de Chartres with another very doubtful look round. "Yet I am about to speak ,of somewhat serious affairs. It "—the man lowered his voice to a whisper—" in effect it is of a plot to rescue the Queen.' "Of a plot to--- Ah, Heaven help me, Monsieur the Marquis I You must tell me." "He is not asking you to take any part in it," interposed mademoiselle, with a perceptible dash of contempt in her voice. "Let me explain the matter, Marguerite," said de Chartres, irritably; for Norval and his daughter were eyeing their companions /with open dislike. "What I am now asking of you, Monsieur Norval, is indeed only a trifling service, though if I wanted far more dangerous work done I would ask you quite as readily. A former valet and very good friend of mine called Lafone is on guard in the Queen's passage • at the conciergerie to-night.. I have seen him, and he has promised, if possible, to bribe his fellow-guard and one of the liter porters, and to let me into the Queen's cell. TYe are not sparing money, and he will succeed in per- suading the guard and porter. I am helpless here, and if Lafone makes his attempt to-night, and nothing happens, he will be denounced. I want Lafone warned that. I run helpless." "Oh, Monseiur the Marquis I " exclaimed Helene, standing with clasped hands and admiring eyes. "Is it impossible for you to go out even for to-night? You can do nothing ? " "We are devoted servants of her Majesty, Helene and I," said Norval, in explanation of the child's words. -"My wife was a s;!rvatit at the chateau here, and had the good fortune to attract the attention of her Majesty who even reineroered</p>
        <p>, her aft-e? her marriage, ana , sent us down to Ilarfleur When Madame Norval was ill. We would do much to help her Majesty, and "—with a side- glance of dislike at Mademoiselle de Caumartin— "we seem likely to have more power to aid her than some others who profess more willingness. Could no one play your role at ie conciergerie to-night, Monsieur the Marquis ?" "Impossible, my good Norval." "Father "—the little lady, who had been pondering something with puckered brows and slowly-rising colour during the past few moments, looked up suddenly with flashing eyes— "could not the marquis take your name and place at the trial this morning, and go out just for to-night under your name ? No one here knows any of us, and I would say the marquis was my father. To-morrow, when her Majesty is safe, Monseigneur will come back and say that he is the marquis and not you." "A good idea," murmured Mademoiselle de Caumartin with condescending approval. "This is a very intellgent child. I should think--:—" But the expression of mademoiselle's valuable thoughts was checked by the look which she saw on the two men's faces. Very steadily they were regarding one another, with eyes whch understood one another's thoughts. Then said Norval otlowly nod soberly: "I am ready, Monseigneur. It is infinitely improbable that you or I will be known to the gaolers here. As for the result of our trial this morning, I imagine that my chance of freedom is about an even one, and that the odds are a whole sheep to a cutlet against yours. I will give to her Majesty my even,chance. "Monsieur Norval," said the other, quietly, after a long pause, "we must. speak very plainly. The Marquis de Chartres will almost certainly be con- demned to death at his. trial. I must take the Queen to the coast. .You hardly, I hope, require my assurance that I shall come back with all speed, but I cannot be Sure of coming back in time.' " Philippe ! " Marguerite de Caumartin' s whispered word sounded as if she had been suddenly and violently stabbed. "You are raving! Condemned to death! Tell me what you have done! . . . Oh, it is impossible! You must escape! You shall! Think—think!" "It i impossible, dearest.," said de Chartres, rather imratiently. His fiancee's infatuation for him had been rather tiresome to the man in tile Tuileries. Here it seemed likely to become a con- siderable nuisance.</p>
        <p>"It cannot be impossible! My love! My dear, dear love, you cannot die! You shall not die without me. And see, Philippe, this brave Monsieur Norval has offered you his chance. You will not take back your promise, Monsieur Norval? You arc so brave, and it is for the Queen's sake, whom you say that you wish to serve I- You will bold to your promise ? " ' "No, he will not."</p>
        <p>In response to the very resolute '-young voice which interrupted her, mademoiselle turned to Helene in chilly anger. "Who has asked you to decide for him, child? Who wants your opinion ? " "The gaolers would like to have it very much, and I will tell it them," said Helene through set teeth, and looking eagerly round the courtyard. , Some angrily whispered words from de Chartres to Marguerite, and the latter with a desperate effort at self-control changed her manner to one of kindly persuasion. "Of count, we could not do anything without your consent," .she said, putting a caressing hand on Helene's shoulder, "but indeed, dear little mademoiselle, your father would not die. To- morrow, when the Queen is out of Paris, he would say who he was, and of course everybody would believe him. Why do you laugh? How dare you laugh at what I say to you? Au I you would go to that man? Monsieur Norval !"</p>
        <p>At the far end of the courtyard a gaoler had appeared. Helene, catching sight of him, had run forward, and mademoiselle had tightened her hand on. Heleae to the sudden grasp of an iron Tice, shaking the small person furiously. And then a yet stronger and more sudden force, in the shape of an upward blow from the restaurant-keeper, had knocked mademoiselle's hand off the child's shoulder.</p>
        <p>"Pardon, Mademoiselle de Caumartin," said Norval, white with rage. "You forget yourself. customs are changing, not without need, as I see, and you shall lay no hand on my daughter in that fashion. She knows as well as you do that I Oliould not be believed or released if just before my execution I declared that 'I was somebody else, and your method of persuading her is ridiculous." "Persuading her," repeated the girl in a fury, "I would whip her till she fainted! These shop children! . . . Ah, no, Monsieur Norval. I did not mean all that, but I am so horrified to think that a chance of saving—of saving the Queen is to be thrown away." Norval turned from her with a gesture of con- tenipt and put an arm round the scared child. "Do not think of this lady at all, beloved," he whispered. "Think only of what you and I and Monsieur de Chartres can do for the Queen. He may save her if he can o fee. 'That is all that matters to us. We are thinking 'only of the Queen's life, darling, and this is a way to save it. Itsides, it is just possible—not very likely, as we know, but just possible—that when I declare myself, and if—nay, I mean when—the marquis returns, I may be set free. Wouldst thou keep me back, little Holene ? Not if I know thee well. Here oome the men. My baby will be brave now, Won't she?" A silence had fallen on the crowd, and from the far side of it came a loud, good-humonred voice:</p>
        <p>"Citizen de Chartres! 'Where is the Citizen de Chartres? They ought to be labelled in big letters directly they come in. Half one's day is spent in looking for them. Now,. citizen! My faith, I would call him the Marquis de Chartres if it would make him come faster; and then I would break both the legs of the Marquis de Chartres so as to be mire of identifying him again. You there, aro either of you the Citizen de Chartres ? " "Yes. monsieur. You want me ? "</p>
        <p>A; Norval stepped forward Helene held her breath for a moment and looked round in half mad terror, while her father and the two others watched her in helpless anxiety. Everyone stood motion- less, and the seconds went by with a life-time of suspense in each. Prompted by what suspicion he himself scarcely knew, the gaoler's companion came forward suddenly and stood in front of do Chartres.</p>
        <p>"Who are you?" he asked, abruptly. "Norval, a restaurant-keeper in the Rue de Seize," was the somewhat uneasy answer. "So, and this is your daughter? I heard her tell someone she was Helene Norval. Well, cheer up, little one," he went on, .touching the child's hair with a kindly hand; "nothing very terrible will happen to you." The little person pointed to her father and asked, In a tremulous whisper: "What will happen, to him?" For answer the man looked at her for a moment. and then beckoned to the deputy gaoler. "Here, Saxe. Lend me thy eyes and brains for a brief moment. They are not worth much, but the dwarf on the giant's shoulder may chance to see further than the giant. This child, who calls herself Helene Norval, came in yesterday with this man who has just called himself the Marquis de Chartres; and she has been by his side all day. A man, thou wilt agree, I think, would be unwise who called himself the Marquis de Chartres just now except under very stern necessity; and yet— what dost thou say, old friend ? " "With de Chartres all day?" was the laughing answer.. "And ten years old? And what do I say? I say fie Thou wilt have seen something ! of life, mignon,. by the time thou art twenty!" The senior gaoler turned away with a pettish I exclamation at the other's lack of comprehension, and found himself face to face with Marguerite de Caumartin. "You there, young madam! Do you know these three persons?" he asked. Throwing a frightened, appealing look at Helene, mademoiselle pointed to Norval. "That is a friend of mine, the Marquis de Chartres," she said. "I don't know either of these other two." With hesitation and irritation on his face the man looked from one to another of the little grour. "Still in doubt? " asked his companion, laughing again. "I should have thought inxself that if my prisoner here were not de Chartres no one would be more anxious than himself to prove it, but all the anxiety is on thy side. Come, wilt thou take the child's word for which is her father and which—oh fie i for shame !—is her lover ? Tell us, little maid,' which is thy father ? " Before ',he sentence was quite finished Helene bad run to Norval, and scarcely conscious of what she was doing was clinging to him with sobs and convulsive trembling. He bent down over her with his arms tightly round her and whispered: "It is for the Queen, beloved; be brave! Thou must be very brave now, my darling," and in answer to his appeal the child controlled, herself for a moment to pint to de Chartres and gasp out: 'He is my father. I thought everybody knev.</p>
        <p>that." ".Then go to him, child," said Saxe, who wns vetting a trifle imnatient. "-Atigther lady wants</p>
        <p>tuY toter, ana Will, r tear, sent-trim. manamc la Guillotine allows no rivals. Coins, citizen, I must take 'your word for it that you are do Chartres. If you have only recently adopted the name I must tell you that your choice of a minnent for doing so is a very unfortunate one."</p>
        <p>As Norval passed flu-31101 the crowd out of sight the marquis pointed to Relene and whispered some- thing to Marguerite. Helene heard it apparently, :for she turned to Mademoiselle de Caumartin. "You are to console Sue! You!" muttered the small creature, with such a passion of scorn in her voice that mademoiselle flushed StrFlet with anger. The forlorn little figure stood very upright for a raomentswith eyes defying her enemy to come any nearer; then it dropped on a bench near by, shaking with terrified sobs. The assistant-gaoler returning at the moment stood over it with a look of amused pity. "Is it as,. bad as all that, chtrie?" he asked, laughing kindly. "Nay; but there are better lovers to be had than the Citizen de Chartres. He was not worth., all these tears." With an affectation of great alarm, Marguerite came forward artd addressed the man.</p>
        <p>"What is the accusation against the Marquis (14 Chartres, monsieur?" she asked. "I pray you Loll me. lam terribly anxious." "As you probably know very well, young madam, he was the Queen's secretary. And if that is not enough his judges will hear something more about him this morning which will settle his business in this world."</p>
        <p>Glancing fearfully at Helene in alarm lest this answer should drive her to speech, mademoiselle moved back as if satisfied; but one of the spectators who had gathered round the little group asked, with casual curiosity: "Smalling more? Tell us about it, good Saxe. We are horribly, dull this morning." "Well, it is no great secret," said the old gcisip, who asked nothing better of life- than a dramatic tale to tell and an interested audience. "A plot for the Queen's escape is suspected, and when we suspect such plots we just go On _executing peop!e till it is disposed " of. Doubtless all of you here," looking round his audience with a grim smile, "would hasten to denounce such an infamy if you chanced to hear of it."</p>
        <p>"Has anyone besides this marquis been arrested in connection with it?" asked another bystander; and as he spoke Marguerite saw Helene raise her head and look from the gaoler to the Marquis da Chartres' and then at herself. The terrified -appeal in mademoiselle's eyes seemed to hold the chile: spellbound and _silent, while the man's narrative went slowly on. "Hardly anybody. They tell me it is a feel:le little affair," he began. Then noticing suddenly that Helene had raised her head he nodded kindly to her. "Feeling better, little maid?" be asked. "That's rWit. Thou wilt be at home again to- day, I hea?! so be happy." There was another moment of silence. The child's face, growing slowly scarlet., was taped to Saxe, and her lips were parted as if to speak. Marguerite* turned away, white and sick; nothing could save her lover now. . . . But Saxe's voice went on again, and with a gasp of relief Marguerite looked round to see if she could get near to Helene and speak to her unobserved, if she kad had a knife with her she .would have stabbed the child without a second's hesitation.</p>
        <p>• "Yes • they have arrested a poor friend of mine called Lafone ; but that will be nothing. They cannot seriously suspect him. And the Queen has been moved into another part of the prison. That is all just now. . . . Who calls Saxe? Ah, is it thou, my friend Let me pass, citizens. The good Picard wants me, and is looking as if he were going to execute us all on the spot." The gaoler pushed his way towards the prison doorway, where his superior was standing, and the crowd, with the most lively personal interest in the communication, surged after him. Helene, still absorbed in comprehending what she had just heard, leant against her bench with flushed cheeks and flashing eyes; de Chartres stood near with folded arms and repressed fury in every line of his face and body. Marguerite, watching the child as a cat watches a bird, wondered whether she could spring at her and choke her without. untimely discovery. Suddenly the full meaning of Saxe's narrative seemed to dawn on Helone, who shook herself, pushed her hair back off her fore- head, and turned round to see where the gaoler had gone. In an instant Marguerite was on her knees in front of the child, and her arms were round her. "Spare him, 1141ene " she whispered in passionate entreaty. "Child, child, for the love of Heaven, II pray you, spare him! See, I kneel to you and beg your pardon for being rude to you, and implore you to keep silence. He is my lover, and if he dies I shall kill myself I " "What do 1 care for your lover ? " answered the little maid, struggling furiously. "He is. a coward. My father shall--' "Listen, Helene. You shall come and live with us, and be a little sister of mine, and I will love you and take etre of you always as if you were my sister. There is nothing on earth you shall not have. Ask for anything and I will swear to give it to you." "Then let me go! " cried the child with a stifled scream. "Helene, dearest little Helene!" Marguerite's words poured out in a scarcely coherent torrent— "don't you see that it is too late now to confess anything? If we all confessed they would not believe us here; and even if they did they would lie angry with Monsieur Norval and execute him as an accomplice of ours. You cannot save him; you can only send my lover to death too. Whht good can it do your father if the Marquis de Chartres dies as well as himself ? Spare ni• lover to me ! Helene, Helene I For the love of the Holy Virgin spare him I You will! . . . I can see in your dear little face that you will not send him to death just to revenge yourself on me. See here, dear," cried the girl, rising suddenly r‘from her knees and releasing Helene, "I am so sure you would not do such a dreadful thing that I hold yen no longer." The Marquis de Chartres had come forward, and the meaning of such few sentences in.lhis appeal as had come to his ears dawned at last Tln his brain. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Is it conceivable, Marguerite," he asked, 'bitterly, "that you are entreating the child to keep silence, and suppose that I shall keep silence too? Heaven above us 1" he Went on, recoiling a step as the girl lifted her hands in entreaty. "She doer, indeed, suppose it! Here you, monsieur."—with a gesture of indignant rage the marquis llRvanced to a gaoler who was coming towards that corner of the courtyard—" there has been a blunder. Nay, little one," lie continued, waving back Helene, who was also running towards the head-gaoler. "You have reason enough truly to suspect us all of being cowards, but you had better leave me to tell my story first. You shall correct it afterwards, if you please. Listen, Monsieur Picard. We have been playing a little comedy, and I must explain it to you. "It his already been explained, citizen," said the other, drily. "It has rather\an ill look about it so far as you are concerned. We have not exactly remarked you as the hero of the piece. The Citizen Norval has just been recognised by one of the porters and has been :brought back to the prison- yard yonder. Go to him, child," he commanded, turning angrily to Helene, "and bid thy teachers explain to thee what happens to children who tell lies. Now, Citizen de Chartres, out here, please. There is no time for farewells. We have lost time enough already, and the court will not care to waste a morning over so simple a case as yours. Ten minutes in the court this:morning, and as many seconds in the Place de la Revolution to-morrow. . . . I That will be all." [THE END. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DRESS-FASIION-110ME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASIION-110ME.</p>
        <p>AN INVALUABLE CHURCH WORKER.</p>
        <p>Church work in South London had 'no more enthusiastic helper than the lady whose name will always be associated with the women's branch of the South London Church Fund, says the Globe. This was organised by Miss Henri- etta M'Arthur, and was carried on in large measure by the work of ladies' maids in the houses of the West End. It was ' Miss M'Arthur who personally organised the 'United Girls' Sfthools Mission in that part of London, and her efforts on behalf of friendless girls in the poor parishes of Lambeth will long be remembered. Allied to keen sympathy and deep religious feel- ing was a practical skill in organisation only too rarely found.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>USEFUL COSTUME,</head>
        <p>USEFUL COSTUME,</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is a very smart and becoming little costume suitable for useful wear. The material in which it is achieved is a heavy blue serge, with a rather marked " herring- bone'? in its weave. The coat, as you will ob- serve, is of the fashionable short-waisted sack shape. From the neck turns back a small collar of White cloth, banded with blue-and-gold braid.</p>
        <p>Down the front to about the level of the waist) is arranged a succession of wide tucks, which are stitched down quite flat, edged on the inner side, just by the fastening, with a band of the blue- and-gold braid, and crossed sat intervals by straps of the same braid. At the bottom of these tucks comes a pointed band of the serge, trimmed with the braid, from which springs a little pleated frill. The skirt is quite plain, and is cut of sensible walking length.</p>
