      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MRS. DORA B. MONTEFIORE.</head>
        <p>MRS. DORA B. MONTEFIORE.</p>
        <p>Not the least conspicuous among the group of educated women who are actively agitating for the Parliamentary vote is Mrs. Dora B. Monte- sore. Her protest this spring against taxation without representation is fresh in the minds of all newspaper readers, and perhaps, says P.T.0., that "imprisonment" in her, own house on Ham- mersmith Mall may count in the annals of the cause as equal to the later experience of her col- leagues in Holloway Gaol. Since that episode, however, Mrs. Monteflore has been on her travels. She went over to Copenhagen to take part in the International Congress held in that city, and afterwards paid a visit to the en- lightened Grand Duchy of Finland—enlightened inajimuch as it has seen fit to entrust its ,women with the vote. Mrs. Montefiore is a keen student of the Russian language an literature. She is a member of the Russian Circle at the Lyceum Club, and delivered an Address there just before the end of the season On Maxim Gorky. reading also her ow* translation of one of his most eharacteristio articles which had appeared in a Russian raw. PRACTICAL SHIRT.</p>
        <p>The subject of our sketch is a simple but very smart little shirt of the latesi and most up-to- date type. Carried out in flannel, cashmere, , nun's-veiling, or delaine, it would be suitable for every-day morning wear; in silk or velvet it iNwould make a charming accompaniment to a. ! smart afternoon costuine. But to turn to the 1 blouse it-3clf. Down the front runs a wide piss- ' tron-shaped piece, • machine-stitched on the edges, and adorned with large handsome but- . tons. Over erch shoulder comes a little sloping</p>
        <p>poke, also' machined at the -edges, from which spring two groups of- fairly wide stitched tucks. The back has also a yoke, which slopes •up from the shoulders to a point in the middle of the back; where there is arranged a group of tucks similar to those in front. The sleeves are very moderate in size, and are of the small bishop type.</p>
