      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Ptomaine Poisoning at the Industrial</p>
        <p>Schools.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday when there were present :—Mr. Brooks (presiding) Dr. Murray, Messrs. 0. Mather, G. Thompson, W. Knowles, W. Houghton, J. Backhouse, J. Ball, J. C. Sutton (clerk). B. S. Newcombe (surveyor). H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), and F. W. Kerfoot (inspector). OPENING OF TENDERS. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. E. HICKS, district auditor, reported that in the past the practice had obtained of the Surveyor receiving and inspecting all tenders for his department and marking on a schedule submitted to the Highways Committee the names of the firms and the prices that he recommended for acceptance. That proceedure was open to objection. Much of the value of obtaining tenders lay in the genorally accepted method of open- ing them ~before the committee in order that their information when first disclosed should be common to all,</p>
        <p>it was decided that in future all tenders should be opened in the presence of the Council. NEW BYE-LAWS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a lengthy communication from the Local Government Board respecting the proposal of the Council to adopt new bye-laws. The matter was referred to the chairman and surveyor to report upon to a future meeting of the Council. Mr. KNOWLEM : If we adopt any section of the Public Heath Act I want that section adopted which deals with music and dancing licences,</p>
        <p>LIGHTING OF WINWICK-NOAD, Mr. KtrowLms brought forward the question of the lighting of the highway to Winwick. He would pro- pose that gas lamps be fixed as adopted by the Warring- ton Corporation, on the high road from Longford to Winwick Church. A large number of lamps would be required and he suggested that the Surveyor should approach the Corporation and ascertain the expense. Mr. BuooKs suggested that the matter should be left to the Winwick Parish Council. Mr. KNOWLES : The Parish Council is not the most business-like of bodies. I have been asked by the Asylums Committee to bring the matter forward. This is a main road and it is a disgrace to us as a district that it is not lighted. The CLERK pointed out that there was a great differ- ence between Winwick and the other parishes. In Winwick they had a very large public institution and the parish required much more consideration than the inhabitants were perhaps prepared to give. Some years ago when the asylum was being erected, it was intended to light the road but the matter was not proceeded with on account of the South Lancashire tramways who would have hai to light the road if the tramways ha d been carried through. It was decided that tho Clerk should write to the Chairman of the Winwick Parish Council explaining that the question had been raised. The surveyor was also instructed to ascertain the cost of lighting the road.</p>
        <p>OUTBREAK OF PTOMAINE POISONING. Mr. lIBRFOOT reported that there had recently been an outbreak of ptomaine poisoning at the Cottage Homes, Padgate. The suspected food had been sub- mitted to Dr. Carter Bell for analysis. Mr. MATHER said he was rather inclined to think it was the Christmas feeding. About 100 children had been affected but he thought it was a storm in a tea cup.</p>
        <p>COST OF THE SVOW FALL. Mr. NEWCOMBE reported that it nad cost the Council £3O to clear the snow in certain parts of the district.</p>
        <p>ROAD ENCROACHMENTS. The SURVEYOR reported that he had seen Mr. Shaw of Winwick as to the two encroachments as reported at the last meeting. He admitted the offence and was agreeable to setting both fences back to a line the Surveyor might fix for him. In further correspondence he felt that the Ecclesi- astical Commiesioners were weakening as to the encroachment in Back-lane, Hulme. They were sending theieSurveyor down to arrange the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>JANUARY sth 1907.</p>
        <p>Of New Year resolutions DRINK many, it may safely be AND THE surmised, have already been MILLIONAIRE. broken. The more the pity of it. Generally the reso- lutions refer to the giving up of something that is non-essential. Most frequently it is the use, or the abuse, of intoxicants. A man will give up drinking altogether, or he will limit himself to so many glasses a day, or he will take nothing intoxi- cating until he is on the point of going to bed. There is thus a widespread acknowledgement from the drinkers that " drinking " in the most commonly accepted use of that word, is a great evil, to be fought with individually, at the cost of effort and sacrifice. Even those who jeer at the teetotalers know that they have themselves got the worst side of the bargain. They cheer themselves by the reflection that they belong to a noble army of conscripts. Every time they lift their hands to their mouths they are helping the CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER Out of his annually-recurring difficulties about money. Everybody knows how, year by year, Mr. GOSCHEN was wont to beam over the remunera- tiveness, from the Exchequer point of view, of the bad habits of the people. Sometimes it was rum that came to the rescue ; sometimes it was the all- round consumption of stimulants; but always in those times there was something for a Chancellor of the Exchequer to rejoice over, there was always the solid, jingling proof that we were not a sober people.</p>
        <p>It is greatly to be regretted that as drink did so much for Mr. GoscuEN, now a Peer of the realm, he did not contrive to do somethieg useful with the funds which it placed in his hands. It is difficult in these days to call to mind what became of his surpluses. They seemed to melt away. No Chancellor of the Exchequer over had so great a reputation on so small a ground of public advan- tage. The public funds used to be spoken of as " Goschens." We shudder at the thought of " Goschens " now. They are down to—what are ; they down to P It is much more agreeable to learn from Mr. GOSCHEN'S successors that the pro- ductiveness of the national vice is not now what it used to be. We do not drink ourselves out of our financial liabilities to nearly so great an extent as we did only a few years since, This must be a great blow to those who have been accustomed to argue that the free consumption of intoxicating liquors is the foundation of our national stability, and therefore of our national liberties and our greatness generally. All of us remember how during the early days of the Boer War the rattling chorus of " Beer, beer, glorious beer" used to mingle itself with "The Cock of the North" and other inspiring strains. Beer is not now living up to its reputation, from the C; °schen point of ,view, or from that of the great brewery cempanies,which no longer give £60,000 or £70,000 for a roadside public .11 ous o. Exit the drinker—to some extent, at least— enter tho millionaire. The proud title is given in France to those reputed to own 1,000,0001., in America to those who own 1,000,000d01. ; but in our own country only to those who have £1,000,000 in what ROBERT LOUIS STETENSON called "good, solid, minted quid." There are more of these than could have been dreamt of by the most riotous imagination fifty years ago. What an almost illimitable fortune £lO,OOO a year seemed to SAMUEL WARREN and his small-minded hero, MT. TITTLEBIT TITMOUSE ! Why the English manager of an American life assurance society draws as much as that nowadays in salary. Few of us would blush to put a value of £lO,OOO a-year on ourselves if we were very badly wanted. And so as the revenue from drink goes down the revenue from millionaires goes up. The big fish are not all caught in the net of the Chancellor of the E xchequer. Some of them succeed in transferring much of their property before they are startled by the appearance of Death on a pale horse. But even some Dukes do that. One of them did it very successfully, we remember, and with the help of Parliament, which does not seern to have been alive to the sort of business that was being transacted. Such is tile the sense of national obligation prevalent in the very highest ranks of society! However, a suffi- cient number of millionaires has been caught since ,the commencement of the present financial year to</p>
        <p>ensure a reduction of taxation. Lst U 3 bs grate- ful, then, to SIR WILLIA.M HARCOURT :whose financial genius so successfully tapped this large source of national revenue.</p>
        <p>Eight of our townsmen THE NEW will this week end—if they MAGISTRATES, are blessed with becoming modesty—be blushing under he newly-conferred honour of Justice of the Peace for the through. It has been no secret for a week or two that a number of names had been submitted to the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lan- caster and yesteriay it was officially made known that the following gentlemen had been added to the roll, i.e. Councillor Burton, Councillor Evans, Councillor Ellison, Councillor Stott, Mr. Thomas Locker, Mr. John Maginn, Mr. William Furness and Dr. Markel. It is nearly three years since there was a previous appointment of borough magistrates and on that occasion twelve gentlemen were honoured with the affix " J.P." Oa that occasion four were. Liberals and eight Conservatives. The appoint- ments this week consist of seven Liberals, one Labour and no C ons9rvatives. Apparently Sir Henry Fowler attaches more importance than Lord Loreburn to the enormous dtsparity-13,000 to 3,000—0 f Tory over Liberal magistrates in the country. We (pito agree with Lord Lotehurn's emphatic refusal to make the post a "party appan- age," and if other Lord Chancellors had shown an equal sense of honour and equity the justices of the peace would not have been, in the majority, so pronouncedly of one colour. The justificatioo for sacrificing principle for party expediency does not seem very sound, but there can be no doubt that it is against the public interest for there to be so large a pre. ponderance of one party upon the Bench. Liberals are looking forward to seeing the grievance Iredressed and in Warrington at any rate they can have no cause to complain about the new appoint- ments. Eight men who have devoted considerable time and labour to the public service have been selected and we have no doubt that they will endeavour to worthily uphold the best traditions- of the office. We have much pleasure in congratu lating them upon the honour to which they have attained.</p>
        <p>The Sanitary Works Corn- BRICK-BUYING mittee appears to have got EXTRAORDINARY. itself into a tangle over the purchase of some thousands of bricks, and the assistance of the Council is implored. A peculiar chain of incidents is revealed in the minutes for Tuesday's Council meeting. On December 5, the committee accepted Messrs. R. Evans and Co.'s tender for 30,000 bricks at 225. per 1,000 subject to delivery at the depot. On the 18th December it was reported that Messrs. Evans required a further 2s. 6d. per 1,000 for carting, and the resolution accepting their tender was. rescinded, Then the tender of the Warrington Slate Company for Greengate bricks at 245. 6d. per 1,000 was accepted.At a further meeting on Decem- ber 20th, the Chairman reported that on December sth,the tender of Messrs.W. and J. R;chardson for Ravenhead bricks had been mislaid and over- looked, and it was now found to be id. per 1,000 cheaper than the one accepted from the Slate Cempany. Under the circum- stances the Council are asked for their instructions in the matter, though the Committee has also accepted Messrs. Richardson's tender. Whether the Committee really wants 39,000 or 60,090 bricks is not made clear, but two 30,000 lots have been accepted at prices differing id. per 1,000. Toe difference only represents 2s. 61. on the whole cmtract, but the Committee seem to have made a bungle of the business and finally left it in a position which requires further elucidation.</p>
        <p>Warrington's interest in the MUSICAL advancement of music has WARRINGTON. appreciably increased of late years. One of the best evidences of the fact is that we now boast an annual Eisteddfod—an institution which origin- ated with our more musically endowed king folk of Waled and has latterly made great headway in England and perhaps more particularly in the notthern counties. There is no doubt that these festivals do infinite good in promoting a love of the best music and encouraging a healthy rivalry amongst those who practise the art. The Warrington Eisteddfod held in the Parr Hall on New Year's Day was the fifth of its kind and we feel sure it would rank worthily with any festivals so recently established. There were competitors from all parts and although to the general public it was perhaps monotonous to hear repetitions of some pieces, still the enthusiast derived pleasure and profit from listening to the adjudicators' comments on the individual efforts. It was a day of triumphs for Warrington talent. In the choral and solo events the local competitors did credit to themselves and to the town and prac- tically all the chief prizes remain in Warrington. This was most gratifying and -should encouage Warrington aspirants to distinction in tke musical world to persevere in their studies. Significant indeed was the tribute of Mr. Tomlin, an eminent musician from America, who in voicing the pleasure which the day's competitions had afforded him said he wished there was the same spirit amongst the masses of the people across the water. Unquestionably, the more we realize the elevating and refining influence of music the better will it be for our national life.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bo= Law seems to MR. BiNAR LAW have a special pleasure in AND reviving the dry bones of ITNE-mrLoymExT. defunct Protectionist falls-</p>
        <p>cies. A few weeks ago he endeavoured to galvanise into vitality the withered corpse of Gorman unemployment statistics. If he had given as much study to the columns of the "Labour Gisette," the organ of the Labour Department of the Board of Trade, as to the German trades unionist returns, he might have saved himself a-great deal of unnecessary trouble. For while that publication gives the Garman figures in full, it accompanies them with a caution to the effect that owing to the dissimilarity of methods of enunajaration in the two countries, no comparison wharever can be made between the figures of unemployed in each country. Why,too, do our Tariff Reformers invariably forget in their excursions into statistics to take into account the important factor of the withdrawal from the German labour unions of do many young workers owing to the compulsory two years of military service P If Mr. Bonar Law could only read the observations of the recognised German authorities in labour matters, he would be better informed, and might even be induced to modify his crude opinions.</p>
        <p>The religious controversy RELIGION in Malta has not apparently AND LIBERTY. been settled by the action recently taken by his Majesty's Government in deciding that all persons in the island shall have liberty "to exercise their resrective modes of religious worship, provided they be contented with a quiet and peaceable enjoyment of the same, not giving offence or scandal to the Government." The publication of his Majesty's proclamation to that effect has called forth a protest from the Bishop of Malta and the Canons of the Cathe- dral. Their claim is that the right of exercising religious worship in public in the island has been for more than a century reserved to the Roman Catholics, who, of cours3, comprise the vast majority of the islanders. This privileged position should, they, pay,be maintained, and they refer to the 100 years' rule of " unconquered Albion " as having given sanction and support to this special consideration. Of course, Lord Elgin's reply to that is that the extension of liberty to all religious creeds is in no way incon- sistent with the protection which has been accorded to the Roman Catholics. Sir Mansfield Clarke, the Governor, too, points out that the principle of equal religious rights for the people of the island was laid down so far back as 1847 by the British Government of that day, so that the appeal of the Cathedral dignitaries to precedent is not supported by the facts. The Maltose do not appear to have learnt</p>
        <p>much from their peaceful life under the tiritish Government; but, in any case, they may make up their minds that the measure of justice which they term an "imposition " will be enforced. It is to be hoped that the gloomy prognostications of the Canons as to " unpleasait surprises in the future exercise of public worship " will not be justified in any acts of violence. The "principles of English lbierty," to which reference is made in the resolution passed at a public meeting, do not con- template the exclusion of one religion for the benefit of another in these days.</p>
        <p>COSTS, COSTUMES, FUR AND MILLINERY</p>
        <p>AT REDUCED PRICES. W. HODOKINSON, LIMITED,</p>
        <p>TADIRS' TAILORS AND COSIUSII3RS.</p>
        <p>BRIDGE! STRBRT, WARRINGTOY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Dr. McNaught is an ideal musical adjudicator and his decisions at the Warrington Eisteddfod, given in the must good-humoured style, appeared to please everyone. .</p>
        <p>o Trade must be good in Warridgton. We met a newsboy yesterday wearing gloves. The gloves were not really a pair, but they were gloves nevertheless, and afforded further proof of the luxury of the age. * * *</p>
        <p>The sad well accident at Grappenhall which occurred last Monday is another proof that all wells should be compelled by law to have special safeguards placed round or over them. Open wells have, from time immemorial, resulted in the loss of many precious</p>
        <p>* * * According to the " Rural World," an applicant for the post of mistress in a country school was asked : " What is your position with regard to the whipping of children ?" She replied : "My usual position is on a chair with the child held firmly across my knees, face downwards." * * * _ _ _</p>
        <p>The visit to Warrington on February Ist of Mr. Sydney Buxton, M.P., Postmaster-General, will be looked' forward to with interest. The fact that the meeting has been arranged by the Junior Liberal Association is an evidence of the usefulness and vitality of that organization.* The police are requested by the Finance Committee to enforce the regulations with regard to vehicular traffic and to compel all drivers to keep on the proper side of the road. Has there been an excess of wobbling this Christmastide we wonder? * * Though dogs are not to be obliged to wear collars this year, we are pleased to see that the police are to be clothed. The Chief Constable has advertised for tenders for the supply of police clothing—a con- cession in the interests of public decency and police propriety which we heartily commend. * * _</p>
        <p>Do you want a tree-guard " solid, substantial and thick ?" The Stockton Heath Tree Planting Corn-, mittee has 50 to dispose of. They were offered to the Warrington Town Hall and Parks Committee, and declined with thanks as being unsuitable in the borough. We can imagine Mr. Hough's sardonic smile. He has long had a hatred of the tree-guards.</p>
        <p>o Colonel Edelsten's taxed costs, which the Corpora- tion have paid in settlement of the action recently entered, amount to ,403 7s. sd. We suppose the Corporation's costs will not be any less sum. The squabble has not left much change out of £l,OOO, and it would probably have paid the Corporation to fight the matter out. 0 e= _ _ _</p>
        <p>One shaft of a factory belching out black or grey smoke will send forth more destruction over a very large area than 200 household chimneys will do. When the s,noke from factories is rigidly abated, according even to the present Act of Parliament, the smoke over England will diminish probably to one- half.—Sir W. B. Richmond in the "Times."</p>
        <p>* The opening of the New Year witnesses an addition to our local parochial magazines. The Rev. F. Vaughan, the energetic incumbent of Emmanuel Church, has issued to his congregation the first number of the "Emmanuel Church Messenger" which is an exceedingly interesting publication. We venture to predict for it an enthusiastic welcome. In the window of a busy eating-house not far fr3m the centre of London may be seen this alarming notice :</p>
        <p>Steak Puddings, 4d. and 6d. Our Own Moke. A remarkable instance of commercial candour ! a a</p>
        <p>A really useful " tip "for householders is given in "Good Housekeeping "this month. " B.G.' declares that one's gas bill can be perceptibly reduced by lighting the gas carefully. One should hold the lighted match to the burner and then turn the gas full on at once, and it lights with an explosion. This explosion affects the meter and sends it forward rapidly. '* * * No less than £4,000 was expended from the Man- chester rates in the effort to get rid of the snow. As a species of out-relief such a huge sum must have made a great difference to the condition of the sub- merged tenth at Christmas time. We wonder how much Warrington spent, and how many extra men were employed. Perhaps one of our labour members will inquire at Tuesday's Council meeting. Is this an example of the co-operation between Committees which is supposed to exist under the Warrington Corporation : From the Water Com- mittee's minutes—" The Fire Engine Committee having requested to be supplied with a list of hydrants, —resolved that the Committee be informed that they were supplied with such list in July 1905." It looks more like the snub courteous than co-operation. A genuine " sharp-shooter " had a glorious time at Boltc.m on Tuesday. John Flanaagan, a Manchester hawker, fired 174 shots in a Bolton shooting gallery, and would probably have been shooting yet had not the proprietor suggested a settlement. The little bill amounted to 14s. Cd, and John found he had no money. His explanation at the police court was that he was handed a rifle by the attendant; no words Were spoken, and as he fell in love with the pastime he would have kept on firing as long as they handed him a loaded rifle. He was committed for seven days. For a " Bowton " man the proprietor of the shooting gallery must have been somewhat green to stand by and see 174 shots fired for nothing, unless he took his enthusiastic customer for Lord Roberts or the Commander-in-Chief.</p>
        <p>* * A few years ago Warrington in musical circles occupied a very lowly position—to-day it stands on the dizzy heights of fame and it is possible if we go on improving in this respect at the rate we have done of late that a world wide reputation will be reached and established. At the Eisteddfod, which was a great success, Mr. Tomlin who was the official choral conductor of the Columbian Exhibition at Chicago, stated he envied Warrington of its musical talent and sincerely wished that the same love" and spirit for music was only established in the towns of the United States of America as it appeared to be in Warrington. Fancy Warrington giving points to America !</p>
        <p>* * * They have a happy way of dealing with a snow-fall in Brussels which should appoal to Alderman Bennett if it does not commend itself to the municipal authori- ties. After the recent heavy fall the snow was swept into mounds in the Brussels parks, and then watered to make it firm. The artists and sculptors next arrived, and worked hard to achieve the effects which have since delighted the Belgian world and his wife: At the chief entrance to the park M Maria succeeded in producing a beautiful group representing " Charity." M. van Hamm° created a formidable automobile, with M. de Mot (Brussels' Mayor) driving it, calling attention to the erstwhile famous" five kilometres the hour " decree, and, at the same time, calmly crushing the effigy of a man under its wheela. M. Samuel made a handsome medallion of the members of the Belgian Royal Family. The great Belgian violinist, Eugene Ysaye, was cleverly executed in snow by a fervent admirer. M. Moser@ s work was a fine litter of pigs ; and animals of all descriptions seemed very much in favour with the pupils of the Academy of Sculpture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>"Written in Scarlet "(serial). Association Football. 3. Women's Chat and Fashions. "The Queen's Secretary:" (Short story.) 6.* Drowning Fatality at Grappenhall District News. Children's Coiner. 7. Rugby Football. Science Notes. Railway Time Table. WOOD CHIMNEY PIECES, Fire' Grates and Tiles. unrivalled selection, inspection invited.—J. S. GARLAND, Builders' Ironmonger, &amp;c., 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. 'Coming Events. On the 13th inst. Warrington will be favoured with a visit from the famous Wingate Temperance Band, when two sacred concerts will be given in the Parr Hall. The thousand guinea challenge trophy which the band won when they secured the Crystal Palace Championship will be on view. Other first-class artistes have been engaged to .appear. The proceeds of the silver collections which will be taken will be in aid of the Runcorn Life-Saving and Grappling Corps. Our readers will be pleased to learn that the Warrington Dramatic Society will present the highly successful comedy " Dr. Wake's Patient "in the Parr Hall on Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday and Saturday, January 22nd to 26th inclusive. The cast is an exceptionally strong one and the performances promise to be most successful. The Society has already given over £2,000 to local charities and on this occasion the proceeds will be devoted to thc Warrington Infirmary Extension Fund. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STKEET.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Page. 9</p>
        <p>Personal. At Chester, to-day (Saturday), Mr. Arthur Dillon, son of the late Hon. Conrad Dillon, and cousin of Viscount Dillon, will marry Mrs. Charles Broadbent, widow of the late Mr. Charles Broadbent, of Upton Heyes, Chester, and daughter of the Right Hon. Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P. We regret that there is no further improvement in the condition of Mr. Luke Talbot, Chief Constable, who lies ill at his residence, Winmarleigh-street. Nursing Association. Mr. Hope, the Hon. Treasurer of the Nursing Association, Workingmen's Committee, begs to acknowledge with thanks the receipt of £26 73. 31, the nett proceeds of the concert held in Parr Hall on the 26th November, from the Concert Committee per Mr. C. H. Hoult.</p>
        <p>The Union Bank of Manchester Limited. The Directors of the Union Bank of Manchester Limited have declAred the usuAl dividend of twelve shillings per share for the half-year, and after placing £2,000 to Premises Reduction Account, £5,000 to Reserve Fund (as against £3,000 for the previous half- year,making the Reserve Fund 032,009),£2,500 to the Pension Fund,carry forward £12,063,as against £5,945 last half-year. - -</p>
        <p>TABLE-, FLOOR, AND HANGING LAMPS in large variety. Lstest patterns.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger Bridge at., Warrington.—Stivt. ' Re-Union of Pupil Teachers. The annual re-union of pupil teachers who attended die Bank-street centre took place at the Winmarleigh Cafe the other evening, wheg, a most enjoyable social was held. The venture was successful in every way, and it is hoped that in succeeding years still greater successes will be chonicled. Mr. P. W Barnes ably -sang the solo" Pray, brethren, pray." Mr. Picton, as usual, presided at the organ, and gave a solo- in excellent style. Mr. C. Cotton and Mr. J. 0 wen also took part in the meeting. Two new members were heartily welcomed.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK TOE BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.--Advt. Popular. Penny Concerts. Almost the whole of the programme at the popular ,concert, in the Bold-street Schools on Saturday, was sustained by Mr. T. Brongh's Juvenile Choir, who -rendered several choruses in a praiseworthy manner, 'fully maintaining their reputation as the leading .children's choir in the district. Songs were given by Misses Jessie Woodland and Ethel Dunn (members of the choir) in excellent style, while Miss Maddock, of Frodsham, was warmly applauded- for several recita- tions. The humorou; element was in the hands of Mr. Dan Johnson,, of Widnes. " HERALD " KITCHEN RANGES and " Peveril " Fire Grates are hard to bet.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at.., Warrington.—Advt• Ragged School. On Monday evening the 20th annual teachers' tea party took place in the schoolroom and was well attended. After the tea, games, &amp;c., were indulged in and a most enjoyable evening was brought to a close by the members taking part in a Watchnight Service. After the tea the children were treated to an excellent concert by Messrs. Seacombe, Johnson, Lloyd, Marsh, J. Johnson, Odell, and the Bros. Wright. Mrs. Blake- more Was the organist. The committee beg to thank -the numerous and generous friends who have given_ food, oranges, &amp;c., which enabled them to cater for 1,300 women and children on Boxing Day and New Year's night. Hearty cheers were given for Messrs. Skelland, Owen and the workers and dimors.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS. AdVti— The Education Bill. -</p>
        <p>The following choice bit of parochial platitude is culled from the current issue of the " Warrington Parish Church Magazine" :—" We have indeed reason to thank those members of the Lords and Commons, notably the Archbishop of Canterbury, Lord Lansdowne and Mr. Balfour, who have so nobly and bravely in and out of Parliament opposed and defeated this iniquitous measure. The Government is angry, and no wonder, but the hollowness of the protest that the will of the people has been thwarted is manifest to the simplest. They dare not appeal to the country for they knew that the Lords have the country at their back and that the country admires and applauds the Lords for their wisdom and courage. Nothing we are persuaded could have done more to establish the House of Lords in the estima- tion and affections of the people than their gallant action in this attempt to oust religion from the schools of the land."</p>
        <p>STRANGE'S A.l. Crystal LAMP OIL in barrels or drums delivered free.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Parochial Tea at Padgate. Success attended the annual parochial tea and entertainment in connection with Christ Church, Padgate, which took place on Thursday evening. The programme submitted at the concert was an unusually attractive one, including a cinematograph exhibition, quartettes and songs by a party from the Warrington Male Voice Choir, conjuring by Mr. Victor Roberts, and an amusing dialogue. There was a large attend- ance and the efforts of the entertainers were highly appreciated. The proceeds will be divided between the Church House and the schools.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER TRH CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SHERB.—Adyt.</p>
        <p>Poor Children's Treat. Through the continued generosity of a number of townspeople, the teachers and friends of the Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission were enabled to provide tea on New Year's Day to about 250 children from the poorest homes in the district. Had all the subscribers been able to see the intense delight of t e little ones, they would have felt amply rewarded for thsir kind- ness. After tea a short programme was given in which the following persons took part :—Misses Lucy and Forrie Pearson, Miss Alice Griffiths, Messrs. W. H. Wallington, Joseph Phcenix, and Henry Roberts. The various items were keenly appreciated and the pro- ceedings were brought to a close by the children giving three ringing cheers for the donors and workers. As the children left the room each one was presented with a cob of bread, an orange and a packet of sweets Sad Story of Distress. A painful case of distress was investigated by Coroner Brighouse at the Warrington Police Station on Wednesday, Brighouse, an inquest was held on the body of John Frederick Thompson, aged four months, son of Joseph Thompson, of Back Atherton-street, who was found dead in bed on -Sunday. It was stated that there were five persons sleeping in one bed on Saturday night,--Inclu&amp;ng the deceased child. The deceased slept on the outside, and on the mother awakening next morning found him dead. —Sergeant Barlow, in answer to the coroner said the husband was willing to work, but could not get any. The family were in a distressful plight, and for dinner there had been only one kipper between the whole family of them.—The Coroner said he hoped all employers who were yearning for labour would take notice of the ease, and also that politicians who were always reminding us that England was never so prosperous as at present, would take note. As practical proof of his sympathy with the family the Coroner gave half a sovereign towards their relief. A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
