      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EMIT NEW MAGISTRATES. SEVEN LIBERALS AND ONE LABOUR MAN APPOINTED.</head>
        <p>EMIT NEW MAGISTRATES. SEVEN LIBERALS AND ONE LABOUR MAN APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>For some time it has been known that an, addition to the borough magisterial bench was contemplated and speculation has been rife ,as to the names of gentlemen to be so honoured. testerday the authori- tative announcement was made that the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster had added the following gentlemen to the commission of the peace for the borough : Councillor Thomas Barton, 82, Liverpool-road, iron manufacturer. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor Luke Ellison, fin Knutsford-road; boot and shoe manufacturer. Councillor James Evans, 14 and 16, Elizabeth street, baker and provision dealer. Mr. William Furness, Fern Lea, Great Sankey, commission agent. Mr. Thomas Locker, 14, Froghall-lane, manufacturer of woven wire cloth. Mr. John Magian, Raddon Court, Latchford, furniture manufacturer and dealer. Mr. Karl Emile Markel, Tower House, Bewsey, doctor of philosophy and chemical manufacturer. Councillor Fred Stott, 160, Battersby-lane, mechanic. All the new magistrates, with one exception (Councillor Stott) are Liberals. The last addition to the borough bench was made in 1904 when the unprecedentedly larger, number of twelve new names was placed upon the Commission. Councillor Burton is one of the most respected and hardworking members of the Town Council, having been continuously in office as a representative of St. Austin's Ward since 1890. He is deputy-chairman of the. Health Committee and chairman of the Fire Engine Committee and renders valuable services in each capacity. He is a staunch Liberal and has taken an active part in election campaigns. For many years he has held the important position of manager of the works of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., having been associated with the concern from its formation. A work which he has particularly at heart is that of the. Workingmen's Infirmary Committee of which he is vice-chairman. He is also a prominent worker on the Old Folk's Treat Committee. Councillor Luke Ellison is also an old servant of the public, have represented Latchford Ward in the Council continuously for eleven years. He takes a particular interest in the work of the Education Committee,holding the office of Chairman of the School Attendance Sub-Committee. He is a prominent Good Templar and a strong supporter of the temperance cause. He is one of the best known and oldest inhabitants of Latchford.</p>
        <p>Councillor James Evans is one of Warrington's most devoted public workers, having been a member of the Council for Whitecross Ward for nine years. A well deserved honour was recently conferred upon him by his election as chairman of the Health Committee and he also bolds the offices of chairman of the Markets Committee and vice-chairman of the Water Committee. He is a particularly active supporter of Liberalism and presides over the deliberations of the Executive Committee of the Warrington Liberal Association. He is prominently identified with the Independent Metho- dist denomination, and has done excellent work as a local preacher. His interests are, of course, more particularly centred in the Whitecross Ward, and his constituents will be pleased to hear of his elevation to the magistracy. Mr. William Furness has also played an active part in public life, and for some years represented St. John's Ward in 'the Council. He is at the present time a useful member of the Great Sankey Parish Council. His association with Liberalism has been long and useful, and he now discharges the duties of bon. treasurer to the Liberal Association. Mr. Thomas Locker is one of our best known towns- men and he, too, is a Liberal stalwart. He has a great reputation as a sportsman, having interested _himself in various branches of athletics. He is an accom- plished swimmer and was the first holder of the iLicensed Victuallers' Challenge Tqphy for the 150 yards championship of Warrington which he secured in 1897. He has figured in polo matches and altogether is one of our most enthusiastic practisers of the natatory art. He has long b,een connected with the Warrington Rugby Football Club and is at the present time a valued member of the committee. His Interest in gymnastics is shown.by the fact that he is a co-opted mgmber of the Gymnasium Committee. Mr. Man Magian is an enterprising and successful business man, having worked his way from a very I bumble position to the head of a large concern. In addition t his own fleuriThing business, Mr.Maginn is I prominently connected with the Alliance Box Corn- . pany—a new concern which has found considerable i employment. He is one .of the most independent of • men, and during the three years that he represented , St. John's Ward in the Council he worked splendidly . for the interests of the town, and of his own Ward in particular. He is a strong Liberal, and during the , last election spoke at many meetings. One of the most popular appointments will be that , of Dr. K. E. Markel whose active partin Warrington's I industrial life is well known to all our readers. As a 'managing-director of Messrs. Jovph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, he has by his skill and energy done much to promote the prosperity of the firm. He has taken the daepest interest in the recreative schemes organized for the benefit of the work people among •whom he is highly esteemed. He is a distinguished chemist and can be accounted one of Warrington's . busiest men. As a great lover of music he takes every opportunity of advancing the art and he is , president of the Warrington Eisteddfod Cammittee. i Councillor Fred Bt2tt is chiefly known as a doughty champion of Lablur, being one of the foremost supporters of trade unionism. He is also a very active member of the local branch of the Independent Labour Party. A great 'deal of his time has been devoted to furthering the aims of the Trades and Labour Council and until recently he acted as Secretary. He was returned t 3 the Council by the burgesses of Orford Ward in 1903 and has been untiring in his work on the various committees. He is vice-chairman of the Distress Committee.</p>
        <p>The new magistrates were yesterday afternoon added to the Commission of the Peace by the County Seal-keeper at Preston. They will be sworn in at the Borough Police Court on Monday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. _ [By " Ovalist."] 'Warrington have made some progress iii tlie League since our last issue, full points being secured at the expense of Bradford on Saturday and Runcorn on- New Year's Day. The victory at Bradford was a fine one and being the first of the season on Yorkshire soil it was particularly welcome. 'Pis true the Park Avenue men were not at full strength, but if they had been, Warrington should have won. They displayed some of their true form and were much too smart for the home lot. Scoring first acted as a fine tonic, and afterwards there was little doubt about the result.</p>
        <p>The great game was that on Tuesday (New Year's Day) when Runcorn paid their annual visit to War- rington. Notwithstanding the vile weather, there was a fine crowd of quite 7,000, several hundred of whom were Runcorn enthusiasts. Of course, Warrington won. They have won every It ome match this season and I hope to see them maintain the record to the end of the fixture list. Runcorn were at full strength and they made a great effort to spoil Warrington's sequence of victories, but had finally to bow the knee with the score 14-9. It was a splendid struggle from start to finish and though Warrington led by 9 points to 2 at half-time they were overhauled by . the plucky " Linnebs "and only secured the verdict in the last five minutes. Considering the htkavy ground the play was brilliantly fast and open, and the crowd got quite a football treat.</p>
        <p>It wonld be difficult to say that the better team won, so well did the losers play at times. But the honours certainly go to Warrington for another reason. They were handicapped by injuries to crack players, they put a weak left wing in the field, and yet they overcame the full strength of Runcorn in decisive fashion. For the Runcorn line to be crossed four times in a match is a novelty this season and had only happened once before SAturday, Rroughton Rangers having put up 16 points to 3 against the Linnets at Broughton on December 5. Runcorn did not go under without a big effort to stem the tide, but they found the home defence very watchful and had much to thank Jolley for. Had the great half-back not placed three goals out of the mud it is safe to say Runcorn would have been more easily defeated. These successes where try-getting failed acted as a fine stimulus and nearly brought about Warrington's downfall. _ _</p>
        <p>At half-time it was all Lombard-street to a China orange on Warrington for they had three tries against a goal, but Jolley's goal-kicking and a smart try to Flannagan reversed the odds. At threequqrter time the visitors looked like winners for they had quite out-played the home men for nearly 30 minutes of the last 40, and were on level terms. The Wirepullers must have had a bit up their sleeves, however, for they swamped the Runcornians in the closing stages and made the match safe with another five points.</p>
        <p>There was one noticeable difference in the play of the teams. Warrington forsook the passing game and went in for footwork, whereas the Linnets tried hard to handle under adverse circumstances and more often than not failed to make much headway. That Warrington /scored four tries to Run- corn's one is sufficient proof that their tactics were the better in the mud. They had an advantage in weight and that told in the loose rashes, though the active scrummagers in green quite held their c4h in the packs. Runcorn were decidedly superior in field- ing and kicking the wet ball,—but they were always artistes in this line. Berry and Dickinson were the only two on the home side who could be relied upon\ as catchers, and they many a time saved the situation. , Tilley was a failure, but be has some excuse. Harrison played a superb full back game for Runcorn, and was ably supported by Jolley add Butterworth. The wings were both very speedy, and it was evident that the Linnets have a well-balanced active combination this season.</p>
        <p>The hero of the day was undoubtedly Dickensan,who scored the last three of. Warrington's ties and was ever in the thick of the fray. Just now he is playing better football than over, and with increased scoring powers also he has become the star of the three- quarter line. Sammy Lees also played a perfect game and I think on the whole he was better than Jolley whose best work was done in punting. Berry showed that his recent improvement was real by another. fine display. A word of praise is also due to Cartwright, who assisted the "A ' team to win at Runcorn in the morning and turned out for the seniors in the after- noon. He had the pleasure of scoring in both matches. The Runcorn match had an important bearing on</p>
        <p>, the South-West Lancashire League, and gave War- rington a useful lead. They have yet to play St. !Helens, home, and Widnes and Runcorn away. Runcorn have Leigh, St. Helens, Warrington, and , Wigan at home, so the probabilities are that the return match at Runcorn on March 23rd will settle the destination of the. Cup. Presuming that each team , wins its other engagements, Warrington would have to get one point at Runcorn to secure the trophy.</p>
        <p>The Sauth-west Lancashire Lague up-to-date is I as follows: ! Pts. P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. W'gton.. 7 ... 5 ... 1 ... 1 ... 101 62 ... 11 Runcorn 6 ... 4 ... 2 ... 0 ... 82 ... 52 ... 8 Wigan 6 ... 4 ... 2 ... 0 ... 95... 54... 8 .Leigh ... 7 ... 2 ... 4 ... 1 ... 88 ... 79 ... 5 ; Widnes.. 7 ... 2 ... 5 ... 0 ... 55 ... 139 ... 4 S.Helens 5 ... 1 ... 4 ... 0 ... 43... 78 ... 2 -- 1 The League Table up-to-date is as follows : I For Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. O. T. P. G. T. P.Centage. Halifax lB 15 2 1 68 65 331 20 27 121 86.40 Oldham . 21 16 4 1 39 59 328 27 32 150 7857 i Leeds lB 14 4 0 55 68 314 21 36 150 77.77 , Runcorn l7 13 4 0 49 83 353 20 23 109 7647 Broughton l6 11 4 1 47 64 286 19 22 104 7P87 Warrington l9 13 5 1 57 81 357 29 38 172 71'05 Wigan 2O 14 6 0 57 86 372 31 36 167 70.00 Hui:islet 2l 14 7 0 57 83 363 48 54 258 6666 Keighley l6 10 5 1 43 56 254 26 35 157 65.62 Dewsbury l9 10 8 1 48 65 291 37 56 239 5526 Batley 16 8 7 1 34 38 182 34 43 197 5312 Barrow 18 9 8 1 31 48 206 36 50 222 5217 Salford l9 910 0 37 40 194 43 50 236 4736 • Widnes l2 5 6 1 24 20 108 24 39 165 4513 I Leigh lB 8 9 1 32 61 247 33 39 183 47.22 Pontefract 9 4 5 0 13 16 74 23 37 157 4444 HuILK.R . 18 711 1 25 44 182 34 42 12.4 4P66 , Hull lB 711 1 28 49 194 51 66 300 4166 Swinton 2O 812 0 41 54 244 44 48 232 40.00 Rochdale l7 611 0 24 45 183 26 54 214 3529 Wak'fild T l6 510 1 24 39 166 36 70 232 3437 Hud'sfield lB 612 0 31 57 233 55 70.320 3313 York l5 510 0 23 38 160 37 54 236 3313 4Bradford -18 512 1 41 55 247 41 51 235 3055 St. Helens 17 512 0 36 46 210 38 58 260 '29.41 Braraley l6 114 4 715 59 44 80 328 9'37 Liverpl.d 20 019 1 10 16 68 104 182 754 2'20</p>
        <p>Halifax strengthened their position on Wednesday by winning at Swinton 13-2. while Wigan accounted for Liverpool City, 37-3. Before the end of next week Warrington may just struggle into one of the first four places as St. Helens and. Liverpool City have both to be met in the meantime. Warrington should also be the first team to turn the 400 points mark.</p>
        <p>The gate-motley at Warrington on New Year's day was only a shilling or two short of £l5O. A. collection was made for the widow and family of the late Harry Myers and this realised £4 13s. 10-id. I am asked to remind my readers that a second collection for the same object will be made to-day.</p>
        <p>Warrington are at home again to-day, St. Helens being the visitors and an easy victory should be pretty certain, especially as the points are made doubly valuable by South-West Lancashire Cup considerations. Warrington won at St. Helens early in the season by 19 points to 10 Flints. The team selected for the match is as follows : —Berry, full back; Tilley, Isherwood, Dickenson and Brooks, three-quarters ; Lees and Jenkins, half-backs ; Preston, Boafdman, Heath, Harmer, Shugars and Thomas, forwards.</p>
        <p>The "A " team to play St. Helens. "A " in a friendly fixture will be: Knight; Landor, Jones, Cartwright and Newall ; Kay and Hoekenhall ; Jolley, Forster, Hewitt, Belton, Etolbrook and Bradshaw.</p>
        <p>The principal matches down for decision to-da.37 are: Leeds v. ,Oldham Wakefield v. Hunslet Bradford v. Wigan Rochdale v. Batley Warrington v. St. Helens Hull T. Broughton Rangers Huddersfield v. Hull Kingston Rovers Dewsbury v. Halifax Salford v. Swinton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>NEW YEAR.</head>
        <p>THE NEW YEAR.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S WELCOME. ,</p>
        <p>As in the past Warrington gave a hearty- if not somewhat noisy welcome to the New Year. Shortly before midnight on Monday the melancholy peajing of church bells announced that 1906 waii fast draw- ing to a close and weary watchers learnt that their vigil was almost ended. Then the sounds ceased but in a moment or two the silence was broken again by the bells which now rang out a most joyous peal. Nor were they alone as disturbers of the witching hour as the shrill shrieks of locomotives, the sonorous note sounded by vessels on the Ship Canal, and the dis- cordant hoots of works" buzzers" were raised in welcome of 1907. On account of the state of the roads there were not many people abroad, but those who' braved the elements exchanged hearty and seasonable greetings as they wended their way home to let in the New Year. Tuesday was observed as a holiday, most of the works in town being closed, but the holiday spirit was somewhat damped by the wretched weather. The attraction locally was the football match between the WarriVon and Runcorn and, despite a cheerless drizzle, which later turned to a heavy downpour a huge crowd assembled on the Wilderspool ground to witness the discoinforture of the "Linnets."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WATCH-NIGHT SERVICES.</head>
        <p>WATCH-NIGHT SERVICES.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CEIURCII.</p>
        <p>A large congregation assembled at St. Paul's Church on Monday night, when a watch-night service was con- ducted by the Vicar. The last few minutes of the old year were spent in silent prayer. Then the Vicar wished all present a very happy and prosperous New Year." At the conclusion of the service the "To Dem "was sung very heartily by the choir and congregation. The offertory was on behalf of the Infirmary and the District Nursing Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There was a good congregation at the watch night service at Wycliffe Church at which the proceedings were of an impressive character. During the course of a brief address the Rev. John Yonge drew a powerful lesson on the uncertainty of life from the recent disasters on land and sea. It behoved them to accept the teaching of those events. •It was right for them to look for as long life as God might think well to give but it was also right to make r uch true uae of the days and years that when they came to the end of the course it would not prove to them untimely. To whom did they look as the great model and pattern in life ? Was it the prosperous man of the world or were they making Jesus Christ their pattern and example? It was to Him that they should be honestly and earnestly looking all through the days and the years as the only Saviour who could take away the sins of the past and keep them from -Ithe sins of the present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURQH.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURQH.</p>
        <p>The watchnight service at this church was well *tended and was conducted by the Rev. S. Pitt, the superintendent of the circuit. There was a moderate congregation. Appropriate hymns were 'sung, and the choir gave an excellent rendering of " Load, kindly light," Nit% Pearce presiding at the organ. The Rev. S. PITT said it was their duty to offer thanksgiving for the mercies which had been vouchsafed to them during the past year. Life was never so rich as it was to-day in the sweetness of their home-life, in regard to literature, their church life, and in their meetings together Sunday after Sunday ministering one to another in holy things. This was a time of humilia- tion. Oh the drunkenness, the self-indulgence, the setting out of temptation before the unwary, the greed, the fraud,the oppression, and the bitterness that existed in this land ? Should they not humble themselves before God? Instead of the various sections of the Church drawing together there were disputes that seemed to separate them more bitterly than before, even those who desired to live in love and charity with their neighbours. It was a pity ecclesiastical and religious contention should come to the pitch it had come. The last moment of the old year and the first of the new year were spent in silent prayer, after which the hymn," Come, let us anew," was sung, and the preacher wished the congregation" A Happy New Year."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The watch-night service at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church was well attended. ,</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Manning during his address said the passing of another year cal,led for earnest thought and serious prayer. There was no real difference between that and any other hour. It was perhaps only senti- ment which led them to invest that hour with special solemnity. But sentiment counted for much in life. They did well to set an hour apart for self-examination and prayer. They were passinr, another milestone in life's journey,reminding them that their opportunities for doing life's work would soon be gone. They could not pass such a time mark without serious thought upon the purpose and issues of life. This was a time for retrospect. That night they thought of the mistakes the failures, and the faults of the year, and they acknow- ledged them not merely to gratify morbid sentment but as ft means of strengthening their resolve to do better in the -future. The closing year suggested that life was a working day, an allottedtime in which to get their appointed tasks done. There were two ideals of life : First, to get out of life all they could of pleasure and profit for themselves, and send, to put into life all they could of service for God and for man. The waning year reminded them that life's- opportuni- ties were limited. The working day would come to an end, they knew not when, and their unfinished work would remain for ever undone. If they failed it would not be for want of opportunity. Life's working day was always long enough for the doing of life's appointed task. There was a task for every hour. If they did their best from first to last they would only finish their task. Therefore there was no time to be wasted. The I waste of the past could not be recalled. That night there came to them a call to earnest living. The lonest day was but short. They knew not when the night would come. To the man who had done his work the night had no terrors. The night brought to him rest and reward. During the year God bad called to himself many prominent workers, whose examples of earnest faithful service they would seek for strength to follow. The service closed a few minutes after midnight with the singing of the customary hymn, "Come let us anew."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>POOR CHILDREN ENTERTAINED.</head>
        <p>POOR CHILDREN ENTERTAINED.</p>
        <p>The members of the Wycliffe Monday afternoon women's meeting (of which Mts. Armitage is the president) on New Years's Day gave a treat to poor children. About 500 of whom at down to an excellent tea provided in the Wycliffe Hall. After tea the chil- dren were entertained with songs, recitations, and a dialogue by the junior scholars of the Sunday school. Mr. Smith, Fennel-street, gave several selections on the gramaphone. When leaving, each child was pre- sented with an orange and an apple, and all seemed to thoroughly enjoy themselves. The committee comprised Mesdames Moore, Womersley, Colley, Bowers, Dutton, Molyneux and Jones. WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL TEA PARTY. The adult members of the Sunday School connected with Wycliffe Congregational Church, held their annual tea party on Thursday evening. An excellent tea was provided, to which about 200 sat 'downy after which the evening was enjoyably spent.. The pastor, the Rev. J. Yonge, presided and was supported by Mr. W. Brown superintendent of the Sunday School, Mr. W. Beswick, deputy-superintendent, and Mr. George Wallington. An admirable musical programme was submitted. Mr. H. Colley opened with a well played pianoforte solo whilst a violin solo was given with good effect by Mr. L. Poole. Miss Nelly Armstrong delighted the audience with her vocal efforts and Miss Alice Taylor recited with considerable dramatic effect. Miss Taylor's class ably presented a dialogue, whilst Miss Maybury's class won high praise for the action song' Little housemaids." equal success being achieved in the song and dance "A night in a dormitory." Messrs. H. Hill and G. Ford created much amusement with a humorous sketch. Perhaps the main feature of the entertainment, was, however, a conjuring exhibition by Professor Brad- ford, who displayed great dexterity and sleight of hand which won for him hearty applause. During an interval, the Rev. J. YONG'S, with a few approprirte remarks, on behalf of the officers, teachers and scholars, presented a silver-mounted walking stick to Mr. E. Pickavance upon his retirement from the office of secretary after twelve years' service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>"ROYAL GINGER"</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>THE NEW TEA TABLE DELICACY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>POLICE ASSAULT. At Widnes, on Monday, ThoMPS Owen, of Harrison- street, Ditton' was fined 40s. and costs for assaulting the police on Saturday night. REFUSING TO QUIT. At Widnes, on Monday, William Tarleton, who was charged with refusing to quit the licensed premises of the Railway and Commercial Hotel on the 22nd ult., and with assaulting Police-constable Worden, was sent to goal for six months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SUDDEN DEXTEI.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEXTEI.</p>
        <p>Henry Hulme. of Hall-road, Ditton, was fopnd dead at half-past five on Monday morning sitting in a wheel- barrow in St. Michael's-road, Ditton. Hulme was employed at Deacon's works, and it is supposed that at the time of his death he was on his way to work. £1 REWARD FOR MISSING MAN. Tao Upper Mersey Watermen and Porters' Associa- tion, Union Buildings, 87, High-street, Runcorn, offer .C 1 reward to anyone recovering the body of Ernest Mann, mate of flat "Concord," Messrs. F. J. Abbott and Co., who Ns as lost overboard on Saturday, December 29t1s, 1906, opposite Hale Head, in the river Mersey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>"NOT EIGHT O'CLOCK YET."</head>
        <p>"NOT EIGHT O'CLOCK YET."</p>
        <p>-- At 7.40 o'clock on Saturday avening George Parker, a clogger in the employ of Mr. Hedgcock, of Ann-street, Widnes, went into the living premises attached to the workshop, and glanced at the clock. He remarked, It's not eight o'clock yet."—About eight Parker was found lying in a pool of blood in the workshop with his throat cut fearfully. Before the arrival of Dr. Edwards life was extinct. •</p>
        <p>DROWNING FATALITY NEAR RUNCORN. garly on Suo4.ay morning the mate of the paddle boat St. Winifred reported to Mr. William Byfleld, captain of the Runcorn Grappling Corps, that the boat was towing some flats to Liverpool on Saturday, and about midday when they were near to Hale Head an alarm was raised that a man named Mann had fallen into the river. The flats were cast loose and the paddle boat was put back, but was unable to pick up the man. His cap was recoverea. Mr. Bylield made a search of the banks on 'Sunday but was unabla to and the body. GOLDEN WEDDING CELEBRATIONS.</p>
        <p>On Monday there was an interesting ceremony at Widnes Foundry, when a presentation was made to Mr. Bsnjamin Brown by the works staff, to commemorate the 50th anniversary of his wedding. The gift con- sisted of a gilt travelling clock and case, and the clock bore the folio ming inscription :—" Preaented to Mr. and Mrs. B. Brown by the staff of the Widnes Foundry Co. Ltd., on the occasion of their golden wedding. January Ist, 1937." Mr. Henry Johnson made the pre- sentation, and wished Mr. Brown much happiness and good health for the remainder of his days, which they all hoped would be many. Mr Brown feelingly replied.</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A GLASS TOWEL. James Smith, last from Widnes was charged at Warrington on Monday with being drunk in Horse- market-street on Thursday, and also with stealing a glass cloth from the Theatre Vaults in Scotland-road, the property of Niessrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Ltd.— Superintendent Monaghan said that at ten o'clock on Thursday morning prisoner went into the vaults. The towel had been put on a copper kettle to dry. When he went in the cloth was safe' but directly he had gone it was missing. About two_ hours afterwards, -he was locked up for being drunk and the cloth was found in his possession. Prisoner was a man who would not work, and went about begging.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>WIDNES MAN KILLED AT ST. HELENS. William Barlow, aged 51, of 123, Moorfield-road, Widnes, was found dead on a railway siding leading from the Clock Face Colliery to the Bold-road on Monday evening. It is stated by the police that Walter Hough, fitter, on going home from work found Barlow lying between the rails of the siding with a large wound on the back of the head and,his arm broken. He was dead. Barlow was employed as a labourer at the colliery brickworks. He was very deaf, and had been warned not to go down the siding, but it was his shortest way home. About 5.15 u train of waggona was shunted on to the siding by an engine in charge of Arthur Chesworth and Brakesman Holland. From the position of the body it is surmised that Barlow had been struck down from behind, having failed to hear the waggons coming. The driver and brakesman did not see or hear anything of him.</p>
        <p>SUICIDE AT WIDNES. At Widnes, on Wednesday afternoon, sfr. S. Brighouse held an inquiry at the Accident Hospital into the sudden death of George Parker, aged 30, residing at Forster- street, who was found dead on the premises of a person named Hedgecock, of Ann-street, on Saturday evening, with a terrible gash in his throat.—Deceased's brother, James Parker, gave evidence of identification, and said that a relative had acted in a similar manner some time ago.—Miss Hedgecock said that when Parker Came into the house at twenty minutes to eight he made the remark, when looking at the clock, It is not eight o'clock yet." He was always a very jolly kind of man. —Charles Naylor, with whom the deceased had lodged and worked deposed to finding him behind the door. He was a sober and industrious man, and always in the best of stiirits.—The jury returned a verdict of "Suicide whilst temporarily insane." FRODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>William Ebor Thornton, Frodsham, -was summoned on Friday for having neglected to have his child vaccinated. —James Pearson' vaccination officer, proved the case.— Defendant said he had a conscientious objection to vaccination, but did not obtain a certificate before, because his wife was opposed to it. She now accepted his view. The child was delicate and not fit for vaccina- tion.—The case was adjourned to enable defendant to bring a medical certificate.—William Edward Davies, Frodsham, was summoned for a similar offence. He did not appear.—Mr. Pearson said defendant had a con- scientious objection to vaccination, and his excuse was that he had not applied for an exemption certificate owing to pressure of business.—A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed.—David Whitby and John Percival were summoned for having trespassed in pursuit of game on land in the occupation of William Moss and Charles Houghton, at Newton, on December 15th.—Evidence was given to the effect that a shot was heard and defendants were seen on the land.—Mr. Harrison,. who has the shooting rights over the land, said he saw evidence that a partridge had been shot and carried by a dog.—Whitby swore that he was in the roadway when he heard a shot, and his dog went on the land.—Defend- ants were fined 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>A TERROR TO THE PUBLICANS AND POLWE At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, befßre Alderman Wareing (presiding), and Alderman Clainn, William Tarleton was charged with having been drunk on the licensed premises of the Railway and Commercial Hotel. He was further charged with having assaulted P.C. Worden whilst in the execution of his duty,— Prisoner who was remanded a week ago, now pleaded "guilty "to the first charge and "not guilty" to the second.—Superintendent Strickland said that risoner went to the Railway and Commercial Hotel, kept by Mr. John Winfield, on SatUrchY Merton -k between three o'clock and half-past. He asked For drink, but as he was very drunk the barmaid refused to serve him. He commenced using language of a filthy nature to the barmaid. He was ordered out, but refused to go. He picked up a pint of beer belonging to another customer and drank it. Mr. Winfield complained at the Police Station, and Constables Worden and Huntingdon were sent to ask the man to go off the premises. He refused to go out when they asked him and they put him out. He then became very violent, and, on the way to the Police Station,he threw himself down and kicked violently about him. He kicked Constable Word3n on the instep of the foot, breaking a bone. The constable was still confined to the house, and could not attend the Court. P.C. Huntingdon and Mr. Winfield gave evidence corroborating the statement of the Superintendent.,- -Prisoner alleged that the constables had thrown him down and kicked him in the mouth.—Dr. Brebner said he was called in to see P.C. Worden on the 23rd December. He found that one of the bones in his left foot had been fractured. It would be six or eight weeks before he would be able to walk properly,— Superintendent Strickland said there were 48 convic- tions registered against the prisoner, who had started his career of crime in 1885 as a boy.lie was birched the'n for breaking into a cricket club's tent. Afterwards he was sent to a reformatory for four years. A number of his convictions were for assaults on the police.— Alder- man Wareing said *that it was clear that the prisoner was a desperate character. The Bench intended to pub a stop, as far as they could, to these assaults on the police. Prisoner would be fined 2s. 6d. and costs, with the alternative of seven days' hard labour for having been drunk. For the assault on Wordea he would have to go to gaol for six months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>OUR GREATEST WOMAN WIT.</head>
        <p>OUR GREATEST WOMAN WIT.</p>
        <p>Lady Dorothy Nevill may be described as one of the institutions of London, amid a popular one at that. A daughter of the third Earl of Orford, Lady Dorothy as girl, wife and widow, travelled much, learnt to know men and cities, and mach; the acquaintance of many celebrities. She describes her recreations as " hortioniture, botany, and the tine arts," but in point of fact her favourite recreation is entertaining. Her house in Charles-street, Berkeley Square, is one of the best liked in London, and her Sunday luncheons, are brilliantly successful. Stuart, clever, .ancl lovely women, painters, preachers, and politicians, actors, judges, and journalists, soldiers' sailors—all people of light and leading meet under her hospitable roof. London has been described as one long whispering gallery to Lady- Dorothy Nevill. What she does not know is not worth knowing; but such is her discretion that she never tells a tale to the wrong person. She is perhaps the greatest woman wit of her time, and everywhere, from Sandring- ham downwards, she iS ft welcome guest Her quaint mob cap and rows of amber beads are a familiar sight in the smartest drawing rooms of London.—Frorn "The Reader."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lena Dpairiek, the wife of a farmer at Columbus ,1 Indiana, died recently from blood poisoning caused by t a blow in the eye from a cow's tail receivel while she . was milking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK COLLIER'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held an inquiry at Haydock on Wednes- day on the body of James Colquit, aged fifty-two, of Blackbrook-road, who died on Thursday last. The Coroner explained that this man was injured on the 12th of September, and it had been necessary to have a post-mortem to decide the cause of death. He said that three doctors were agreed that pneumonia was the cause of death, and it wouldbe unnecessary to go into the accident. A verdict ol " Death due to natural causes " was returned.</p>
        <p>-ifft-.-§EDDON, M.P., AND NATIONAL SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. for the Newton Di;ison, presided on Tuesday at a social gathering held at Wolverhampton-buildings to celebrate the opening of the new rooms of the St. Helens Socialist Society. About two hundred members and friends were present. Mr. Seddon said that the growth of Socialism locally was very satisfactory. The opening of the club marked a new epoch in the history of Socialism in St. Helens. They looked with every confidence to the future. Speak- ing of Socialism nationally, he was glad to say that during the last session of Parliament they had been able to bring about a new kind of legislation, and t;) place on the Statute Book of the country the first Socialistic measure—namely, the Act for the feeding of starving school children. He hoped that duing the coming year they would be able to bring within the range of practical politics a scheme of old-age pensions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Peter Healey, colliery contractor, of Collins Green was summoned by his wife, Clara Healey, for persistent cruelty. The wife said she was afraid of him on account os his drunken conduct, threats and viol 3nce. He had threatened both her and her baliy with a knife, and last June was chargad with threatening her with a gun, and bound over to keep the peace. She had left him several times and had had one separation order.—The Bench made the order asked for, allowing the plaintiff 10s. a week.</p>
        <p>Ellen Robinson was charged with stealing, from No. 16, Cherry-street., Earlestown, a piece of beef, Yalu% is. 10d. *belonging to John Crooks.—Crookes deposed' that after he had been drinking w:lh the woman (who lives nest door to him) he found her in his house, going away with the piece of beef which she had taken from his pantry.—P.C. Simpson said he was informed of the theft, and the woman admitted it.--The Bench bound her over under the First Offenders' Act to come up for judgment if called upon. Tho following were fined 2s. 6d. and costs for drunkenness: Joseph Bassett, Joseph Williams, George Pimblett (Elaydock), Jams Potter (Golborne).—Patrick Hannon, Mary Brown (fiaydock), and Ernest Boyle (Golborne) were fined ss. each.—John Langan and James Jolley, for using obscene language, were each fined ss. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LIVERPOOL FARM SCHCOL.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL FARM SCHCOL.</p>
        <p>The annual distribution of prizes in connection with the Liverpool Farm School, Newton, took place last Friday evening, the ceremony being performed by Mr. F. J. Wood. The proceedings, which were well attended and proveto be very enjoyable, were opened by the boss with the singing of a hymin, after which an address was given by Mr. Wood and the song "Dear harp of my country," was sung by the boys. Then followed the distribution of prizes, the results being as follows :—Religious knowledge : R. Lees, H. Maines, A. Green, J. Guyers and S. Bell. Arithmetic: J. Ball, S. Dickens and S. Hughes. Composition: F. Hart, D. Hatton and B. Edwards. Geography: W. Cowell T. Higgins and A. E. Burgess. History: G. Milnes, J. Sillory and J. Chesterman. Drawing: 3. Cook, A. Wright and J. Collings. Farm: W. Lewis and James Davies. Joiners: W. Cowell and G. Milnes. Manual woodworkers: Sam Hughes and W. Ricks. Plumbing: T. Douglas and A. Peers. Tailoring: J. Ball and E. Taylor. Shoemaking: E. Old and A. Green. Darners : H. Baker and A. Price. Baker: G. Curtis. Laundry: A. Peers and E. Old. Printers: W. Ricks, W. Mitchell and T. H. Bridson. Cricket: C. Holmes, Football: H. Baker. Gymnastics: Best all round, J. Guyers; parallel bar, R. Tims ; the horse, R. Lees ; gymnastic drill, E. T. Wright. General work : H. Stevens, J. Kelly, J. Shannon, S. Hughes. C. James, W. T. Wilkinson, A. Stavenson, W. Jarman, J. Leith, and G. V. Perry. Office: J Cook. Good conduct: H. E. Main, J. Lawson, W. Lewis, E. T. Wright, F. B. Ennifer. Herbert Love, Joseph Cranshaw and F. Lathom. Band ; A. Peers, T. Taylor, J. Saunders, E. Fricker, G. Mikes, James Guyers, W. Cowell, A. E. Jones, W. Fallows, A, Wright and T. Douglas; special, a. W. Evans. Tidiness: A. G:een and A. Diggle. Special : A. Noakes and J. Davidson. An excellent entertainment was afterwards given. ASSAULTING A SCHOOL ATTENDANCE OFFICER.</p>
        <p>William Stirrup was summoned at Newton on Thurs- day last for assaulting Alexander Walker, School Attendance Officer at Haydock.—De fondant asked for an adjournment, having only beenserved with his summons on Christmas Eve, which did not allow him time to get legal advice. Mr. Steel, for the Education Committee of the County Council, opposed the application, and as the defendant would not undertake to pay the costs of an adjournment the else proceeded.—Mr. Steel said the prosecutor, formerly a police officer and now School Attendance (Ulcer at Haydock, called at defEndant's shop, he being a grocer and hairdresser in Penny Lane, to enquire about. the xbsence of cl?fendant's boy aid girl from school. Defendant told him they were sick, I and on plaintiff asking for some particulars Stirrup came out from behind the counter and seized him with the intention of pushing him out of the shop. Had not the plaintiff been a strong man he would have fallen on his back.—Plaintiff gave evidence to that effect, and said the attack was quite sudden and unexpected ; defendant had been writing, and quietly put his pencil down, came round, and without any warning rushed upon him.— Defendant cross-examined plaintiff, apparently intend- ing to prove that he had warned plaintiff against going into his place without knocking, and told him that when he called to make enquiries he was to stand on the step outside and take his answers.—lte-examined I have never used bad language to Stirrup. He has used it to me, and has apologized to the Committee for having done so.—George F. Clarke, secretary to the local School Committee said he summoned defendant to appear before the Committee, and he there admitted having used bad language to Walker, and having put his hands on him. He searned*to think the officer had no right to go into his shop.—Defendant, being asked if he wished to give evidence on his own behalf, declined, repeating that he had not had time to get witnesses.—Fined 10s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>EX-MINERAL WATER MANUFATURER IN</p>
        <p>TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>At St. TTPlena, on Stturday, William Johrison, an ex-mineral water manufacturer, was sent to prison for tT3 wçk fo" sleeping out at the cheinical works, and refusing to pay maintenance towards his wife.</p>
        <p>ST. lIELENS COLLIER'S STRANGE DEATH. Thomas Colqultt, aged 45 years, who had hr employed at the Ashton Green Colliery, St. Helen., has died under somewhat strange circumstances at his home. 2, Blackhrook-roid, Haydock. It appears that on September 1.21 h h.st Colquitt was injured in the pit, by a haulage rope breaking and lascerating his leg exten- sively. He was medically attended, and on November 141,h he went to the colliery and resumed work. The leg broke down, and the poor fellow was compelled to lie up aga7n.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NEW ST. HELENS SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>NEW ST. HELENS SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Education Committee have secured a site off Robins-lane and Marshall's Cross-rnad from Capta,n Hughes, of Sherdley, for the proposed Council school for the Sutton district. The Board of Educa- tion have approved of the Den Lon's Green and Newtown Council school, and it has been decided to call it the Ravington-road- Council School." The large new scho9l that has been erected at Thatto Heath will dom- mance its career on the 7th inst. SUDDEN DEATHS AT ST. HELENS. Elizabeth M'Cauley, aged sixty-two, of 77, Parr-street St. Helens, died suddenly on Monday morning, soon after she had come downstairs for breakfast.—Elizabeth Ann Burrows, aged eight months, has died at the resid- ence of her parents, 1.6, Hamer-staeet, St. Helens, and the county coroner has decided to hold an inquest to-niortow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LIBRARIAN'S SALARY.</head>
        <p>THE LIBRARIAN'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary Committee of the St. Helens Corporation have decided to recommend the Council to increase the salary of Mr. A. Lancaster the borough librarian, from £223 to £250 per annum. Mr. Lancaster has held his office for the last twenty-five years, during which exceptional developments have been made in the libraries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>TIPSY CABBY'S ADVENTURE.</head>
        <p>TIPSY CABBY'S ADVENTURE.</p>
        <p>In charging a cabdriver with being drunk whilst in charge of a cab at St. Helens, on Tuesday morning, Police-constable Smith told an extraordinary story. Ile said that about one o'clock in the morning he saw a cab horse acting somewhat strangely in Liverpool-road. In turning a corner the horse went on to the footpath and the cab overturned. He found the cabman partly under the cab, and with the assistance of some passers-by the cab was lifted up, when it was found to contain two men passengers. The officer liberated the fares, and put the tipsy cabby inside and drove him to the Town Hall where he was locked up. The bench imposed a small fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FATAL CHRISTNIAS FALL.</head>
        <p>FATAL CHRISTNIAS FALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquiry at St. I Helens, on Wednesday, into the death of Martin Tyrells, 25, Back Clock -lace-road, St. Helens. Deceased was a roadman for the Whiston Rural Ccuncil, and sixty-two years of age. On Christmas Eve the wife said she went to bed, and some time afterwgirds she heard him fall downstairs. She got up and found him at the bottom of the stairs. He recovered himself, and managed to get upstairs by himself. He told her he had slipped on one of the steps and fallen backward. He was taken to the Providence Hospital, but died from the injuries caused by the fall about midnight on Friday. A verdict of Accidental death " was re corded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Services for -Sunday.</head>
        <p>Services for -Sunday.</p>
        <p>- A young ,people's service will be held at Wycliffe Church on Sunday morning, when an address will be delivered by the Rev. A. A. Lee, Bootle. The annual presentation of Bibles to members of the Pastor's class will be made at the close of the service. In the evening the Rev. J. Yonge will speak on the Wycliffe motto for 1907.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church: Preachers, 10.30, Rev. S. Pitt; 6.30, Rev. J. Manning. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : Preachers, 10.30, Rev. E. J. Gawne ; 8.30, Rev. S. Pitt. Golborne-street Baptist Church: Preacher, Mr. D. R. Waddill, Liverpool. ' Latchford Baptist Church: Preacher, Rev. J. F Matthews. Subjects, 10.30, " The final choice " ; 6.30 the annual " Motto " sermon.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A., the Rev. J. Yonge will speak on A new year and a new start."</p>
        <p>Padgate Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>A watch-night service was held on New Year's Eve when a good congregation assembled to spend the last hour of the old year in the church and to see the open- ing of the New Year in the same place. Mr. F.Wright officiated in the enforced absence of the Rev. W Mellor, the resident minister, through a severe.cold. litscliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a very good meeting of the Wycliffe Society on Sunday. The Secretary presided, and referred to many pleasing seasons of refreshing to the members during the year. Although they had always felt that their past had been defective in the sight'of God, there were experiences in all their lives that gave them the remembrance of unspeakable joy. Therefore let them try to increase those happy seasons, and commence the new year b being faithful to God. Then the future would be bright, glorious, and happy. Mr. C. F. Fooks spoke upon" This the time for taking stock."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Legh-street Primitive Methodists</head>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodists</p>
        <p>A large congregation assembled in Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday evening when a choral service was held, selections from the" Messiah" being given. After the preliminary overture, by Mr. F. Podmore, a programme was gone through which delighted even the most fastidious in the music-loving audience. The &amp;Lionises were especially effective, no pains evidently having been spared by the conductor (Mr. A. Spruce) to train the choir. Mr. J. H. Corrin was the tenor soloist; Miss A. Woodland and Miss Cruickshank the sopranos ; Mrs. W. Taylor, contralto ; Mr. B. H. Pearce, the bass; the latter being accom- panied on the cornet by Mr. W.Rutter, in "The trumpet shall sound."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>St Paul's Church.</head>
        <p>St Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 11E. Peters, for three years curate at Bed- worth Church, entered upon his duties on Tuesday as curate of St. Paul's Church. A presentation was made to the rev, gentleman at a tea and entertainment (held in the Centtal Girls' School, Bedworth, a few days ago) promoted by the YoungNlen's Institute. The presenta- tion consisted of a handsome set of silver fish knives and forks and a set of silver dessert knives and forks. Both sets of plate were in walnut cases, and each bore the following inscription Presented to the Rev. E. E. Peters by the members of Bedworth Church Institute on his leaving Bedworth, Christmas, 1906." Mr. Peters has been accorded a hearty reception by the members of St. Paul's parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>"MARGARET E. PARKER -A MEMORIAL"</head>
        <p>"MARGARET E. PARKER -A MEMORIAL"</p>
        <p>We have received from Mr. C. E. Parker a copy of a book bearing the above title and which he hag written in order to keep fresh in the minds of man the memory of the life and work of his mother. ThY book contains nearly 100 pages and forms the qui@ pathetic record of a noble and valuable life. Mrs- Margaret Parker was the decendant of the Parkers of. the Forest of Bowland on the borders of Lancashire and Yorkshire, who filled the office of bow-bearers to the Kings of England, the first being mentioned in the reign of Henry VIII. She herself was born in 1828 and was the eldest of a family of nine. lier early married life was spent in Dundee, Scotland, and it was here that her great services to the cause of temper- ance commenced. Mrs. Parker and her husband both became converts to temperance in 1674 as the result of. the visit of John B. Gough, the great temperance orator. In 1874 a women's deputation waited upon the licensing magistrates of Dundee to present a petition signed by 8,000 of their number asking for the reduction of licences. The ladies marched through the town led by Mrs. Parker and others, and it was Mrs. Parker who addressed the Provost and magistrates. She joined the Good Templar movement, was elected to the position of G. \V. Vice-Templar of the Grand Lodge of Scotland, and was sent as a representative tl the Grand Lodge Sessions held in America in 1875. On her return to England' full of crusade fire," Mrs, Parker, together with " Mother" Stewatt of America, matured plans for the formation of a National Associa- tion, and together they called the convention of temperance organisations held at Newcastle-on-Tyne in 1876, from which sprang the British Women's Temperance Association. Mrs. Parker was elected first president, and subsequently went a second time as a delegate to America. The book con- contains many Other chapters dealing interestingly with Airs. Parker's home life, her personal character- istics, her work 1r social parity, and her interest in industrial and social problems. Numerous ad Iresses and letters Of great interest are published including an address entitled" People I have met," which Mrs. Parker delivered in California, and e another on " Woman's place in the Church." We must cm- gratulate Mr. Parker on the result of this labour of love on his part. The memoir contains, we should imagine, a true insight into the life of a great Christian and remarkable woman. It is well written land abounds in reminiscences and anecdotes which I add a charm to the biographical narrative. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Dublin Motor Show,</head>
        <p>Dublin Motor Show,</p>
        <p>In view of the large number of motor cars which may be expected to be sent from England to Ireland in connection with the motor car show to be held in Dublin from the sth t.) the 12th January 1907 the London and North Western Railway Company have made special arrangements for the careful handling of cars to and from their steamers. We also learn that the London and North Western Railway Company in their usual enterprising way are prepared to sail, if necessary, a special serviee of steamers to meet the demands of the exhibitors sending cars to Ireland, and returning them to England at the close of the show. The route via Holyhead constitutes by far the quickest service between the two countries,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Sequel to the November Election.</head>
        <p>Sequel to the November Election.</p>
        <p>As a result of being knlcked down by a horse and trap in Hoyle-street, Warrington, on the day of the municipal elections, a six-years-old boy named Stanley Heaton, residing with his mother at 10, Catherine- street, succumbed to injuries in the Warrington Work- house on Friday the. 28th ult. At the inquest on Wednesday, it was stat.d that the boy, along with other companions, was marching with a prosession with lighted torches in the street, when a horse and trap, driven by a youth named Daniels, knocked him down and injured his head. He was taken to the infirmary, but later was removed to the workhouse hospital suffering from scarlet fever. The injuries he received entirely accounted for his death. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and the driver was exonerated from all blame.</p>
        <p>TO STAR LIFE ASSURANCE POLICY HOLDERS. The Norwich Union Office offers to guarantee a bonus - of 30s. per cent. per ann. on all with-profit "policies. Fall particulars from BISGOOD (Pc MARSHALL, 5, Moorgate St. Buildings, London.—Advt.</p>
