      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>ner uottnnes. ine brit isn -.e..?..41ire the v: one' over now has a population of nearly 40,000,000.</p>
        <p>Mr. Andrew Carnegie visited Wakefield to open the free library presented by him to filo city at a cost of £B,OOO. Just before the cere- mony the sun burst forth after a heavy shower. "Just my ,good luck !". declared Mr. Chrnegie; "always in sunshine. All through life I have been the darling of fortune. Nerve yourself for rain and storm; then' be sure you don't fail to bless your kind fate if you get sunshine."</p>
        <p>The reference by the Russian Press to the strained relations existing between Sir Claude MacDonald, British Anibaador at Tokio, and the Russian Ambassador'ther?., M. Bakhmetieff, . recalls the fact that the latter is an old Etonian, who wa3. promoted to Tokio on account of his intimats knowledge of the English language and English customs. Almost his earliest duty brought about a little unpleasantness: betwent dkimself and Sir Clatide MacDonald, which has !"Tnereased so much that, in the interests Of the Anglo-Russian entente, a section of the Russian Press is urging; the • new Minister of Foreign Affairs, M. Isvolskv, to recall him. Count Witte has been suggested as a subStitute.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Thomas Price, Premier of South</p>
        <p>Au9tralia. lead-er of the Labour Party in</p>
        <p>that State. has been indulging in some interest. 11,3, autobiographical reminiscences. After open- ing a mission ball in Adelaide, be said he knew a good deal about missions. In his childhood in Liverpool he attended a Methodist mission school. On his first appearance there he at next to a respectable boy, who poked fun at his humble and ill-fitting clothes. "I'll punch your nose when we git outside." said the future Prime Minister, and he did:. More than- that, he rose to be 6uperintendent of that mission school, and he "married the best girl in it. * •</p>
        <p>A student of heredity referred to the well- understood fact that the .offspring of distin- guished parents very rarely inherited the gifts of their progenitors. A man who aspires to dis- tinction, therefore, has a much better chance if he is descended from Parents of no great capacity. - But his chances are better still if his parents had talents which circumstances pre- vented them from revealing to tile world. A woman may, for `etample. PG a girl, have nourished vain ambitions to shine in music, Painting, or literature, and grown up lamenting that her aspirations were nipped in the bud. When. later, she is married, it is highly pro- bable that she will find her ambitions realised in her children, one or more of whom are almost suv,. inhorit the coveted talents.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT BOOT COMPANY. •• • 6 6 6 ' ;si = •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>ISIr.%A. J. Balfour, besides being an enthu- siastic golfer, is a good tennis player and bicyclist. His other fad is music, which he takes seriously. Handel he considers the most eminent of composers, and he rarely mieses at- tending the Handel Festival at the Crystal ,Palace.</p>
        <p>Lord Cromer is such an industrious man in Egypt that he rarely accepts an inyitation to (huller. In order to maintain his strength and to -get through Iris day's work he has to go- to bed early and to rise at about six in the morning. * *</p>
        <p>When the German Emperor was a student at a college at Cassel his pocket-money was strictly limited to ss. a week, most of which went not to the tuck-shop, but in tipping servants. And yet this heir to an Imperial throne did not con- sider himself badly treated—or, at any rate, he never said so.</p>
        <p>The policemen of England and Wales are so numerous that if they were arranged in a single file at intervals of a yard they would form a procession long enough to reach from London half-way to Brighton. In the Metropo- litan area there is one constable to every 470 people, and in the provinces one to 942. The average cost of each "gentleman in blue" is a little under £lOO a year.</p>
        <p>Lord •Bute should have been born a biblio- pliile, yet long before he had done with his -tees, indeed, from quite early boyhood, he became a naturalist, and by the time he was f fteen there was not a bird of the air nor a beast of the field, nor a plant, with whcee habit 6 he was not familiar. He has travelled far and wide, is a young man of clear head and keen judgment, and he is one of the most patriotic a Scotsmen.</p>
        <p>The Grand Duke Nicholas is believed to have the longest neck of any prince in Europe. It almost of giraffe dimmisions. Hies cousin. the Grand Duke Constantine, comes next; and Then the King of the Hellenes. It has been re- marked that the long-necked hardly ever die of apoplexy, and that they are not liable to vio- lent anger.</p>
        <p>Mr. Simeon Hart has just completed fifty years' service as verger and sexton of the parish church at Eastbourne. His father was sexton -there for sixty years, his grandfather was clerk for twenty-eight years, and, his great-uncle, great-grandfather, and great-great-grandfather each held the office of clerk.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Yarmouth during his thirty-four years of life has played many parts. His mar- riage to Miss Alice Thaw, of Pittsburg, is, of course, remembered. Before that he had been an officer in the "Black Watch," a ugar planter and poultry farmer in Australia, and had occupied a place on the professional and .amateur stage.</p>
        <p>, President Roosevelt is a hunter of big game, and a splendid shot. In the midst of his ardu- ous duties as President he contrives to keep abreast of all the best current literature. He is a great admirer of Kipling, and he possesses a collection of al the works that author has pro- duced. * *</p>
        <p>Despite his years, which number '62, Sir Charles Dilke is one of the .most active members of the House of Commons. An enthusiastic sculler, he goes through a course of training -every year. His week-ends during the Session he spends up the river at_Shepperton,where, on Saturdays, accompanied by a powerful oars- man, he is frequently to be seen pulling hard in a double sculler. Fencing is another of Sir -Charles's exercises.</p>
        <p>The Earls of liVinchilsea are no longer the igreat landed magnatee they once were, when they owned Eastwell Park in Kent, long occu- pied by the Duke of Edinburgh, and now the properly of Lord Gerard; Kirby Hall, the stately maneion in Northamptonshire, now a rooflees ruin, where Sir Christopher Hatton entertained Queen Elizabeth; and estates in ether parts of England. •</p>
        <p>Sir J. Lawson Walton, at a dinner in Edin- burgh, said one might attain success at the Bar, "devilling" until one became an arch-fiend and Attorney-General's "devil," By writing a book, By marrying a rich attorney's laughter, 6-r By a miracle. e last played a large part in the applicatioß of any of the precepto.</p>
        <p>Lord Aberdeen's sons, the Hon. Dudley and -the Hon. Archie Gordon, have strongly de- veloped mechanical aptitudes, and worked for . a time as ordinary apprentices in one of the big shipbuilding yards at Aberdeen, 'being treated in exactly the same way and leading the fame hardi healthy life as tit* other lade. Doubtless they have inherited their taete in this matter from their father, who ie one of the most expert amateur locomotive engine-drivers in the , three 'kingdoms. /.</p>
        <p>Recent figures on the population of Japan make the total about 5,000,000 more than that of the British Isles. Tokio has about 1,500,000, Osaka 900,000, and there are 20 other cities with more than 50,000 each. , However, in making comparisons it must be remembered that England's sons are scattered throughout</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ON CRUTCHES '7 YEARS.</head>
        <p>ON CRUTCHES '7 YEARS.</p>
        <p>PRONOUNCED INCURABLE. SUFFERED FROM SPINAL PARALYSIS. , MIRACULOUSLY CURED BY DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS. JESSE ROSLING, Church Lane, Preston, 'near Hull, writes :—" For ieven years I was suffer- ing with Paralysis and Spinal Weakness. The 'eeling had gone entirely :rom my limhs,and it was with the utmost difficulty bat I could walk with !rntches. I lost flesh -apidly, ard was uhable to -ork. The doctors here taid I was incurable. rhree months ago I com- lencecl to take DR. A SSELL'S FLESH-FORM- AND STRENGTHENING TABLETS, and am glad to Mn. Rosr.r.,.. (from Photo), say gi, the present time I can walk without assistance. Afte-r the first month I gained three pounds in weight; the feeling came back to my limbs, and I must say that the cure you have effected is nothing short of miraculous. lam able to work a little now, and have had a number of visitors coming to tee me who could not believe that Dr. Cassell's Tablets bad done so much good. You are at liberty to publish this letter,as I can- not do too much to show my gratitude." DR. CASSELL'S FLESH-FORMING AND STRENGTHENING TABLETS create Nerve, Blood. Healthy Flesh, Strength, Vitality, Prevent Premature Greyness, and delay Old Age Appearance. The remedy of doctors and scientific men; the perfection of medicine for weak decaying nerves and body in young or old. Ask for DR. CASSELL'S FLESH-FORMING AND STRENGTHENING TABLETS, price IQ.d., and 2s. fid. per bottle. Sold by all Chemists, or post free from the famous Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd., King Street West, Manchester. Advice free by letter.</p>
