      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON EISTEDDFOD.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL CONTESTS AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Local Competitors to the Fore.</p>
        <p>Although the Eisteddfod is an esser.tially Welsh institution, it is undoubtedly thriving in our midst and New Year's. Day witnessed the holding of the fifth annual festival of the kind for which Warrington musical enthusiasts have been responsible. It speaks volumes for the energy and perseverance of the com- mittee that a venture which in 1902 was entirely new to the town has become 'firmly established as one of the leading musical events of the year' There is evidence, too, of a we!come advance in public appreciation, and the promoters must feel gratified at the ,success of their efforts to interest the public in musical culture. Of course, in the matter of popular support there is sti!l much to be desired, but the progress already made is most encouraging. Tuesday's Eisteddfod worthily upheld the high standard set at previous meetings, and most of the competitions were exceedingly keen. The committee were fortunate in their choice of adjudicators; the duties being discharged very care- fully and tactfully by Dr. W. G. McNaught, F.R.A..M. and Mr. Wilfred Jones, IL AM. (vocal music) and Mr. J. Partingtoia (instrumental contest.) The decisions appeared to meet with general approval,, each adjudi- cation being accompanied by a close weighing of the merits and defects of the competitors and the impart- ing of sound advice. At the afternoon meeting the public attendance was not large, but there was a decided improvement in the evening, the hall being well filled. The Rev. W. Wynn Davies, of Bangor, once more appeared as conductor of the meetings and was as genial as ever. The most pleasing feature of the day's proceedings was the admirable series of sliccesses which fell to Warrington competitors. In each of the Choral eyents,Warringtonians had the proud distinction of carrying off the awards and local soloists also did • themselves credit. It was a source of dis- appointment that only two choirs—Warrington Male Voice and St. Helens Glee Club took Dart in the chief choral contest, but the victory of the Warring- tonians after a close fight, was greeted with great enthusiasm. An able accompanist was found in Mr Ernest Wilkes, A.L.C.M. The committee which had had the arrangements in hand consisted of the follow- ing : —Messrs. Nathan Cook (chairman), J. Broadhurst, W. Oritchley,A. Greenough,C W. Nurse, W. Knowles, George R.atcliffe, W. Woodcock aud J. Wilkes, with Mr. Cr. Morrell (hon. 'treasurer), Mr. R. W. Cook (hon. secretary), and Mr. P. J. George (assistant secretary). 11. special word of praise is due to Mr. R. W. Cook who discharged the secretarial duties admirably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>AFTERNOON MEETING.</head>
        <p>AFTERNOON MEETING.</p>
        <p>The afternoon meeting was chiefly notable for child- ren's contests which afforded great pleasure to the audience.</p>
        <p>PIANOFORTE SOLO.</p>
        <p>_ The opening item was the pianoforte solo for corn- petitors not more than twelve years of age, the test piece being" Playfulness" (Gustav Lange). The chil- dren selected to appear in the final were Nellie Little- wood, Minnie Foden, Emily Wobleyand Albert Hart. Before making his award Dr. MeNaught emphasised the fact that if children were to become good pianists, they must learn to get such control of their fingers that they would be able to give expression to the rhythmic instinct. There were twenty-six competitors in this event and the playing of some was quite spoiled by con- tinuous pedalling. With regard to the final,he awarded the prize to Nellie Littlewood who displayed good talent and played with feeling. The marks were allocated as follows :—Nellie Littlewood, 56 ; Minnie Foden, 51; Emily Woolen 49; Albert Hart, 53. GIRLS' CHALLENGE SOLO. Out of five competitors, the following were selected to appear on the platform :—May Isherwood, who sang "Bells of Gold " ; Violet Brown, who sang "The Gift " ; and Gladys Thomas, who sang "Angels ever bright, and fair.' The judges had no hesitation in awarding the prize to Violet Brown who, Mr. Jones said, possessed a remarkable voice for her age. Her intonation was good, and the general effect was most pleasing.</p>
        <p>BOYS' CHALLENGE SOLO. There were eight competitors, and those who had the good fortune to sing in the final were Willie Fryar ("Flight of Ages ') ; Fred Tarbuck (" Angels ever bright and fair ") and John Greaves ("The Swallows.") The winner was Fred Tarbuck, of Sutton, who, the adjudicator remarked, possessed an exceptional voice— one of the best boys' voices he had overheard—and gave a very artistic rendering.</p>
        <p>ESSAY COMP ETITION. In the competition for the best essay on "The town of Warfington,' Councillor Evans decided to divide the prize between Ben Farrar and George Woodcock.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CHOIR COMPETITION. • Great interest centred in this competition, for which the test piece was the favourite " Don't forget the old folks" (Jude). The following choirs competed : Warrington Secondary School (conductor, Mr. R. H. Albery) ' • Warrington Harmonic Juvenile Choir (con- ductor, Mr. T. Brough) ; Albert-road Juvenile Choir I,conductor, Mr. J. Platt) ; Sutton United Juvenile Choir (conductor, Mr. Wt Knapper). Dr McNaught said there were -evidences of excellent capacity in all the choirs and it was delightful to hear how enthusiasti- cally and expressively the children sang. The first prize of .C.l and gold centre medal was won by the Warrington Harmonic Juvenile Choir whose singing called forth warm praise from Dr. McNaught. He said the tone was pretty and delicate and there was at once the note of feeling and sympathy. It was evident that their .peculiar tone was inspired by their inward feeling. The voices were rather thin and not dead in tune in the first verse, but the attack of the next verse was good, showing high training. The general effect was dainty, pretty and springy, and a feature was the elastic rhythm. The Warrington Secondary School succeeded in winning the second prize of £l. The adjudicator said their rendering was tuneful and sweet anigthey kept well in tune. There was not sufficient delicacy and daintiness but there was a pretty blend of tone which charmed the ear. The choir was well trained and obedient to the conductor. The perform- ance was fairly expressive but did not quite secure the pathetic note. With regard to the Albert-road choir, the children were very enthusiastic but the voices were not thoroughly blended. Th 3 execution was free and fluent and the agreeable enunciation was a conspicuous feature.. The singing, however, needed more delicacy combined with warmth. The speciriity of the Sutton United choir was the unity of the attack at the outset but there was a suspicion of failing intonation after- wards. The marks were allocated as follows :—War- ringtonHarmonic, 72 out of a possible 80; Warrington Secondary, 65 ; Albert-road, 60; Sutton United, 61. The result was most enthusiastically received.</p>
        <p>SOPRANO SOLO COMPETITION. Gounod's " 0 Divine Redeemer" was the test piece for this competition which was one of the most inter- esting of the day. The ladies appearing in the final were—Miss Martha Farriman, Miss Alice Woodlann,and Miss Carrie Hastings. The adjudicator said Miss Farriman showed uncommon dramatic facultigs but she rather tended to sacrifice beauty of tone to intensity of expression. Miss Woodland possessed a voice of very agreeable tone but he would advise her to paint her vowels a little more vividly. She sh@wed a nice refinement of style and, although at times there was an absence of colour, she caught the necessary feeling of rapturous adoration. Miss Hastings had a very charm- ing voice and it had that peculiar" tearful "quality that touched one. She was best in her quieter moods but she had very little resources for working up a big climax. Her singing was very pretty, if she could get a larger style and deeper expression. He awarded the prize to Miss Woodland (Warrington) with 66 marks, each of the other competitors obtaining 63 marks.</p>
        <p>TENOR SOLO COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The competitors appearing on the platform were Mr. J. H. Berry, Mr. J. H. Corrin, and' Mr. W. G. Hughes, and their interpretations ol "Comfort ye "and" Every valley" (Handel) were listened to with considerable pleasure. Mr. Berry. (Bolton) reCeived the prize, the adjudicator remarking that he possessed a very good voice, though at times it was inclined to be a little metallic. He advised every tenor he had heard that afternoon to cultivate close singing,as they sang far too openly. On the whole Mr. Berry's rendering was a very fine performance. Mr. Corrin's voice was good but he sang to openly on the to notes and took far too much liberty with the solo. His enunciation, too, was rather defective. Mr. Hughes started out of tune, but that was no doubt due to nervousness. The adjudicators could see he was an artist and could have sung much better. . CIiiTRCH OR CHAPEL CHOIR COMPETITION. _</p>
        <p>This competition, which brought- the -afternoon meeting to a close, was wexcellent one and was fol- lowed with great interest. The contestants were Stockton Heath Wesleyan Choir (conductor, Mr. F. E. Penketh), Bewsey-road Wesleyan (Mr. William Hipkiss) and Legh-street Primitive Methodist (Mr. A. Spruce). Gonnod's " Send out Thy Light "was the test piece. The prize of £4 4s. and gold centre medal went to Stockton Heath Wesleyans whose singing, said Dr. Mc Naught, showed a very agreeable blend while the execution was pleasantly clean and fluent. The enunci- ation and rhythm were good and the attack was first rate. Altogether the performance was a very interest- ing one with many good features, Although the tenors were rather weak in the, balance. He awarded the choir 61 marks. Legh-street, with obtained 62 marks, showed good unity in the attack and the enunciation was painstaking. There was a fair balance of parts, the alts being the weakest. Bewsey- road (54 marks) possessed good sopranos but the altos were weak. There wr.s a good unity of attack but the singing was rather muffled, and the basses were uncertain. The choir was very well drilled but lacking in the tone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>EVENING MEETING.</p>
        <p>The evening meeting was most successful and enjoy- able. Agreeable breaks in the succession of contests were provided by the Harmonic Juvenile choir and Stockton Heath Wesleyan choir—the winners of the afternoon contests—who delighted the audience. Dr. McNaught expressed great admiration of the perform- ance of the Elarmonic Juvenile choir, stating that the children sang most charmingly and were a great credit to their teacher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>PIANOFORTE SOLO COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>PIANOFORTE SOLO COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>This contest, for competitors between the ages of twelve and eighteen, attracted a large number of entries, the test piece being "Chant du Soir " (Paul Ambroise). Those selected to appear in the final were Miss Jessie Farrar, Master Charles Eckersley. and Miss Florrie Bennett, and Dr. McNaught said there were others who were almost equally worthy of appearing on the platform. The prize was awarded to Miss Jessie Farrar, Dr. McNaught observing that she promised to be a very good player. She had the faculty of playing with expression and her performance was quiet and modest, there being no "show." Real feeling was evidenced, although perhaps there was not quite enough colour, and there were some charming passages in the execution. She showed that precious faculty—without the possession of which no one could make a fine singer or player—the faculty of temperament, the power to respond to the varied demands of the piece. He awarded her 66 marks. Master Eckersley obtained 62 marks, the adjudicator stating that his playing was very effective, but there was a tendency to over-accent. Miss Florrie Bennett also gained 62 marks, Dr. McNaught stating that in her case there was even more of the temperament which responded to the melody of the piece, and a command of style. She, however, hesitated occasionally and there was not enough climax.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MALE VOICE GLEE COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>MALE VOICE GLEE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The test piece for this competition was Parry's "Sailor's Chorus," which was rendered by the Warring- ton Co-operative Glee Society(conductor,Mr.A.E.Trow), Hindley Independent Male Voice Choir (Mr. James Layland) and St. Helens Glee Club (Dr. S. B. Siddall). The victory—a most popular one—rested with the Warrington Co-operative, the judge specially comment- ing dpon the resonance of tone and the confident execution. The conductor showed the. brains to reserve his finest effect for the climax on the last page and the choir made the performance not merely a peformance, hut an interpretation. He awarded them 67 marks. St. Helens Glee Sooiety (65 marks) showed well unified tone but it lackel richness. The execution was vigorous. The inner parts were not filled well enough and the general effect therefore was somewhat hollow. Hindley choir (61) had evidently not quite so much experience and the voices were not so well knit together as in the other choirs. The rhythm was too heavy.</p>
        <p>CONTRALTO SOLO COMARTITION. This competition provided one of the treats of the Eisteddfod. The finalists are Miss C. Roscoe,Miss Lily Fairhurq and a lady from St. listens who did not give her name.Mr.Jones said the adjudicators had no hesitation in awarding the prize to Miss Lily Fairhurst (Warrington) who was a thorough artist, making the most of a very agreeably toned voice. What was more, she had the soul of an artist, and the adjudicato..s derived pleasure from listening to her. Dr. McNaught supplemented Mr. Jones's remarks and expressed the hone that • Miss Fairhurst would apply her talent to the interpretation of thehighest class of Music. Miss Roscoe possessed a good voice, but it was somewhat unequal, and she was rather lacking in the dramatic instinct. The third com- petitor gave a very plucky rendering, but she was at a disadvantage in having to follow Miss Fairhurst's excellent performance. The competition, added the adjudicators, was the best solo contest of the day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>INSTRUMENTAL SOLO.</head>
        <p>INSTRUMENTAL SOLO.</p>
        <p>This was a new feature and attracted many competi- tors, the selection being "Harbour Lights " (Pod more). The adjudicator awarded the first prize of .21 Is. and a gold centre medal to Mr. W.Weedall of Swinton whose playing, he said, was an artistic triumph: Mr. A. Allanson, of Warrington, secured the second prize, his performance, said the adjudicator being very fine indeed, but the tempo was rather too slow. The third award went to Mr. Henry Banner, of Warrington, who gave a very intelligent rendering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>BASS_ OR BARITONE COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>BASS_ OR BARITONE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>This competition, said the adjudicators, was not very interesting, and all the competitors sang wrong notes, which was something exceptional at the present time. The test piece was Hatton's "Revenge," and the finalists were Mr. L. Greenwood, Mr. R. Tranter and Mr. E. Pearce. The winner was Mr. E. Pearce (War- rington) Oho, in the opinion of the judges, gave by far the best interpretation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>CEEALLENGE SOLO COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>CEEALLENGE SOLO COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The song selected for this contest was "The Glorious City" (Gonlon, Temple) and the competitors who sang in public were Miss A. Woodland, Mr. R. llranter, and Mr. Walter Shaw. Dr. McNaught said he thought the singers were worthy of a better composition. He awarded the prize to Mr. Shaw (Runcorn) who, he said, was an experienced vocalist and sang with confidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MALE CROM COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>MALE CROM COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>This was the- chief contest of the day but it was shorn of some of its interest by the fact that only two choirs took part :—Warrington Male Voice Choir, (Nit.. W. S. Nesbitt, conductor), and St. Helens Glee Club (Dr. S. B. Siddall). The Southport Vocal Union had also entered but failed to put in an appearance. Both the competing choirs gave stirring enderings of "The Britons " (Prothroe). It was , a source of gratification that the Warrington choir ' carried off the prize of £l2 12s. and silver cup. i Dr. McNaught, n giving his adjudication, said the piece was not an easy one to sing. It called for a peculiar faculty very frequently exemplified in its highest degree in Wales—the fervent dramatic faculty. Both thechoirs had been well prepared, and were com- petent to do the work set them. In the case of St. Helens choir the opening was good, and the members caught the colour and the determination necessary. They were warriors on the march, and there was a big tone and good attack. The solo was sung with fervency, but the choir later was not always dead in tune. The tone and execution were not always well knit. On the whole, however, it was a fairly dramatic rendering. The choir did not fulfil the promise of their youth. (Laughter.) In the case of the Warrington choir the tone was well knit nearly all through. It was square and finished. All the phrases were refined, and all the words were uttered and every point made. The render- ing of the solo was not so good in the second choir as in the first. it was perhaps a little too forcible. The tone of the choir on the whole lacked beautiful reson- ance and wanted livening up, bnt all the same the correct picture was there. His marks were :—Warring- ton.6B, St. Helens 64.</p>
        <p>The Rev. WYNN DAVIES thanked all who had assisted in arranging the eisteddfod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>COMPLIMENT FROM AMERICA.</head>
        <p>COMPLIMENT FROM AMERICA.</p>
        <p>Mr. TomLIN, an eminent musician, of Chicago (the official choral director of the Columbian Exhibition, 1903) who had attended the eisteddfod as an old friend of Dr. McNaught, briefly arldressed the meeting, and said he wished there were to be found among thp masses of the people of America anything compared to what he hadheard that evening in Warrington from a musical point of view. (Applause). He offered thecompetitors his most sincere and hearty complhnents upon their work. They had rhythm in their hearts as well as in their voices. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>L. &amp; N.-W AND THEIR EMPLOYEES.</head>
        <p>L. &amp; N.-W AND THEIR EMPLOYEES.</p>
        <p>Provision of Further Recreative Facilities.</p>
        <p>In industrial undertakings conducted on the most modern lines great attention is paid to the social side of the employees lines and ever increasing facilities are pro- vided for the recreation of the workers. Amongst those firms which have shown an active interest in this direction is the London and North-Western railway who in Warrington have made handsome provision for the Social enjoyment of their servants. Comparatively recently the railway institute at Bank Quay was opened where the railway men found ample provision for indoor recreation. The latest step to be taken is in arranging facilities for summer sport, and this , week was commenced the work of laying a bowling ' green on the vacant land adjoing the District Offices at Arpley: Here there is suffieient space for a large green and being conveniently situated it ahotild prove a great attraction. The green will be constructed on the most approved principles and, it is hoped, will be open for play at the at the latter end of the coming season. For those to whom bowling does not appeal it is proposed to construct a miniature open air rifle range in the institute grounds. Not the least pleasing feature of the innovation will be the great improvement which will be effected in the appearance of the Arpley premises as it is intended to lay out the grounds with shrubs and flower beds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Promotion for Lord Acton.</head>
        <p>Promotion for Lord Acton.</p>
        <p>The "World "says : Lord Acton, who has been attached to the British Embassy at Madrid for a short time, previous to which he had been Second Secretary at Berne since 1903, has now been promoted to First Secretary, and 'goes in that capacity to the British Legation at the Netherlands Court. Lord Acton has' already arrived at The Hague, and will be joined there by Lady Acton, who has been visiting her mother-in-law, the Dowager Lady Acton, at Cannes. Lord Acton, who is in his 35th year, i half German by blood, his mother and grandmother having both been Germans. His education, however, has been quite English, and he is a graduate of Magdalene College, Oxford. He married Miss Dorothy Lyon in I 1904, and has two-taughters. Lady Acton is a highly- cultivated woman, with marked literary, artistic, and musical tastes. She is fond, moreover, of outdoor life and sports, is a graceful horsewoman and a first- rate tennis player. Both Lord and Lady Acton are excellent linguists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HOW TO TREAT HEAVY COLDS.</head>
        <p>HOW TO TREAT HEAVY COLDS.</p>
        <p>The Head Master of a School writes :—" Council School, Won, near Llangollen.—Sirs,—For the last four or five years I have used Angler's Emulsion for colds,•coughs and rheumatic pains, and am pleased to testify to its soothing powers.—‘(Signed) HENRY CooK, Head Master."</p>
        <p>A mother writes of its effect upon her little girl : " 301 Oakhill-road, Beckenham.—Dear Sirs,—My little girl is very delicate, and used to suffer a great deal in the winter with bronchitis but has not had an attack since she began taking Angler's Emulsion four years ago. As soon as she gets a cold I give her the Emulsion and it relieves her at once. I have always recommended it wherever I could, and have always heard the same opinion—that it is wonderful.—(Signed) M. TATTER- SALL."</p>
        <p>As soon as you feel a cold coming on, begin imme- diately with Angier's Emulsion in doses of two tea- spoonfuls every two or three hours. This remedy not only soothes the mucous membrane of the lungs and air passages, relieves the harassing cough, and allays the soreness and irritation of the throat and chest, but it also keeps the digestive organs in a healthy condition and gives tone and vitality to the whole system. In most cases it requires but a few days' use of Angier's Emulsion to completely eradicate a case of "cold." If you will send 3d. for postage, and mention this paper, we will send you a free sample. Angier Chemical Co., 32, Snow Hill, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SAD DROWNING FATALITY -- Distressing Accident at Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>SAD DROWNING FATALITY -- Distressing Accident at Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>Mr. Yates, coroner, held an inquest in the Large Room, Grappenhall, on Tuesday, on the body of Alice Smith (37), who met her death by drowning the previous day. Mr. Kinsey, Shadewood Cottage, said the deceased was his step-daughter. Witness left home on Monday morning and left his step-daughter in charge of the house, witness's wife being in bed suffering from rheu- mtism. His step-daughter intended to do the washing, and when witness departed sha seemed to be in her usual health, On returning about one o'clock witness went into the house and noticed that the fire was nearly out. He shouted upstairs and asked his wife where Alice was. Witness's wife replied, "Isn't she down there? She brought me a cup of milk at eleven o'clock." Witness then went and looked round the outbuildings, but could not see anything of her. Then he noticed a bucket at the side of the well, and on getting closer he saw a brush on the footpath a few yards away. The footpath had been brushed and going down the steps to the well he saw the deceased in the water, head foremost, with her feet sticking out a foot. Witness had not the strength to get her out, so he went to the Police Station. The constable's wife returned with him, and they got the body out. Immediately afterwards P.C. Sumner arrived and tried artificial means of respiration but without success. Dr. Jago was in the meantime sent for, and he pronounced life to be extinct. At the time the deceased was wearing strong boots with nails in the toes and tips on the heels. Thera was some snow on the steps leading down to the well. Witness thought the deceased must have gone to fetch water for the cows and fallen in. _</p>
        <p>She had never expressed any intention of taking her life.</p>
        <p>Richard Thomas Dutton, a clerk, residing at Cedar- terrace, Latchford Locks, said he was engaged to be married to Miss Smith, and the "happy event" should have taken place in the near future. Witness had courted Miss Smith for over six years. Witness saw her on Sunday morning, afternoon, and evening, and they parted good friends. Mr. Yates: Has she been subject to giddiness ? She complained of her head aching at times. P.C. Sumner spoke to being called to Shadewood Cottage on Monday afternoon. He could not under- stand how Miss Smith could get into.the well. The water was ift.,9in. deep. Dr. Jago said he attended Miss Smith professionally. She suffered from giddiness resulting from indigestion. She was perfectly right in her mind, and of a very happy, cheerful disposition, His opinion was that Miss Smith went giddy whilst on the steps of the well and fell forward into the water. She would then be quite helpless. Miss Smith was the last person in the world he would have thought would commit suicide • she was so absolutely devoted to the,invalid mother. 'He never saw.a greater case of devotion.</p>
        <p>The -jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned, death being due to accidental nausea."</p>
        <p>DEAR NIEIIES AND NEEBEWS,</p>
        <p>To use an old phrase, we are now on the threshold of another year; so I feel that it is my duty to wish you, one and all, every prosperity for the coming twelve months.. Of course it is usuatat this time to make many resolutions; but, alas, like the proverbial pie crust, many are liable to be broken, therefore it is necessary to think exactly which resolves are possible and which are not. I recollect when I was of the Children's Corner age, what numerous rOolutions I used to make, such as saving up every pe.nny I got.; but, bless me, the first tuck shop I had to pass, well you know what happened. II was generally a penny poorer. Now I dot. ,s want you to make such impossible attempts as that. What I want my dear friends to make up their minds to do is to resolve to do nothing but what is right.; be dutiful, be obedient, and always have good feelings one with another, and at the end of the year you'll find that you have discovered the true secret of Jsa-Ppiness. Well, I will leave off sermonising, and "get into lighter vein. Of course, as most of you are aware, we editor persons have to do our work a week or two before you see it in the paper, and I may as well tell you that I am now writing this letter some days before Christ- mas; therefore I am unable to tell you how 1 enjoyed Christmas Day; but hope to do so in my next letter. lam eagerly awaiting the post, which will bring me in maily little interesting accounts ,of how you enjoyed yours. And don't forget the splendid prize for the best description. a.. I have been asked by several nephews for particulars about. Mason's Com- petition, which gives sii footballs free every week. Well, the partieurars will be' supplied by Messrs. Newhall and Mason, Nottingham, upon receipt of a postcard asking for a sample bottle of their famous Essence of Coffee. So I should advise ,all you boys to write for one. The prizes this week for presents given in aid of poor and needy little ones are awarded to Olive Kingdon, who sends in a beautiful warm shawl, and to Hilda Brown, for a very acceptable petticoat. These and many other articles have been distributed to very grateful recipients.</p>
        <p>Yours, as usual,</p>
        <p>WELCOME TO</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>,M. Wadsworth, George Wheatley, Einmie Dunn, Nellie Bray, Bessie Lewis, Florence Lewis.</p>
        <p>ANSWERS TO CONTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>HILDA M.—Thanks for postcard acknowledging the box. Glad you enjoyed the con- tents. • ANNIE R.—Atin afraid I cannot find room this week for your contributions, but will try later on. They are very good. Eve. S.--Flease accept my apologies for the delay caused through pressure of • Christmas business. ,By the time you al read this you will have received them. T. TIIACKER.—I hope you will like the Extract lam sending. Write to Newball and Mason, Nottingham, for particulars. They will send by return.</p>
        <p>'HE PRINCESS WHO COULDN'T</p>
        <p>MIL:</p>
        <p>Once upon a time there was a Princess couldn't smile, and she had never smiled laughed in her life. When she was .7 the sunbeams stole through the palace \\ and played, she had stared at them ingly with her big blue eyes, but er or " cooed " as most babies dk •J• 01 shook her rattle to amuse her</p>
