      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>NOT ILL-BUT AILING.</head>
        <p>NOT ILL-BUT AILING.</p>
        <p>Advice to Those Out-of-Sorts.</p>
        <p>There are thousands of women and men who, though they are not really the true sense of the word, are always ailing. They constantly dwell on the borders of ill-health. They are like the mountain-climber who in scaling the dizzy ridges is ever tempting fate by ventur- ing near the edge of a precipice. A sudden gust of vtind a slip br a false step will fling him into the yawning chasm beneath. People who are always ailing, though not really ill, spend their lives at the edge of an awful abyss, into which the first epidemic or first chilly weather may sweep their frail forms. But apart from the danger of always ailing, this condition is in itself most miserable and trying. You have neither the energy to share other people's pleasures, nor the strength to resist brooding over more or less magnified trouble. Sleepless nights, loss of appetite, severe headaches, bouts of sickness, and pains after meals, only accentuate the ever-present feeling of great depression and weari- ness. You lose interest in the home, ambition in life, and are often cross, irritable and hard to please all because of some insidious weakness which you find it difficult to understand.</p>
        <p>Because of the sedentary lives they lead, women are very liable to suffer froni this "always ailing" con- dition.</p>
        <p>THESE DISTRESSING SYMPTOMS EXPLAINED. All this is a sign that you are low in tone, and that you need a course. of Bile Beans for Biliousness to restore your lost health and buoyant spirits. Bile Beans enable you to " live " every day, not merely to drag through it. Bile Beans owe their great success to the natural and unfailingly efficacious action of certain precious herbal extracts of which they are wholly com- posed. They are a gift direct from forest to patient. Bile Beans not only contain certain rare vegetable extracts, but these extracts are united in a certain manner which multiplies the virtue of each ingredient. One ingredient, for instance, has a special power over the liver and regulates the flow of bile; another directly assists and strengthens the stomach : others again exert a wholesome influence over the bowels, and so on. But not one of the ingredients inter- feres with the rest. There is perfect harmony of action, the disordered conditions which are causing trouble and menacing life are corrected, and new vigour and vitality is firmly established throughout the body, blood, and nerves. in short, Bile Beans change the life of constant ailing to one of lasting health and happi- ness. How different are Bile Beans from old-fashioned, mineral purgatives, on which many people rely even to-day, ignorant of their dangers. The wise housewife always has a box of Bile Beans handy, and the far-sighted business and working man has a few Bile Beans in his vest pocket, for by reason of their gelatine coating Bile Beans can be carried loose without losing their virtue. Bile Beans may be obtained of all chemists, at is. lid. per box, or 2s. 9d. for box containing three times the quantity. Refuse all sub- stitutes, and accept only the genuine Bile Beans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BRUTAL HUSBAND.</head>
        <p>BRUTAL HUSBAND.</p>
        <p>After forty years of married life Ellen Brandwood, who formerly resided at Fearnhead,Warrington, applied at the Warrington County Petty Sessions, on Wednes- day, for a separation order against her husband, Arthur Brandwood, on account of his persistent cruelty.—Mr. Steel, who .appeared for complainant, stated that defendant came into an annuity fourteen years ago, and since then he had given way to intemperance. The cruelty was not only in occasional thrashings, but he had used threats and disgusting language in front of the children. Three homes had been broken up, and the children and the complainant had from time to time been locked out.—The magistrates granted an order, with an allowance of ss. per week.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at Dewsbury on Wednesday of " Bill " Stadden, ex-international football player, who died at the Dewsbury Infirmary on Sunday night from self-inflicted injuries to his throat, after, it is supposed he had strangled his wife.</p>
        <p>Potato Cakes raised with Brown CE%</p>
        <p>Poison's "Paisley Flour" are lighter, better raised, more wholesome and much easier of digestion than ever before.</p>
        <p>Take 2 ozs. "Paisley Flour" to 1 lb. of the mixture of ordinary flour and mashed potatoes, mix these well together, then proceed in the usual way, using no other raising agent.</p>
