      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>GETTING INFAMATION OUT OF PA.</head>
        <p>GETTING INFAMATION OUT OF PA.</p>
        <p>My pa, he didn't go down town, . • it evening after tea, Rat got a book an' settled down As comf'y as could be. I tell you I • was awful glad. To have my pa about To answer all the things I had Be,m trying to find. out. And so I asked him why the world Is round instead of square? And why the piggies' tails are curled? And. why, don't fish breathe air? And why the moon don't hit a star? And why the dark is black And just how many birds ,there are And. will the wind come back? And why does water stay in wells ? And why do June bugs hum? And what's the roar I hear in shells? And when will Christmas come? And why the grass is always green, Instead of -sometimes blue? And why a bean will grow a bean, And not an apple, too? And why a horse can't learn to moo? And why a cow ,can't neigh? • And why do fairies live on• dew? - And what makes hair grow gray? • And then my pa got up, an', Gee, " The awful words he said! I hadn't done a thing, but he Jut seat me off to bid. OLIVE M. KINGDON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>as much as to say: "What arc yuL., for, and whatever do you exp.,</head>
        <p>as much as to say: "What arc yuL., for, and whatever do you exp.,</p>
        <p>I‘, (_lO</p>
        <p>After a while the king and anxious about their little daufzlitc,r and pcnt much time in the Royal nun.2ry amuse the little princess. Tae got "run down." It was all to could make the' little girl hiu school-days began, her parent</p>
        <p>C</p>
        <p>and thing-s to try and amuse her. : were in vain. At last her school-;1,.,s and it was getting time she married. But the King said, "Who is going to marry a girl who cannot sthile?" so he had papers posted up everywhere in the town, which bore this inserip- tion:—" Whoever can make the ,princess smile may take her for his wife," but no one ever answered this inscription. One day, while the princess was talking alone in the woods, Fhe fell and sprained her ankle so badly that she could not walk home. All .-at once a young gentleman came along the road and .askd the princess what was wrong. When he heard of the accident, he said, "I must 'carry you home." The princess was very grateful, and thought the young man would make her a nice husbands. When they arrived at the palace the King and Queen were very grateful, and all at once the princess smiled. Mc:a came the happy ending.</p>
        <p>P. L:AV rs.</p>
        <p>.-...-..4-..</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am you. • for the lovely box of chccnlaft f ire for the contribution to the "Child:r(:l': C-'l'•'eT." am writing you a comic called:—</p>
        <p>A GOLLIWOGS ROMANCE</p>
        <p>A Golliwog once met a Doll, , Who would have cut him cokl'y; But, smitten by her yrJi.ty He bowed and spoke out biJy— Audacious .Golliwog, to stand And speak to her so boldly! "0, spurn me be your slave; To you devoted ever. Just watch me do the cake-walk, pray, Although I'm plain—l'm clever!" (He .cake-walked very well, indeed; He was distinctly clever!) Then, just to show the -pretty doll His limbs were so elastic, He hummed a rag-time tune, and tripped Upon the light fantastic— ('Twas cfuite a treat to see him trip Upon the light fantastic). No doubt the Golliwog was plain— As plain as any Gorgon— But, oh! to see him with a atraw • Propped on his nasal organ (There's art in balancing a straw Upon one's nasal organ!) When Dolly saw him, after that, • At lightning "cart-wheels " spinning, She found she could no more resist His tricks, they were so winning--- (Who can resist a Golliwog Whose tricks are aU so winning?)• So when be said, "Come for a stroll— That is, if you're at leisure?" Most affably she took his arm, to And murmured, "Yes, with pleasure." (He was a clever Golliwog, That's why she said, "With pleasure I ")</p>
        <p>+</p>
        <p>"A CONTRIBUTION FOR CHRISTMAS.!'</p>
        <p>A play acted by the children of a family would, no doubt, succeed in entertaining an audience for an evening at Christmas. A suit- able play should he chosen, so as to afford pleasure for the little ones of the family. Per- haps if the character of "Santa Claus" could be in the play, the result would be successful. If a small family should intend hiving a play, other friends could act.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a piece of poetry, which is called—</p>
        <p>WHEN CHRISTMAS COMES</p>
        <p>When Christmas comes, with sugar plums, And all the fun and noise, We'll wake to find our stockings lined With oranges and toys! And in the snow outdoors we'll go. Or sit with Sis while chestnuts hiss, And pull the hottest out!</p>
        <p>When Christmas comes and maps, and sums, And pens, are put 'Luray— The dinner o'er we'll clear the floor, And make a space to, play; And then what glee for us there'll be When Aunt and Uncle bring Jim, Bob, and Kate to stay quite late, And with us romp and sing!</p>
        <p>When Christmas comes, with horn and drums We'll march upstairs and down, And Christmas night with-all delight meriy morn shall crown— But if next day Mamma should say, "You've made me work to do," 0 With brush i and pan to help we'll plan And sweep the whole house through! EDITH ASTLB.</p>
