        <p>A PROFITABLE OAT CROP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Parke's manurial experiments on the growth of oats at Kincton, in Warwickshire, have been carried out for another year with the co-operation of Dr. Dyer. The oats followed beans, and were a good crop, the favourable effects of the season being such that even the portion of the field left unmanured yielded 42 bushels of oats and nearly a ton of straw per acre. A dressing of 3 cwt. of superphosphate per acre raised the yield to 55 bushels of oats, with 24cwt. of straw. When the superphosphate was followed by a top dressing of 1 cwt. of nitrate of soda, applied after the plant was up, the crop was' 67 bushels of oats and 1.? tons of straw • while a second hundredweight of nitrate, put. on a few weeks later, raised the yield to 74 bushels of oats, with over 2 tons of straw, per acre. On these two plots the manuring cost, roughly, 20s. and 325. per acre respectively, while the returns latained in increase of crop were over three quartets uFbats, with over one ton straw, per acre in the other. The man- gold manuring experiments this • year were spoiled by the irregularity of the crop, owing to failure of the young plant thrpugh drought.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAX'S MATCHSS,</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Wigan Oldham 1 3 11 Hunslet ...... -.... 3 1 .9 Hull K. R. Keighley Warrington Barrow • Warrington Win at. Bradfurel.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men accomplished a fine performance at Bradford on Saturday, when tbey recorded their first win in Yorkshire this season. There was only a small gate of 2,000, the big Association fixture between Brad- ford City and Leeds City attracting 18,000 people. Bradford were poorly represented, both the regular halves being absent. Warrington had a strong team, including Tilley late of Widnes, who played on the wing. Brooks was absent, Jordan filling the vacancy. Teams and officials :</p>
        <p>Warrington : Berry, back ; Tilley, Isherwood, Dickinson and Jordan, three-quarter backs; Jenkins and S. Lees, half backs; Boar c man, Preston, Heath, Harmer, Thomas, and Naylor, forwards. Bradford.---Grum, back; Dunbavin, Davison, Connell, and Dechan, three-quarter backs ; Wilson and Bartle, half backs; Greenwood, Smales, Feather, Mann, Walton, and Wilman, forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, 'Air. W. M'Cutcheo.n, Oldham. Touch judges, Mr. G. Holdsworth, Halifax, and Mr. C. W. Gibson, Kei.ghley.</p>
        <p>Wirriitgton were soon attacking after the kick-off as a consequence of mulling. Dickinson took a smart pass from Lees and got over, and Preston kicked a goal. Greenwood 'and Mann led a dribble to the other end of the field, but they were unsupported, and the ball was soon back again to near the Bradford line. Isherwood was stopped in an effort to get through, and Jordan making his mark, Preston landed a goal. Once more Bradford got into their opponents' qua:Ors, and Jordan to stop the progress of a forward rush, kicked over his own goal line. From the succeeding scrummage, how- ever, the ball was_sent over, and Smales scored a try, but Gunn failed at goal. At half-time the position was :—Warrington 2 goals I try (7 points), to Bladford 1 try (3 points.) On resuming Warring- ton were soon once more on the attack, and Tilley dropped at goal. The ball went very wide, but right into the midst of the forwards who had practically a clear field for a try, which was scored by Naylor. Preston missed the goal. The next incident of note was a splendid run by Connell from his own "25 "to the visitors' 23." Dechan was in attendance on him all 'the way, and between them they had Berry, the Warrington full back, beaten. When Connell made the pass, however, it was of a bad character, and Dechan was not able to gather the ball, thus was a certain try thrown away. Not long after, as the result of a scramble on the Warrington goal line, Mann scored, but Greenwood failed at an easy goal. The ball now travelled freely from one end of the field to the other, and as the result of good passing Jenkins added a try for Warring- ton, and Preston kicked the goal. Then Dunbavin scored a try, the result of a smart individual effort, and Gunn kicked the goal. This finished the scoring, and the final read :—Warrington 3 goals 3 tries (15 points). to Bradford 1 goal 3 tries (11 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>NEW YEAR'S DAY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>NEW YEAR'S DAY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P, G. T. P. Wigan Warrington Hunslet 4 1 31 Salford Halifax Swinton 2 4 16 Huddersfield ... -1 1 5 Oldham 1 4 14 Barron' 1 1 5 St. Helens - 0 0 Leigh</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Warriligton Defeat Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Warriligton Defeat Runcorn.</p>
        <p>A Great Struggle at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The New Year's Day match was certainly one of the most interesting bf Warrington's programme for not only have Runcorn been making the running in the League, but they are Warrington's closest rivals for the South West Lancashire Competition, in which the match also counted. Unfortunately the weather was of the vilest description. The ground had been cleared of snow, but rain set in and soon reduced the sodden turf to a sticky morass. Nevertheless the crowd turned out nobly, and there would be quite 7,000 spectators of a most exciting match. Runcorn were at full strength, but Warrington were badly handicapped by the absence of Isherwood in addition to Fi4h,Taylor, and Hockenhall. Cartwright had to fill the vacancy at centre, after playing in the " A " team match in the morning. Teams and officials : Warrington : Berry, back ; Tilley, Cartwright, Dickenson, and Brooks, three-quarters; Lees and Jenkins, half backs ; Preston Thomas, Harmer, Heath, Naylor nnrl Boardman, foram:di._ . .</p>
        <p>Runcorn : Harrison. back; Deakin Butterworth, Moran, and Flannagan,threequarters;Joleey and Watton, half-backs; Richardson, Padbury, Williams, Leyland, Walkei, and Dixon, forwards,</p>
        <p>Refeiee, Mr.A. Sznith,Halifax. Touch-judges, Messrs. J. Hayes (Leigh), and W. Cross (Preston). Preston started the game and Warrington were the first to become agressive, the forwards rushing play to the Runcorn quarters in vigorous fashion. Harrison and Jolley cleared with good punting!, ha Warrington were persistent and Cartwright got away on the left with the ball at his toes. Butterworth tried to shoulder him off, but the Warrington man was the stronger and took the ball over at the corner live minutes from the start amid a perfect storm of cheering. Preston failed at goal. Runcorn rallied in determined fashion and attempted the passing game.Flannagan was put in posses- sion but did not get past Brooks. The Linnets were. awarded a penalty in good position, but Richardson I failed at goal. Lees changed the scene with a beautiful break away down the centre and in the rush which followed the Runcqrn line had a very narrow escape, Harrison just sending. a skidding ball into touch. At once almost the game travelled to the other end, Walker, Richardson and Padbury excelling in a dribble which beat Berry and would have resulted in a score but for Brooks' arrival on the scene in the nick of time. Warrington were again penalised, and again Richardson failed. The Warrington forwards finally cleared out the invaders and Naylor led a dribble which Runcorn were powerless to stop.Butterworth attempted a fly kick to touch but missed,and Dickenson coming up with a rush scored far out.Breston again found the angle too wide and the ball too wet for goal-kicking. Smart footwork by Runcorn drove Warrington back, Berry saving grandly. While attacking, the Linnets got their usual penalty and this time Jolley kicked a goal. Thomas was the next to become conspicuous for he dashed over under the Runcorn goal but was ruled off- side. Warrington were determined in their work and I kept play in the Runcorn quarter. Dickenson replied to a punt by Harrison in masterly fashion. He put in a short kick, followed up speedily, and snapping the ball under Jolley's nose raced clean away and over the line with Warrington's third try. The cheering was great, but Preston could not land a goal. Half-time: War- ringion, 3 tries (9 points) ; Runcorn, 1 goal (2 points). On resuming Runcorn seemed certain to score and nothing but Lees coolness saved Warrington. Jolley marked and kicked a second goal. This seemed to inspire the Linnets who overplayed Warrington at this stage. They again went in for passing andsome smart work by Jolley. Butterworth and Deakin was only stopped on the line, where Berry upset the wing man. Warrington were unable to clear the line and in a second the ball was sent across to the other side where Flannagan took his pass beautifully and rushed over at the corner. Jolley failed at goal, but the Runcorn supporters were now on good terms and encouraged the Linnets with repeated cheers. NVarringtort seemed unable to rise to the occasion though they once got perilously near the line when Richardson marked. Butter- worth ran smartly and put his side on ibe offensiveagain. Tilley had a kick charged and the home team's escape was lucky. Still Runcorn persisted and Deakin made a fast dribble on the right wing which sent the ball over the line. Here his forwards dropped on it, but were clearly offside and the home crowd breathed again. Things were most exciting, the ball being forced past Berry and over the line once more, Harmer sending dead in the nick of time. Then came another penalty and Jolley equalised the scores with a fine kick from near touch. The odds looked greatly in favour of the Linnets at this point. Padbury got away down touch and over the line where Brooks bundled him out of play. Had he passed the Linnets must have scored. Warring- ton now awoke to the seriousness of tie position and a desperate rmh took play to the Runcorn end. Here, the Linnets were penalised, but Tilley made a hash of an easy shot. Warrington were irresistible at this stage and Drools dribbled over, only to have the ball kicked clean from under him by ‘Vatton. Some strenuous work ensued on the line. The Warrington forwards got possession, Less handled and with Dickenson close up he passed. The lengthy centre crashed over despite Jolley's tackle and ‘Varrington were leading again. What a cheer there was—a cheer redoubled when Preston atoned for previous failures with a fine goal. It transpired that Jolley was hurt in trying to stop Dickerson and left the field. Only a minute or two , remained during which Warrington had the best of ' matters. Final : Warrington 1 goal 4 tries (14 points). Runcorn 3 goals 1 try (9 points.)</p>
        <p>RUNCORN " A " v. WARRINGTON "A." (Lancashire Rugby Combination). At Runcorn. 'rho opening stages were fairly even, and then Knight made two good runs after fine passing, but was unable to get through. Runcorn then attacked, but weie easily repulsed. Kay kicked over till Runcorn line, and Cartwright scored a try, although Riley had plenty of time to kick dead. Cartwright missed the goal. Half-time : Warrington, 1 try ; Runcorn, nil. Restarting, Warrington quickly increased their lead. Hewitt scored a second try and Cartwright landed a fine goal. Runcorn now played up better, and forced Ashton to touch down, but afterwards did little else but</p>
