        <p>kick. Warrington missed a.penalty goal in front of the posts. Runcorn pressed strongly, and Shaw crossed, and Riley kicked a goal. Final result: Wallington " A," 1 goal 2 tries ; Runcorn " A," 1 goal 1 try.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS</p>
        <p>READING BY LAMPLIGHT UNDER WATER</p>
        <p>- An electric are lamp, capable of efficiently sup. plying the needs of under-water illumination fot ship salvage and other similar, work, as well as for operations in expfth,ive atmospheres, has been tried in the harbour of Aberdeen in connection with repairs affected on the dock gates there, without taking them out of position. It Was found that. a thorough examination of dock gates could. be made by the aid of the lamp, and in order to demonstrate its power one of the divers returned for a nes.vspaper, and aftez descending to the bottom of the dock, a depth of 20ft., read a' portion of it through the tele- phone to thoseoon the surface.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>AN ARTIFICIAL VEGETABLE,</head>
        <p>AN ARTIFICIAL VEGETABLE,</p>
        <p>Dr. Stephano Leduc, of Nantes, has created an artificial vegetable. The " seed " is a minute pellet of about one millimkre in diameter, con. sisting of one part sulphate of copper and two parts glucose. lie " tests "itin a solution of gelatine, containing 3 per cent. ferrocyanide of potassium; and a small quantity of marine salts. The " plant " grows rapidly in a -glass tube, says the Paris correspondent of the Daily Telegraph, and resembles •seaweed. Some of the specimens- thus created measure 18in. long. The artificial plants behave exactly like natural plants under the influence of heat, light, chloroform, &amp;c. They turn towards sunshine as do many real plants. But, of course, they cannot Propagate themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>THERMIT .IMPROVEMENT</head>
        <p>THERMIT .IMPROVEMENT</p>
        <p>Dr. Hans Goldschmidt, of Essen-on-the4lul)r, Germany, publishes an improvement in the well- known thermit •compound. Be has found by ex- tensive experiments that a mixture of calcium and aluminium, or an alloy of calcium and aluminium, when mixed in a finely-powdered or granulated state, with a metal compound, also produces a high thermic effect. He has also found that a good thermit mixture is produced by a mixture of calcium and aluminium or of an alloy of both and of iron oxide, or of iron oxide protoxide. He has found that, if these corn- eounds are .intimately mixed together, the ther- mic effect is greater than with - the usual and well-known thermit mixture, and more than double the quantity of metallic iron (i.e., two- thirds of the total weight of the mixture) can be added, the slag being liquid as well as the iron.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>MANUFACTURE OF IRON IN CHINA.</head>
        <p>MANUFACTURE OF IRON IN CHINA.</p>
        <p>Iron in China is made by mixing four parts of the ore, one part of decomposed coal,dust, and one part of small coal. The mixture is• placed in crucibles each about 18in. deep and 6Ain. in dia- meter. The crucibles are heated in a furnace having walls about 3ft. high, and a floor 4ft. by 6Aft., which is covered with clay and spread with a layer of coarse coal to a height of 7in. or Bin. above the clay. The furnace holds about sixty of these crucibles. The space between them is filled with small coal, and on top is placed a 3in. layer of small coal, followed by a layer of cinders and ashes of the same depth. About sixteen hours of strong heat suffices to convert the mixture into a mass ,of carbon iron, says the Iron Age. This is made into wrought iron by reheating over a wood fire and by ham- inering it when red hot.</p>
        <p>PICTURES OF LIVING PLANTS In tll.O</p>
        <p>Science Notes" in Chambers's Jour.</p>
        <p>vial, we are told of an experiment which is being made in America ~to epitomise the life-history of a plant within Abe duration of an animated photograph. The writer thus explains how it is done: "In the making of an ordinary animated picture a. large number of separate and distinct photographs as e taken consecutively on a travel- ling band of celluloid, at the rate of some six- teen every second, and in the reproduction of the picture the separate images are thrown upon a lantern-screen in the same, order and at the same rate of speed. It will be remembered that the blending of these images in the spectator's eyes, which are incapable of perceiving them ,separately at such a speed, results in the produc- tion of the illusion of a single picture instinct with life and. motion. In photographing the life history of a-growing plant the separate pictures are taken at a comparatively long interval of time—about one picture an hour—so that\ the complete exposure will' embrace the appearance of the first tiny shoot above the soil, and the en- tire growth of the plant until it flowers and seeds and withers. A few Inindred photographic exposures will cover the entire cycle, and pro- duce a film which, projected at the ordinary rate will occupy only a few minutes of the student's time. In these few minutes he will observe the whole story of its life compressed into two or three minutes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ANOTHER. ELECTRIC FURNACE MATERIAL.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER. ELECTRIC FURNACE MATERIAL.</p>
        <p>Another new material has been evolved from the electric furnace, and has received the riiame of " alundum " from its discoverers. The sub- stance, though not of first importance. seems likely, says the Timcs, to occupy a useful posi- tion in the \industrial arts, and affords another illustration of the potentialities of the appliance which M. Moissan and also Dr. Borchers have done so much to bring into use as a medium for high temperature research. The manufacture of alundum, introduced by a firm at Worcester, Massachusetts, appears simplicity itself. It con- sists in using bauxite (the 'raw material employed in the production of aluminium) in a purified form, and in melting this- highly refractory 'sub- stance in the electric furnace in a homogeneous bath. Upon cooling, the molten material crystallises into solid masses, which are after- wards. broken up into small pieces by powerful crushers, sifted and graded. The material thus produced is stated to be but slightly inferior to the diamond in hardness and abrasive qualities, athile its chemical composition resembles that of corundum. It is asserted to be considerably harder than the natural product. At the Worcester works eleven furnaces have been in- stalled, each with a productive capacity of three tons of the material per twenty-four hours. The temperature at which the furnace charge melts into a homogeneous mass is estimated at from 6,Cakleg. to 7,ooodeg. Fahr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>BIRDS THAT CROSS THE ATLANTIC,</head>
        <p>BIRDS THAT CROSS THE ATLANTIC,</p>
        <p>The occurrence of Americat birds in this country raises the. interesting question—How do they cross the intervening :2,000 miles of ocean? Can we suppose a bird is 'capable of sustained flight for a sufficiently long period to accom- plish this? As regards the rate of flight at. tamed by birds in their migratory journeys some interesting computations are to be found in Gatke's "Heligoland as Mt Ornithological Ob- servatory." His figures are 125 miles an hour for the hooded crow. 208 for the northereblue- throat, and 245 for the Virginian plover. At the latter rate the Atlantic could be crossed in about eight hours and three-quarters. Some six other American birds, including the red-crested snipe and the Esquimau curlew, are recorded from the Scilly Islands. . Other American birds, in- cluding the yellow-billed- cuckoo, have been re- corded in Ireland. And in connection with this crossing of the Atlactitic by American birds, it is interesting to note an apparent attempt to cross in the, opposite direction. In a British Associa- tion report for 1887 we read: "At Rathlin O'Birne (West Donegal) immense flocks of birds —starlings, thrushes, and fieldfares—passed west from December 15th to 23rd. The nearest land tio the west of this rocky island is America. This ;s not an isolated occurrence. The westerly flight of land birds at stations off the west coast of Ire- land has been noticed on other occasions." It would be interesting to know if any of these enterprising migrants ever, resell the other side.</p>
        <p>THE BIRTH OF A WONDERFUL STAR. A message from Mani,' which at cannon-ball speed would take 300 years to reach the earth, would come in five minutes on rays of light. Yet, says Science Siftings, a message by this light-wave telegraphy, was started from the con- stellation Perseus somewhere about 300 years ago, and it 'flashed along day and night at 186,000 miles per second while new nations were being born and while they were slowly gaining a foothold, while they grew into independent nations, While the nations passed through great struggles, and it was still approaching 16.000,000,000 miles everS, day when the Boer War was befit.. fought out. Then, on February 21st, -1001, the signal was seen; The apparition was named Nova Persei by the startled astronomers: it proved to be the brightest of all recorded temporary stars except one; its fluctuations were greatest of all, and in some respects it was the most wonderful star. Its maximum brightness was reached on February 0/1".1, 1 : + L.l itn (WO • +;TVIP.,</p>
      </div>
