      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>°LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>°LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE ro CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Mr. Parker and Non-Trading. Sir,—May I be permitted to say "I told you so." Mr. Parker has shed the effulgence of his intelligence on this simple question, which everyone in Warrington (except Mr. Parker) has understood for years, and we I know just as much about it as we did before he came upon the scene. The oracle has now closed its mouth ; the last word is spoken. For this relief, much thanks. Mr. Parker appealed to the public to support him. The public responds not ! I rejoice in the good sense of the public and am now content to join the silent throng.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>LIBRA.</p>
        <p>Warrington, January 4th, 1907. The Beamont School Appointment. Sir,—Your correspondont, Charles Turner, "a War- rington teacher and a native" charges me with having stated at the recent meeting of the Education Com- mittee anent the above matter "that none of these men (the 17 local applicants) are capable of conducting a school with the requisite skill as teachers and organisers." It is hardly necessary for me to deny that I made such a foolish statement, or one that could possibly bear any such construction, and it would be better if Mr. Turner (before he again ventures to make such charges against me) would carefully read what I really have said, and then try to understand the meaning of the simple language in which I generally express myself.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>BENRY ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>Poor Children's Christmas Treat. Would you kindly allow me through the medium of your valuable columns to explain- why it is proposed not to continue this Treat at the present season ? The Committee were of opinion that having regard to the work being done by the Committee of the Children's Camp, the funds available for the Treat, if diverted in that direction would produce a much higher result on the expenditure. When it is borne in mind that (a) the Camp is now established on a permanent basis (b) that during last season the number of children who enjoyed a week's holiday at the seaside exceeded 500 (c) the facilities afforded for poor persons to enjoy a holiday at very little expense, I think the public will appreciate the reason for endeavouring to divert the cost of the Children's Treat into the other channel. My reason for making this communication and explanation is that subscribers have been making inquiries as to when and where the Treat is to be' held. Funds are needed for the Camp Scheme, and those who subscribed to the Treat, and are desirous of help- ing the Camp, may send donations or subscriptions either to Parr's Bank ; the Hon. Treasnrer, Mr. A. H. Lee, Tapestry Works, Warrington ' • or to W. H. J. JENKINS. Hon. Sec. Bank Chambers, Warrington. January 4th, 1000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OLD FOLK'S TREAT. ENJOYABLE GATHERINGS AT THE</head>
        <p>OLD FOLK'S TREAT. ENJOYABLE GATHERINGS AT THE</p>
        <p>PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>It is always a delight to see the aged people, enjoying themselves and those who were responsible for the arrangement of the annual treat to the old folk which was held at the Parr Hall on Thursday, must have been gratified to see how highly appreciated their efforts were. For many years these happy gatherings have been amongst the most eagerly looked-forward-to of the festive season, and the generosity of those towns- people who subscribe to the fund could not be better applied. A cheering scene presented itself to anyone who chanced to visit the Parr Hall at noon on Thursday. Nearly 500 men aged sixty years and upwards—the oldest xvasninety-one—were seated around the festive board, and ladies and gentlemen were busily engaged attending to their wants.lt was an interesting assembly,comprising many different types of old men. While some were bent and infirm, there were many with whom Father Time has dealt more kindlj, and they looked quite hale and hearty. All appeared to thoroughly enjoy the good old English fare of beef, mutton and vegetables, followed by plum pudding. Those who desired it were supplied with beer, and tobacco was also distributed. A large band of workers was in attendance to wait upon the guests, the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), who is chairman of the committee, being amongst the most active. The Mayoress, and the ex-Mayoress (Mrs. Bolton) and the Mayor's chaplain (Rev. W. Brac,ecamp) also assisted. The committee which had had the arrangements in hand consisted of the following : President, the Mayor • Vice-President, Mr. T. Shackle- ton ; Hon. Vice-President, Mr. Peter Caldwell ; Hon. Secretary, Mr. T. A. Sutton ; Hon. Treasurer,Councillor J. J. Nield ; Alderman Forshavv, Conncillor Burton, Captain A. F. G. Potter, Messrs. C. E. Bunch,T. Locker, W. Rogers, Thomas Bowers,S. Davies,Thomas Atherton. Alfred Jolley and W. Webster. Special mention should be made of the services of Mr. T. A. Sutton who worked most assiduously as secretary to ensure the success of the treat. Dinner over,there was a little speech-mailing. The Mayor presided and was supported on the platform by members of the committee. The MAYOR heartily wished all present a happy and prosperous new year and expressed the great pleasure it afforded him to see such a large attendance of old men. It was additionally gratifying to observe that they all appeared so clean and respectable. As far as he could remember, that was the largest gathering of men they had entertained during his connection with the committee. There were at least 460 present. (Applause.) He did not believe there was a more deserving institution in the town and he felt sure that if many of the subscribers could see that ussembly they would double their subscriptions. (Applause.) He was, however, pleased to say that such generous support had been forthcoming that they were inn good financial position, people having given most willingly. It was twenty-seven years that day since he joined the committee and during that period many changes had taken place. He believed that not a single gentleman who was a member of the committee when he was elected to it,was alive to-day. The institution had. been in existence over forty years and during that time three or four Mayors had occupied the presidential chair He hoped that with the support of the townspeople he would be able to discharge the Mayoral duties in a manner that would give satisfaction. In com- memoration of his election to the Mayoralty he had decided to present each guest with a photograph of himself. .(Applause.) His Worship then thanked the committee and officials for their services and made feeling allusion to the death of two valuable members—Mr. Sampson and Mr. Legge. Referring to the provision made for that day's treat he said the food included :—Beef, and ham, 9501b5. bread 6501b5. ; vegetables, plum pudding, cakes, &amp;c., 1,1901b5. ; total 2,7901b5. Mr. ISAAC JOHNSON, one of the guests, tendered the sincere thanks of the old men to all who had helped to provide the treat, He cordially congratulated the .President on the high honour conferred upon him by the Council, and hoped that he and the Mayoress would be blessed with health and strength to carry out the duties, so that the coming year would be one of the happiest of their lives. (Applause). The guests signified their approval of these sentiments by cheering loudly, and the MAYOR briefly responded. The dinner bad been cooked by Mr. Toft, and dr. and Mrs. Hare assisted in serving it. During the dinner Mr. W. B. Randall played selections on the organ. AGED WOMEN ENTERTAINED. In the evening it was the turn of the aged women of the town to participate in the bounty of the Committee and right royally were they entertained. Tea was pro- vided for them in the body of the hall, and over five hundred sat down to an inviting repast. There was an abundance of good things, and tho provisions, beef and ham sandwiches and cake, quickly disappeared. The ages of those invited to the feast ranged from GO to 90, quite a number who had exceeded the latter ripe age being included in the guests. After tea oranges Were distributed, and the aged dames became the proud possessors of photographs of his Worship the Mayor. Then followed a miscellaneous programme submitted by the Merrions. These versatile artiste are well-know in Warrington, and a crowded attendance on the part of the general public showed how they were appreciated. Humorists of the first water, their efforts were greeted with shrieks of laughter from the old women and repeated rounds of applause, in fact the entertainment will live long in the memories of the guests as the best ever witnessed at similar functions. Prior to the entertainment the Mayor wished the old ladies a Happy New Year. Votes of thanks were accorded to the Committee and the ladies who had helped at the tables. Councillor J. J. NIELD proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor for presiding, a vote which was accorded with great heartiness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Assessment Committee.</head>
        <p>Assessment Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Union- Assessment Committee was held at the Workhouse yesterda) morning under the presidency of Mr. A. J. Jolley. There were also present : —Messrs. J. Billings, S. Davies, G. Mather, IL It. Meacock (valuer), H. Lawrence (for Mr. W. H. Woodock, assistant overseer for Warring ton), J. Fairclough (assistant overseer for Burtonwood) and J. C. Sutton (clerk)—Mr. William Houghton appealed against the assessment of the Chapel House Inn, Burtonwood, which had been raised to ;C5B gross and £5O ratable—The Committee declined to reduce the assessment—Mr. H. W. Guest asked for a reduc- tion of the Assessment of Guest's Tannery, Winwick- street, which now atands at £360 gross and £2BB rateable. He pointed out that the concern had been formed into a limited company and the premises were now rented from Mr. E.J. Guest at £3OO per annum. —The mattrr was referred to the valuer to report upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES. ---- [By Our Special Correspondent.]</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES. ---- [By Our Special Correspondent.]</p>
        <p>THE CLOSING YEAR.</p>
        <p>Among the Free Churches the year that is now past shows, on the whole, satisfactory progiess. Never, I suppose, have the Free Churches been in so strong a position in this country as they are at present. The spirit of unity among the different denominations is mare and more prevalent, and there are signs of increased success in the social and spiritual work undertaken by the churches. Social Problems are being better understood, and the duty of the Church in relation to the outcast and needy is being more and more realised. Then, too, there is a tendency to lay greater stress on the duty of the Church in foreign fields, and many predict a speedy revival in mission- ary enthusiasm. That there is abundant room for this, all who knew the facts would acknowledge. FORTHCOMING EVENTS. Looking forward, 1907 seems to promise many important and interesting events. Among these, Free Churchmen are especially looking forward to the forth- coming meetings of the National Free Church Council to be held from March 4th to 7th at Leeds. I have obtained a draft programme of these gatherings, and from it I should say that considerable variety will mark the occasion. The Coliseum, one of the largest buildings in Leeds, has been specially engaged, as well as a number of other halls and churches. Dr. Rendel Harris, the well-known Quaker, is to be President, and among the preachers are to be :—Revs. Charles Brown, J. H. Jowett, J. Scott Lidgett, M.A., W. L. Watkinson, Eynon T. Davies, Trevor H. Davies, J. Monro Gibson, M.A., L.L.D , J. G. Greenhough, M.A., A. T. Guttery, and J. E. Rattenbury. One item should create considerable interest, the paper by Sir Oliver Lodge on " The Problem of Science and Religion." AN INTERESTING LIST. Other interesting items include :—" The Holy Spirit in Scripture and Experience," by Rev. R. F. Horton, M.A., D.D. ; "The Old Testament in the Religious Life of To-day," by Professor A. S. Peake, M.A. ; "The Public Ministry of Women,' by Mrs. Bendel Harris : and " The Ritual Commission and the Duty of the Free Churches," by Dr. W. Robertson Nicoll, M.A. It is hoped that Dr. Griffiths John will be present to speak on " Foreign Missionary Work." Amongst the public questions to be dealt with are a, National Campaign on Sun-ay Observance, Temper- ance Policy, the Congo Question—to be introduced by Rev. John Harris and Mr. E. D. Morel—" Welsh Dis- establishment," to be introduced by Sir Alfred Thomas, M.P., and Rev. J. Glyn Davies. EDUCATION BILL'S FATE. In some quarters the decision of the House of Lords to kill the Education Bill was received vrithout surprise, though it must be admitted that right up to the last no one knew *hat was going to happen. Opinion in Free Church circles is,l find,not altogether one of regret for the fate of the Bill, though, of course, anyone at all interested in the matter cannot but regret the wastage of the work of the whole session of the House of Commons. Still, it must be confessed that from the very first there wereprovisions in the Bill which Free Churchmen regarded with great dislike. From the very time of the introduc- tion of the measure it was felt that the Government had given the most generous terms possible to the Church of England. When, after the extraordinary proceedings in the House of Lords, the Government made their further proposals, these concessions were regarded with feelings of great uneasiness by Non- conformists, and it is doubtful whether all the Free Church members in the House could have supported the amendments. But this question is no longer at stake. The House of Lords has defied the Govern- ment, and the Bill is dead. THE POSITION NOW. One thing now is certain, that never again will the Church of England be offered such generous terms as were .embodied in the Government's proposal. Surely the height of folly was i eached when such an admirable chance of settling the great difficulty was refused. Now it means that the war must continue, and it is to be feared with increased bitterness. Of course. there can be no question of the Lords rejection of the' Bill .being regarded by Free Churchmen as a settle- ment of the case. It is important to remember that the General Election last January was fought largely on the Education Question, and the Government received the clearest possible mandate for legislating to insure Natlonal Education under Popular Control and the abolition of Tests for Teachers. If this Mandate cannot be carried out in one way the Govern- ment must attempt it in another. In this they will have the united support of the whole of Noncon- formity.</p>
        <p>GIPSY SMITH IN AMERICA. I have received most glowing accounts of the campaign conducted by Gipsy Smith, the National Free Church Council's Missioner, in Boston, U.S.A. The American papers are full of the wonderful revival which has set in, the ouly comparison with the state of affairs now existing being that of the Moody Revival in the seventies. Tremont Temple, one of the largest halls in Boston, was packed each night, long before the advertised time of meeting, and two other halls had to be engaged to hold overflow meetings. There were in all 45 week-day meetings, with an attendance of 100,000, four Sunday meetings attended by 12,000, and a big Children's meeting at which 3,500 were present. About three thousand people during the mission have expressed a desire to follow Christ No less than 900 cards were signed at the Children's Meeting</p>
        <p>UNIVERSAL APPRECIATION. Of course, such figures are almost incredible, but as a leading A.n;lerican minister says, Gipsy Smith is one of the few Evangelists who possesses the confi- dence of all the Churches, and after the remarkable mission tour which he conducted recently through South Africa nothing seems too great for the Gipsy Evangelist to accomplish.. At the close of the mission a testimonial of appreciation, signed by 200 ministers, was presented to Gipsy Smith, together with a hand- some silver loving-cup. The scene at the presentation was most dramatic, the Evangelist being surrounded by a group of representative ministers and the vast audience rising to sing, " Blest be the tie that binds." Gipsy Smith has now gone to Portland, Maine, where great preparations have been made for his mission.</p>
        <p>GIRLS' GUILD GROWTH. Some remarkable figures were presented to the General Committee of the National Free Church Council recently, showing the growth of the Girls' Guild, an organisation in connection with the National Council for assisting Free Church girls. There are now 400 branches and 20,000 members. Fifteen Permanent Homes in provincial centres are now open for the use of girls. In many of these homes pro- vision is made for the reception of girls who may be without a home, whilst classes for various subjects are a feature of the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 5, 1907.</p>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL -- -- Business of Tuesday's Meeting</p>
        <p>The adjourned monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening at 6 o'clock when the following, among other business will be transacted. Appointment of a co-optative member on the Educa- tion Committee to fill the vacancy occasioned by the election of Mr. T. S. Steel as a member of the Council. Appointment of a Representative Manager of the Heath Side National School in place of the late Mr. Councillor Brough.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRESS COMMITTEE. Councillor Toole will move that in order to assist the efforts of the Distress Committee, an instruction be given to the head officials of the various departments to notify the Inquiry Officer at the Labour Bureau when requiring workmen. WATCH COMMITTEE. Resolved that Mr. Councillor Evans be elected a member of this Committee in the place of the late Mr. Conncillor Brough. A letter was read from the National gociety for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, asking for a contri- bution to the funds of the Society. The Town Clerk advised that a contribution could not legally be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>VICTORIA PARK.</head>
        <p>VICTORIA PARK.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee considered an applica- tion from the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee to be entrusted with the manage- ment and control of Victoria Park. It was resolved, that Victoria Park be placed under the management and control of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, subject to the following conditions :- 1. The land usually let with the Old Warps to bo reserved. 2. That the land shall be used as a park only. 3. That such portions of the land as may here- after be required as an approach to or for the purposes of a new bridge across the river at Howley be given up when required. STREET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING. THE EDELSTEN ACTION. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Thornton having objected to a lamp fixed to the Ebenezer Church in Buttermarket-street, this committee resolved, that the lamp be .emoved. Resolved, that Mr. Alderman Forshaw be elected a member of this committee in the place of the late Mr. Councillor Brough. Orders were made for payment of accounts amounting to £466 123. 11d, including the sum of £403 is. 3d. being the amount of the plaintiff's taxed costs in the action of Edelsten v. the Corporation and William Bolton. _ _ .</p>
        <p>A deputation from the 'lnfirmary Extension Com- mittee, consisting of the Chairman (Mr. W. P. Rylands), the Rev. H. V. Pigot, and Mr. George Dakin, together with Mr. Segar Owen, waited upon the committee with respect to the purchase by the Corporation of the land given up to the street by the setting back of the Infirmary extension. Resolved, that the Council be recommended to pay the sum of £lOO for the land and all rights and interests therein. A letter was read from the Warrington Tradesmen's Association calling attention to the. "unsatisfactory lighting of some of the pri.cipal thoroughfares "and alleging that it was a cause of loitering and much, damage being done to shop fronts, &amp;c. Resolvedf That the Association be informed that the subject od improved lighting of the principal thoroughfares hae been under consideration for some time and tests wer now being made, and that the tradesmen might protec their shop fronts from damage by fixing suitable iron guards as had been done in many parts of the town. The Committee considered at considerable • length a statement prepared by the Town Clerk with regard to the negotiations which had taken place between the Executors of the late Mr. Joseph Shaw, their tenant Mr. T. Crampton, and the offer of Mr. T. J. Lee, with respect to the remaining properties required for the improvement in Bridge-street.--Resolved, that the Town Clark re-pen negotiations with Shaw's Execu- tors with respect to a site for the re-building of their premises adjoining the Grand Clothing Hall, and that the consideration of the offer of Mr. T. J. Lee be deferred for the present. PUBLIC HEALTIL-A3SISTANOE FOR THE MEDICAL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>OFFICER.</head>
        <p>OFFICER.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee has accepted the following tenders for Hospital supplies ;—Messrs. J. M. Porter, Limited, for fish, poultry and game ; Messrs. William Owen and Son, for groceries provisions, and cleaning materials ; Mr. Abel Haigh, for milk ; Mr. H. Singleton, for butcher's meat and suet; Messrs. Pierpoint and Co., for bread and flour. The Committee discussed the question of providing the Medical Officer of Health with assistance to relieve him of some of his duties until he is in a better state of health.—Resolved that Dr. Gatlikin, of Huddersfield, be appointed to assist the Medical Officer of Health at a salary at the rate of £125 per annum, the engagement to be terminable by one month's notice on either side, which may be given at any time. Miss Flora A. Hughes having tendered her resignation as a sanitary inspector of the borough, the resignation was accepted with regret, and the Town Clerk was asked to convey to Miss Hughes the appreciation of this Committee of the services rendered by her.— Resolved that the question of the appointment of a successor to Miss Hughes be deferred until the next meeting. BRICK CONTRACTS-A PECULIAR INCIDENT. _</p>
        <p>The Sanitary Works Committee met on December sth and accepted the tender of. the Warrington Slate Co., Ltd., for the supply of 350 flags at 3d. each and the tender of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., Ltd., for the supply of 30,000 bricks, at 225. per 1,000. On December 18th the Committee met again and the engineer reported that Messrs. Richard Evans and C0.,1 Ltd., required 2s. 6d. per 1,000 for carting bricks, and ' that this would increase the price of their tender to 21s. 6d. per 1,000. Resolved, that the resolution accept- ing their tender for bricks be rescinded. Resolved also that the tender of the Warrington State Co., Ltd., for the supply of 30,000 Greengate bricks at 21s. 6d.per 1,000 be accepted. There was a further Committee meeting on December 20th when the Chairman repoited that when the com- mittee opened the tenders for bricks at their meeting on the sth December, 19011, one tender (that of Messrs. W. and J. Richardson) had been overlooked, having been mislaid, and that at their last meeting this tender should have been brought forward, but owing to a mis- understanding was not. The committee were further informed that the tender of the Warrington Slate Co. Ltd., for the supply of Greengate bricks which had been accepted at the last meeting was id. per 1,000 more than the tender of Messrs. W. and J. Richardson for the supply of Ravenhead bricks, and after fully discuss- ing the matter it was decided to place the circumstances before the Council and obtain their instructions. Resolved, that the tender of Messrs. W. and J. Richard- son for the supply of 30,000 Ravenhead bricks according to sample at 21s. sd. per 1,000 delivered at Longford Depot be accepted. BRANCH READING ROOM.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Museum and Library Committee a letter was read from the Town Clerk intimating that the General Purposes Committee had appointeda sub- committee to confer with this Committee on the ques- tion of a site for a Branch Reading Room, and the Secretary was directed to arrange a date for a confer- ence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TOWN HALL, PARKS AND B</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL, PARKS AND B</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this bommittee an application from the Warrington Association of Bands for an increase in the annual grant was considered, but the Committee could not see their way to recommend an increase.</p>
        <p>~ „,. The Borough Surveyor and the Bath-keeper were instracted to report on the resolution of the Council that, steps should be taken to provide additional and cheaper bathing accommodation. Councillor Wilkinson was appointed a member of this Committee in place of the late Councillor Brough. Plans of the Soldiers' Memorial were approved and accepted on condition that there is no liability what- ever upon the Memorial previous to its erection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>LAWSON CONTRACT.</head>
        <p>LAWSON CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk having written to the members of the Water Committee asking whether they desired the reports and papers placed before the Lawson Contract Sub-Committee. Resolved, that he be requested to supply the members of this committee with such reports and papers. ELECTRICITY.-ENGINEER'S SALARY INCREASED.</p>
        <p>A letter from the Tradesman's Association asking this Committee to extend the times for the issue of return tickets to 8.30 a.m., and to allow the return half to be used any time after 5.30, was read. Resolved, that the application be not entertained. A further letter from the Tradesmen's Association complaining of the unsteadiness of the electric current and the unsatisfactory supply of electric light during the daytime was read. Resolved, that the Town Clerk inform the Association that no complaints have been received by the Electrical Engineer, and that any of their members who have any cause of complaint be requested to bring the same to the notice of the Engineer. Resolved that the salary of the Electrical Engineer (Mr. Mathias) be increased from £3BO to £350 per annum, and that he be allowed to take not more than two pupils at any one time. The Engineer was also instructed to prepare a scheme for the automatic recording of the times of opening and closing the Swing Bridge at Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>LAND PURCHASE REFORMS. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee a letter was read from the Town Clerk of Sheffield sug- gesting the desirability of greater powers being con- ferred on municipalities with respect to the acquisition of lands in the outskirts of their areas, without being subject to some of the restrictions imposed by the Local Government Board on the granting of sanctions for loans for the purchase moneys, and without being required to declare at the time of the purchase to what municipal purpose the lands acquired shall ultimately be devoted; and requesting the Corporation to pass a resolution in favour of the above object. Resolved, that the Council approve of such object,and that copies of the resolution be sent to the Prime Minister, the President of the Local Government Board, and the Member for the Borough.</p>
        <p>TO CURE A COLD IN ONE DAY Take Laxative Bromo-Quinine Tav lets. All Chemists refund the money if it fails. E. W. Grove's name oft each box. ls. I,ld</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MAGAZINE CUTTINGS.</head>
        <p>MAGAZINE CUTTINGS.</p>
        <p>A TRIBUTE TO LORD CROINIEII.</p>
        <p>The editor of of the " NlokattAm " of atiro says : I "Never since the days of Joseph has Egypt had a ruler so just as Lord Cromer. His wocd has become almost a synonym for justice. It is becluse he is just that the present agitation against him has spread. Egypt has prospeced immensely since the days of the British occupation; the value of land has risen enormously,and when the irrigation schemes are complete it will rise still more, It is precisely this growing wealth of Egypt that constitutes our danger. There ate multitudes of greedy and rapacious vultures, both in Egypt and in other countries, whose mouths are watering for the opportudity of plundering our country. Between these vultures and tho Egyptian stands Lord Cromer, and as long ashe stands there the vultures will be foiled."</p>
        <p>THE TELEPHONE SUBSCRIBER AND THE "DOUBLE-OHS."</p>
        <p>Boskin's office boy, says the" New York Press," was away on a vacation last week, and Boskins undertook to answer the telephone. He is now firmly convinced that ways of telephone girls are past finding out. He had always been treated courteously by the sweet-voiced "telephone-girls," but he has come to believe that they were only making game of him. Boskins has to call No. 2,100 " John " Exchange several times in the daily course of business. The first day after the office boy had gone he took down the receiver with confidence. Gve me twenty-one hundred John,'" he said. " What is it ? " asked the girl in a shocked voice. "Twenty-one hundred," repeated Boskins .with precision. "Oh, you want two-one-naught-naught," said the girl kindly, as she gave him the number. Boskins felt rebuked, and he remembered the correc- tion, storing it away for future reference. "Always say each figure separately," he told himself. "Don't attempt any fancy combinations." He was in a good humour the next time he called" the number.</p>
        <p>" Give me two:one-naught:naught," he said.</p>
        <p>"Two-one-double naught," said the girl promptly, with the accent on the "double," as though correcting him.</p>
        <p>The next time he called the number he decided to try the new combination. _ _</p>
        <p>" rwo-one-double-naught," he said pleasantly. " Twenty-ono-naught-naught," said the girl, and Boskins retired still morebewildered. That afternoon be had to call the number again. "Twenty-one-naught-naught," he said, and held his breath.</p>
        <p>"Twenty-one double-oh," corrected the girl as she gave him the connection. Boskins was so confused by this time that he could hardly talk. It worried him all the afternoon. He wished the office boy had not gone away. Then, in despair, he took a pencil and figured out that the number r:ould be given in nine different ways. They were : Twenty-one hundred. Twenty-one-naught-naught. Twenty-one-double naught. Twenty-one-oh-oh. Twenty-one-double-oh. Two-one-naught-naught. Two-one-double naught.</p>
        <p>Two-one-oh-oh. Two-one-double oh.</p>
        <p>Boskias studied them all over, and hope rose again within him. The next time he approached the telephone it was with an air of confidence. _ _ _</p>
        <p>" Twenty-one-oh-oh," he said. A smile played about his round face. He wondered how the telephone girl would receive this combination. It was the only one of the nine that she had not given him. There was a moment's silence after he hod spoken the number. Boskins smiled at his own craftiness. This, indeed, was a Machiavellian triumph. The girl asked him to repeat the number. "Twenty-one-oh-oh," said Boskins, like one propound- ing a riddle. Oh, you want two-ten-naught," said the girl brightly. Boskins weakly hung up the receiver. He thought about it a long time, and finally decided that it was useless to stake his poor wits against those of the telephone girls. "They might have a hundred combinations, for all I know," he grumbled. Boskins hired an office boy the next day.— Telephone Journal."</p>
        <p>-" National</p>
        <p>GOAT-SHOOTING IN THE ROCKIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Horace Annesley Vachell, author of "The Hill,' contributes an interesting article to the January " Windsor" on "Goat Shooting .in the Rocky Mountains." He says :—" The Rocky Mountain Goat must not be confounded with the ordinary goat which has reverted to a feral condition. According to the Century Dictionary, the Rocky Mountain Goat (llaplocerus Montanus) is a near relative of the chamois, and not a goat at all in any proper sense. Mr. Baillie- Grohman, says that he never saw one in the Rocky Mountains. I have seen one, only one, not far from the Teton Basin, in Wyoming, but the task of stalking him fell to a friend who was with me at the time. I lay down and watched the stalk through my glass. My friend managed to crawl within a hundred yards of the goat. Suddenly I saw the goat bound out of sight, and immediately after two faint clicks came to my ears. Despite his experience, my friend had forgotten to load his rifle ! Crossing an ugly gulch, we had taken out our cartridges, and, in the excitement of seeing a rare animal, had forgotten to put them back. My own rifle was unloaded, and had the fortune of the stalk fallen to rne,the same exasperating miss-fire would have occurred. "After this experience I searched diligently for goats, but without success. Finally my brothers and I went to British Columbia, in the hope of shooting some in the Selkirks. We camped not far from what is now the town of Golden, and we secured the services of an excellent guide—a trapper, known to all and sundry as' Shorty.' For six 'days, I remember, my two brothers and I walked steadily, from sunrise to sunset, up and down the moun- tains to the north-west of Golden; but not a goat did we see. It was mid-November' and the winter had set in with extreme severity. Each night the mercury in our camp thermometer fell below zero; and our small tent was perforated with holes ! To travel an immense distance with a particular object not in view at the end of it, to undergo boreal hardships before the body is hardened to endure them, to suffer at one and the same time from hunger, cold and fatigue, is an experi- ence common to all who pursue big game in a wild country. But on this occasion the cold seemed more piercing, the fatigue more insupportable, than any we had endured before. In company with the guide "Shorty," Mr. Vachell eventually got within reach of a herd, high up on the mountain, and he adds ;—" In less than a minute I had shot six goats, including the two veterans close by me. The herd,thinking that danger must come from below, bolted straight at me. I could have cubbed one with my rifle. The biggest, however, of those I had shot ' was only badly wounded. I ran forward to despatch , him, forgetting where I was. He strugged on, poor beast, and I, anxious to end his sufferings, never marked where I was going till I saw the goat vanish, and felt myself slipping slowly and steadily after him down a steep slope which ended abruptly on the edge of a precipice. With desperation I dug my heels and my rifle into the snow, staying my progress, but only for a moment. Then I realized, with sickening conviction, that the shale beneath the snow was slipping, and that I must slip with it into the gulf below. I lay perfectly, still, dumb with horror, and I well remember shutting my eyes for the inevitable plunge. And then on the very ragged edge of eternity, I came to a blessed full stop, Ido not remember how I crawled back to life and sound ground, but I was dripping with perspira- tion as if I had just -come out of the hottest room in a Hammamm. Next day the wounded goat was found, stone dead, some three thousand feet below the spot ' where he had disappeared." The January issue of the "Windsor Magazin.) " is a , very notable New Year number, falling not a jot behind , its enormously successful Christma3 predecessor, for it includes complete stories and articles by Eden Phill- potts, lan Nlaclaren, Arnold Bennett, Frances Rivers and Lloyd Osbourne, in addition to a large instalment of Max Pemberton's fasoiniting new romance " The Lodestar." No fewer than twenty-seven coloured cartoons this month represent" The Universities and Public Schools," and a fine art section contains thirteen beautiful reproductions of the animal paintings of Mr. Edwin Douglas—one of which is given as a special frontispiece plate. An exceptionally interestinr, article deals with the French Foreign (Ince, and the whole , number is superbly illustrate'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ROAD AND ITS PRICE.</head>
        <p>THE ROAD AND ITS PRICE.</p>
        <p>The grim little tragedy was not altogether nncorainon upon the Indian frontier, but it gained vividness from the brevity of the letters which related it. The first one, that in the woman's hand, written from a house under the Downs of Sussex, told of the birth of a boy in words at once sacred and simple. They were written for the eyes of one man, and Major Dewes had a feeling that his own, however, respectfully, violated their sanctity. The se,ond letter was an unfinished one written by the husband to the wife from his tent amongst the rabble Abdulla Mahommed. Linforth clearly understood that this was the last letter he would write. "Jam sitting writing this by the 14 ht of a candle. The tent door Is open. In front of me I can see the great snow-mountains. All the ugliness of the shale slopes is hidden. In such a moo:111ga, my dear may you always look back upon my memory. For it is over, Sybil. They are waiting until I fall asleep. I have been warned of it. But I shall fall asleep to-night I have kept awake for two nights." He had fallen asleep even before the letter was completed. There was a message for the boy and a wish: May he meet a woman like you, my dear, when his time comes, and love her as I love you," and again came the phrase, "I am very tired." It spoke of the boy's school, and continued: "Whether he will come out here it is too early to think about. But the road will not be finished—and I wonder. If he wants to, let him ! We Linforths belong to the road," and for the third time the rhrase recurred, "I am very tired," and upon the phrase the letter broke off. Dewes could imagine Linforth falling forward with</p>
        <p>his head upon his hands, his eyes heavy with sleep while from without, the tent the patient Ohiltis watched until he slept. "Ho did it happen ?" he asked. "They cast a noose over his head,"replied the Diwan. Dewes nodded and turned to Luffe.</p>
        <p>"These letters and things must go home to his wife. It's hard on her, with a boy only a few months old." "A boy ?" said Luffe, rousing himself from his thoughts. " Oh ! there's a boy? I had not noticed that. I wonder how far the road will have gone when he comes out." There was no doubt in Luile's mind, at all events, as to the boy's destiny.—Froin "The Broken Road," in the " Cornhill Magazine "for January.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>FOR THE OUTDOOR MAN AND WOMAN.</head>
        <p>FOR THE OUTDOOR MAN AND WOMAN.</p>
        <p>In every respect "C. B. Fry's Magazine" amply justifies its claim to its breezy title " The Outdoor Magazine," and there is not a solitary phase of sport and open air pastime which is not dealt with, and by experts, in its pages. Two current features which have caused some stir in the respective circles they inti- mately concern are "Advanced Golf," by James Braid, the Open Champion, and " The Modest Man's Motor Up-to-Date," by Major C. G. Matson, whose original. " Modest Man's Motor " brought him instant recog- nition as an authority on motoring matters. With ' regard to he Golf Series Mr. Braid makes no pretence of offering his readers elementary instruction in the Royal Game. He speaks rather to the individual who has served an apprenticesphip on the links and has a thorough grsp of the main principles of the pastime. But what James Braid has to say on this subject will interest and benefit expert and tyro alike. A HAUNT OF HENRY THE EIGHTH..</p>
        <p>Much curiosity has been aroused by the slow and careful restoration of Hever Castle, the ancient home of Anne Boleyn, and in the January "Pall Mall Magazine," with many beautiful drawings by Mr. Hedley Fitton, the tale is told of how the work has been carried out. One room, we learn, enriched with much rare old Tudor furniture, and reproducing that half-historic, half-melanchoiy aspect which age alone can give, is the square chamber where Henry VIII. slept during his many visits to Sir Thomas Boleyn's Castle. Facing the four-post bed, remarkable in size, magnificence, and elaboration and in the manifold richness of its carving, stood a throne-like chair, whose spell was even more potent. I was led by winding stone stairs in the thickness of the outer wall to the famous Long Gallery in which Henry and Anne paced and whispered ; here in their 1 trysting-place by the beautiful old oriel window, on which are emblazoned the Tudor arms in painted glass; j from here, too, they say it was that Anne watched for Henry's appearance on the hill-top. Since the estate passed into the hands of the present owner many fantastic stories have found their way into print, whose burden was that violent hands were being laid upon the old grey pile. These roofs explained at once why such stories should have sprung into existence for a forest of ugly scaffold-poles must have hidden the Castle from view for many a long month ; and again. the Castle within must have echoed with the din of hammer, the chip of the mason's chisel, the creaking of the carpenters' saws, the murmurous hum of labour busy on a remarkable piece of restoration. Only an anchorite would dream of living in a medifeval castle without taking some such measure. A moat cannot defend us from a clammy dampness, coating even the inner walls and floor with a mysterious growth where lurk ills which prey on man. So modern wit steps in and Hever Castle now rests upon a lower floor of asphalt. Nor is a lake a new feature of Hever, for the engineer discov- ered traces of one, possibly prehistoric in making their foundations. We entered the Castle once more, and visited the library and study, both lined with bookcases of sabicu of a soft golden brown hue, and hard as ebony. But even these specimens of modern craftsmanship pale before the Minstrel Gallery in the great dining-hall, whose surface has been literally covered with the most beautiful network by the sculptors in wood. Wherever thii- eye turned it fell upon some object which illus- trated the devotee care which has been bestowed on the most minute details of the Hall. .No plane has been allowed to touch the panels of wall or ceiling, but only the adze, as you may see by glancing at their surface ; whilst the long table, the buffet, the tapestry, the coffers, the chairs, the armour, the heraldic emblazon- ments in the lofty windows, are eloquent of the period when Hever was in its glory. Already time has cast its kindly colours upon the new work, whilst the old itself seems but in its youth, now that it is stripped of mantling ivy and its open wounds are healed. A bowling green, a maze, a rosary lie just beyond the inner moat and lend other pleasant features to the scene, and occupy the site of Annie Boleyn's garden. Up yonder on the gentle hill-top is a row of old trees, and the path that runs through them is called Annie Boleyn's Walk. I see the beginning of a wide avenue of chestnuts which some day may rival that famous one at Hampton Court. I see plantations springing up in all directions and already the marshy meadows and the hungry land have assumed rich park- like robes, and over the greensward the deer may roam and browse at will. For, as in the days of the Hevers, there is to be a deer park here—nay, Hever " itself is the old word for "deer park," or, rather, a park for stags.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARSHIP CONSTRUCTION IN 1901%</head>
        <p>WARSHIP CONSTRUCTION IN 1901%</p>
        <p>A fairly satisfactory number of new ships for the Navy, says" Engineering," have this year been floated ; three battleships, two armoured cruisers,and a consider- able number of torpedo and sub-marine craft have been launched, the total tonnage of the twenty-one ships being S,(180 tons displacement, which is considerably below the average of the six preceding years-117,700. This, however, scarcely represents the relative fighting efficiency of the ships, as this year will be notable as signalising the transitional stage in respect of design, and the fighting efficiency per unit of displacement has been enormously increased. This is perhaps best reflected by the fact that whereas six years ago the average price of warships was £7O per ton of displace- ment, it is now £lOO per ton. A greater area is pro- tected by armour of considerably higher resist- ing power per unit of thickness and conse- quently more costly of production, so that the whole of the righting will be done within effectivey protected quarters, -rendering the enemy's light rapid-firing guns up to 6 in. bore inefficient. But an influence affecting total cost still more is that all the primary guns are of the highest power. The cost of a pair of 12-in guns, with their barbette mountings, is over £lOO,OOO ; the placing of ten such weapons in a battleship, as compared with four formerly, and of eight in cruisers instead of a number of light weapons on central pivot-mountings of a relatively low cost, accounts for a large part of the difference in price. In other words, continues " Engineering," the hull and the machinery of a modern battleship represent only about 45 per cent, of the total cost, as compared with some- thing approaching 60 per cent. formerly, and this, too, notwithstanding the great increase in speed. Thus, while the tonnage output of warships for the year is only equal to 73 per cent, of the average for the previous ssx years. the cost of the ships launched this year in their completed state—about £B,soo,ooo—is eqnal to 92 per cent. of the cost of the ships launched in the past six years.</p>
        <p>DON'T DELAY IF YOU FEEL "OUT OF SORTS Or if you have a friend whose constitution has been weakened, whether by overwork of brain or body by exposure to the weather, by working long hour in, ill-ventilated rooms, by recent sickness, or by any cause tending to weaken the bodily powers ; in a word, if he is one who suffers from any form of complaint which may be the result of any of theabove or similar causes; if, for instance, you can see that he is troubled with Indigestion, the bane of our Country and of modern civilization; or, if you think he has Low Spirits, that sure sign of some Nervous Disorder, bringing in its train a host of other complaints such as Melancholy, Lassitude &amp;c., or, perhaps, Neuralgia, and frequently, Sleeplessness, which of itself is sufficient to weaken the t congest constitution, and to make life a burden; or, if being free from these, he is regarded by his friends or himself as being of a delicate constitution, subject to Bronchitis, Coughs, Colds, a tendency to Consumption, or other Chest Affections, and if he, like thousands of others, failed to get relief from other remedies,tell him ' he need not be disheartened, that there is one Remedy whose virtues are so great, that ,it has succeeded where others have failed, and that there is hope he may be cured,asthousands of others have been cured before him by making a sensible and timely use of Gwilym Evans Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. If your friend then gives it a fair trial, he will be fully convinced, as others have been, that this is the Best Remedy of The Age. You or your friend may ask "What is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters ?" It is a Preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffering from Debility, Nervousness, and General Exhaustion, and if any value be attached to human testimony, the efficacy of this medicine has been suc- cessfully established. Its claims have been tested and proved by the medical profession and others, and corro- borated by the written testimonials of eminent men. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contain not only as suitable quantity of Quinine in each dose, but the active principles of the following well-known herbs— Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, and Dandelion. The use of Quinine is well kown but it has never been satisfactorily combined with these preparations, until after overcoming considerable difficulties, the proprietor was able to secure a perfectly uniform preparation, combining Ili the essential properties of tbe above plants in therg greatest purity and concentration. It is now estab- lished as a family medicine,and is increasing in popular favour the more it is known and tested. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a tonic "Pick-me-up," scientifically mixed in happy proportions. Sold everywhere in bottles, 2s. 9d. and 4s. Gd. each. Should any difficulty be experienced in procuring Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded carriage free for the above prices by the Sole Pro- prietors : The Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. We particularly caution our readers against imitators, and impress upon hem the importance of always seeing that the name Gwilym Evans "is on the Label, Stamp, and Bottle without which none is genuine.</p>
        <p>(Copyright. )</p>
        <p>"I think men</p>
        <p>easily from one to the other; and Mrs. Lansing says he hasn't been to call on the teacher quite so often lately. I know he didn't go home vith her from meeting last Sunday night, and I saw him looking at Lauretta. I don't believe but he has a good deal of feeling for her, left alone the way she is." _</p>
        <p>"More feeling than she would have for him, I guess," said Joseph, rather grimly. He was a little inclined to be severe on Lauretta ; he had _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>always thought so much of Edward. "I guess Edward is pretty well suited with the school- teacher," he said again; "and she's handsome as a picture, a sight prettier than Lauretta." "I don't know," said I; "and I don't know</p>
        <p>about her being handsomer. You men always think if a girl has blazing red cheeks, her beauty is settled. Lauretta is more delicate-looking, but</p>
        <p>it seems to me she is much prettier." "Not according to my way of thinking," said Joseph. Joseph is a good man, but he never trusts one woman's opinion of another's beauty. It was some three months after Lauretta's mother</p>
        <p>died, and the poor girl had lived alone through one of the hardest winters we had ever known: snowstorm after snowstorm, and bitter cold, and she did have a lonesome time of it. I went in there all I could ; but much of the time it was too bad for me to walk. I lived half-a-mile away, and we didn't keep a horse, and it was before the electric cars were put in. Well, poor Lauretta got along somehow; she never complained, she was always just as sweet, and meek, and gentle; but she grew thin, and there was a sad little droop at the corners of her mouth, and her blue eyes seemed to be always lookine, past you, though she was prettier than ever. Black was very becoming to Lauretta. it was Easter Sunday when that happened which no one has ever been able to explain. I, for one, have never tried to. It has always seemed to me just as well to leave some things unexplained. Easter Sunday was a beautiful day, the first real mild day we had had. The air was soft as June, the snow had gone except for patches here and there, the trees began to look green and filmy, and once in a while you could bear a bird. I may as well tell it just as it happened, as Lauretta told it</p>
