      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEEK ENDING 29Th I DECEMBER, 1906. Total. Male. Female. Births Legitimate 2O 12 8 „ Illegitimate 1 0 1 Deaths Excess of deaths over births, S. The ages of mortality were :—Under 1,6; 1-5, C; 5-15, 3 ; 15-25, 1 ; 25-65, 7 ; C 5 and upwards, 6. Total, 29. The deaths included:-1 from measles, I from diphtheria, 1 from phthisis, 1 from other tubercular diseases, 1 from cancer, 11 from lung diseases, 1 from accident, 9 from all other causes. The following cases of infections disease have been reported during the week :—Diphtheria, 1 case : Erysipelas, 1 case. Number of patients in hospital :—Scarlet fever, Borough 3, Newton 1 ; total, 4. Enteric fever, Borough 9, total 0. Diphtheria, Borough 3, total 3. Other diseases (phthisis), Borough 2, total 2. Totals Borough, 17 ; Newton, 1. Total, 18. Weather Return —Mean temperature 31, and there- fore 9 deg. lower than the preceding week. Maximum temperature 45, on the 24th inst. Minimum tempera- ture 2:3, on the 29th inst. Rainfall 1.43 inch. General direction of wind, N.W. J. G. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-01-05_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Noah's Drive.</head>
        <p>Noah's Drive.</p>
        <p>The old superstition that 13 is an unlucky number. •was fairly shattered on New Year's night, when 13:. tables were occupied by the Noah's Ark Whist Drive- at Messrs. Atkinson's Ltd. well-appointed Ca. Thirty-two games were played, intervals being agree- ably filled with music, singing, and full course sipper. The winners ran out as follows :-Ist prize (ladies), Miss A. Wainwright ; 2nd prize (ladies), Miss-- Roberts ; lst prize (gents.), Mr. Percy Warburton ; " Booby," Mr. Barnes.—Novelties were freely intro- duced and thoroughly enjoyed. "Auld Lang Syne " completed an evening pronounced by all to be a very woi thy successor to the " Parliamentary " Drive of 1906, introduced by the same Committee—Messrs:. Cooper, 'Warburton, Bayley and Throssell. The. catering of Messrs. Atkinson's Ltd., was perfect in every detail.</p>
        <p>Marriages. TURNER—ROBERTS—December 29th, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath, William Turner to Mary Elizabeth Roberts. WITKISS—BURTON—December 30th, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. Cation With4s to Florence Burton. TAYLOR—TAYLOR—January Ist, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. T. W. Barber, Joseph Taylor to Gertrude Taylor.</p>
        <p>In Memoriam. In ever loving memory of my dear Father, WILLIAM, OWEN, who fell asleep December 31st, 1899, aged 71 years. Gone but never forgotten.</p>
