        <p>in two days. The light fell off a third on the fol. lowing day. and this was followed by strange fluctuations, while little later a nebula sur- rounded thq, central star and grew outwards at the velocity of light. • The message, as interpreted by Kapleyn and Wilson, is a striking one. It tells us that three centuries ago an invisible dark star, travelling at great velocity, encountered a dark nebula, that the surface of the star was made white hot by the friction, but rapidly cooled, that the heating was partially repeated as stray nebulous wisps were penetrated, and that as the star's bright rays shot outwards they lighted more and more of the previously dark nebula. which became visible for a little time. 3o far away was tais collision that in four months the light travelling across the line of sight illuminated a breadth of nebula barely perceptible to the unaided eye, PADGATE. _— COTTAGP. HOMES CHILDREN ENTERTAINED.—The Padgate Nigger Minstrels on Saturday paid a visit to the Padgate Cottage Homes and entertained the chil- dren for a couple of hours. There was a fair number of visitors, notwithstanding the depth of snow lying on the roads, and these were accorded a hearty welcome by Mr. G. Mather, chairman of the Homes Committee. The niggers hardly came up to their reputation on this occasion owing to the indisposition of several of the troupe, and the sketch, entitled "Tommy Tittlemouse in London," had to be abandoned. The songs and patter however; went well.' Mr. F. Turner sang "Down by dat ribber" and" When your love grows cold," and Mr. S. Critchley rendered his famous song" Mad." Mr. S. Oddy was heard to great advantage in his songs "On the Zuyder Zee "and " Queen of the earth," while the " Sea-side Sultan "was sung by Mr. 0. Burrell. The best song of the evening was "1 never stopped running till I got home" by Mr. Joseph Davies,the interlocutor, who gave a capital rendering. Mention must also be made of the juvenile nigger, Master James Young, five years of age, who sang" The old folks at kosae," with much clearness, and was also responsible for a stump speech. In addition to theniggers the Padgate Juvenile trio—the Misses Bessie Harter, Florrie Turner and Mary Mellor—sang " The Three Quakeresses " and were heartily encored, while Percy Boyd and Thomas H. Blackwell caused much amusement by " Jack-in-the- Box."</p>
