        <p>CABINET MINISTER TO VISIT WARRINGTON. We are pleased to announce that the Right Hon. Sydney Buxton, M.P., Postmaster-General, will visit Warrington on Friday, February Ist and will address a meting in the Pair Hall. He is coming here at the invitation of the Warrington Junior Liberal Associa- tion who are to be congratulated upon having induced so prominent a statesman to address a Warrington audience. It is many years since a Liberal Cabinet Minister appeared in our midst and we are sure that the Liberals of the town will give Mr. Buxton an enthusiastic welcome. " Jehn Junior " writing in "Vanity Fair" on 11Ir. Sydney Buxton M. I'., says :—Three and fifty years ago he was born full of kindly Socialism ; but, being an amiable fellow, he has not always pushed his principles too far. With all the advantages of a decent education at Clifton and Trinity, Cambridge, he naturally joined the London School Board very soon after that curious machine was invented ; and after failing for Boston, siiting for a while for Peterborough, and vainly con- testing Croydon, Poplar sent him to Westminster as its Radical representative just twenty years ago. Poplar has stuckto him and he to Poplar,and last year our newest Government improved him into his Majesty's Postmaster-General, in which office he has failed to ' disturb our epistolary correspondence by any 'fad or any showy attempt to interfere with the red tape of an intricate department. Not being an irritating man, he was very properly , made a member of the Conciliation Committee in the matter of the great dock strike led by John Burns; and he has served on a Royal Commission on Education, on an Income Tax Committee, and as under-Secretary for the Colonies. He has also been gnilty of writing, more or less aptly, on " Fair wages," on " Political Questions," and on "Mr. Gladstone" ; and less drily on "Fishing and Shooting "—his two favourite relaxa- tions. Though he has not yet ridden himself of his Socialist tendencies he is quite well liked ; he has been twice married, and he is an industrious, capable, and serious politician, though he declined to join the opposi- tion to the Aliens Bill of last year. He is a great sportsman, who is said to prefer fishing to politics.</p>
